.\tools\CLAUDE.md:782:| Codex GPT-6 Astra (xhigh) -- *** MIGRATED FROM 5.6-SOL BY OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-09-04 ("GPT 6 Astra came out; use extra high effort"), VERIFIED LIVE BEFORE THE EDIT: `-m gpt-6-astra -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh"` returned the liveness token, named itself `GPT-6`, and quoted the first line of CHECKPOINT.md verbatim.  THE MODEL WAS REACHABLE ONLY AFTER A CLI UPGRADE THAT THE CLI COULD NOT DO ITSELF: 0.148.0 answered `The 'gpt-6-astra' model requires a newer version of Codex`, `codex update` failed twice with `Downloaded Codex package archive did not contain the expected package layout` (its staging dir was EMPTY -- the extraction, not the archive: the 0.153.4 tar.gz fetched from GitHub by hand had exactly the 0.148.0 layout), so the archive was unpacked into `~/.codex/packages/standalone/releases/0.153.4-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc/` and `current` re-pointed by junction, with 0.148.0 kept as `current.0.148.0-bak` for a one-command rollback.  `codex --version` now says 0.153.4.  EFFORT RAISED TO `max` BY OWNER DIRECTIVE THE SAME EVENING: accepted by the CLI (header `reasoning effort: max`, token returned) and PROVEN VALIDATED by the grok discriminator -- a bogus value errors with `Supported values are: 'none', 'minimal', 'low', 'med...`, so acceptance proves the tier; `panel.sh` carries `CODEX_EFFORT="max"`.  Re-check the model name after any further CLI upgrade -- a wrong name is a SEAT FAILURE. *** | `codex exec --sandbox read-only --skip-git-repo-check -m gpt-6-astra -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh" "$(cat brief)" < /dev/null` (the 5.6-SOL form until 2026-09-04) — the `< /dev/null` is required. Windows argv caps ~32 KB. *** `panel.sh` NOW HANDLES THIS ITSELF, as of 2026-08-30 -- do NOT hand-trim a brief for it. *** Above `ARGV_CAP` (30000) codex and kimi are fed a POINTER (`PTR_PROMPT`: read `$BRIEF_ABS` and review it as its own Questions section directs) instead of the contents; below it the inline form is byte-unchanged. Both are FILE-READING seats, so this costs nothing -- and grok (`--prompt-file`) and agy (`--add-dir`) were already immune, leaving these two as the only argv-fed seats. **The block previously WARNED and then launched anyway**, which cost both seats on a 38 KB brief and is the check-that-cannot-fail family's sibling: *a message reporting an ACTION the code does not take*, exactly like the re-firing message. `PANEL_ARGV_CAP` overrides the cap **only so the branch is testable in both directions** without keeping a 38 KB fixture; it is not a tuning knob. Controlled by extracting the real decision block out of `panel.sh` and `eval`ing it -- 6/6 rc=0 fixed, EXTRACT FAILED rc=9 pre-fix. **A HAND-WRITTEN BRIEF STILL NEEDS TO BE READABLE**: the pointer form only helps seats that can open the file, so it is not licence to grow briefs without limit -- the packet-fed Ollama seats still get the whole thing POSTed and still burn budget on it. Never the trailing-`-` stdin form (died silently once). |
