.\regress\artifacts\armbfxwidth32\pass2_01a0339\codex.txt:1105:regress/pass2_ledger.tsv-138-2956c7e	2026-09-07	PASS 2 on the #509 diff, run BEFORE this SHA existed: panel_20260907_175054 (preserved: regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/panel_p2_20260907/), brief _scratchpad/brief_sgimtedepth4dead_p2.md with the whole 1-hunk product diff, the 13-row list and the entire 9-row kill table inlined.  SEATS: codex, grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (6); the kimi CLI WALLED on a 403 weekly limit so the seat is COVERED, not lost; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED, held for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.  TWO SEATS RETURNED **NOT RATIFY** AND THE ROUND WAS CHANGED BY IT.  *** THE L2 FLOOR BREACH: `depth == 32' -> `depth >= 32' scored 13/13, A CLEAN SWEEP, because nothing drove 64bpp.  glm DERIVED it, the kimi substitute derived it independently, and grok derived it AND classified it clause (d) -- the guard's own operand with its bound relaxed, `==' -> `>=' on the same constant -- and it is not hypothetical: it is option F2, which the round considered and rejected.  BUILT AND MEASURED (killtable_20260907/M9_ge32.raw): 13/13.  Repaired IN-ROUND by a 14th row, `depth64 top', which wants the CI byte -- it asserts what the round does NOT change and takes no position on what 64bpp ought to do.  Table 2 then ran ten mutants at 14 rows with ZERO deviations from the predictions pre-registered in PRETABLE.out, and M9 is killed by that row alone.  Note the shape: M6 (`>= 16') is a LARGER edit and was already killed; the SMALLER one escaped -- the accept-row-at-the-near-bound pattern with the far neighbour unpinned. ***  M4 RECLASSIFIED (d) -> (e) on grok's reading, verified by opening the file: DEPENDS-ON takes the predicates DOMINATING the edited line plus the lvalues the GUARDED BODY reads, and that body -- exactly the two `|=' statements -- never reads MTE_MODE_COPY, which is tested later in the transfer loop.  Filing M4 as (d) would import its dual, `&& !(mode & COPY)', into the FLOOR, and that dual is a cell this instrument PROVABLY CANNOT KILL: every fill row stays green, `copy top' stays green because dest[0] of a same-type copy is invariant, and the gate OPENS so the tail rows would score bytes a CI-CI PUT never wrote.  Disclosed as clause-(e) debt.  THREE WRONG RECORDS IN THE PROBE'S OWN COMMENTS, all found by seats, all corrected: (1) 'every GET arm computes buf[0] << 24' is FALSE -- CI shifts nothing and ABGR and the default arm shift buf[3]; four of five arms, two of them a different byte, and the pre-existing sgirebufshiftub row's own TITLE carried the same overclaim and is corrected there too.  (2) '`depth8 top' reds under `!= 8'' is FALSE -- `!= 8' is false at depth 8 so the branch stays cold; `>= 16' and `!= 8' agree on ALL FOUR reachable depths and are EQUIVALENT MUTANTS.  **Table 1 had already shown it** (M7 killed by depth16 top ALONE, one red not two) and I read the score and not the row list.  (3) the header still called a 14-row detector 'a witness, expected to FAIL, must not be gated'.  The gate flag was also renamed rgba_ran -> fill_top_ok, because fill dest[0] == 0x11 is produced by the RGB arm too.  ONE SEAT FINDING REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT AND ONE REMEDY REJECTED ON ANOTHER SEAT'S: glm claimed M3's copy kills 'rest on undefined bytes' -- under M3 the PUT is RGBA, which writes all four, and the log shows the defined 0x00/0x1a; deepseek proposed gating the copy rows on `copy dest[0] == 0x1a', which grok's byte-walk shows is VACUOUS since 0x1a is what BOTH CI-CI and RGBA-RGBA produce -- the gate would always be open, including pre-fix, and the rows would then score exactly the host stack the gate exists to avoid.  *Both of those seats' errors trace to ONE ambiguous sentence in my brief, which described the gate as per-row when it is a single shared flag; two seats recovered the true reading from BASE 13/13 alone and two did not.  That is a BRIEF defect, not a seat failure.*  CARRIED TO THE 2026-09-09 ADJUDICATION as fable_queue.md 7g, six questions under a heading that NAMES the row: the HIGH one is a tension inside grok's OWN two answers -- its M4 reclassification uses DEPENDS-ON clause (1) (dominating predicates) while its MTE_TLB_B/C answer uses clause (2) (body-read lvalues) in a place where clause (1) already applies, since the switch DOES dominate.  If clause (1) means what it says, the three case labels are floor cells, which is codex's position reached by grok's own rule.  I did NOT build the `dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A' mutant: the score is predictable and would not settle the RULE, which is the question.
