.\regress\artifacts\hygienecensus_20260923\f2_20260923\panel_20260923_145405\codex.txt:2881:GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:26610:   "note": "*** COMMITTED 2026-09-12 as 599a7a8 (local only, no push).  gate 10 PASS 384 checks at the 22 pin (regress/artifacts/wdcaltdigin/killtable_20260912_closure/gate_sh_rounding_22rows.log); pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv.  Still IN WORK: the closure records (closure.by) are owed to the records round that follows #524. ***\n\n*** PASS-3 CLASS CLOSURE MEASURED -- EVERY PREDICTION HELD, INCLUDING THE ONE BUILT TO FAIL IF THE\nDERIVATION WAS WRONG.  2026-09-12. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/wdcaltdigin/killtable_20260912_closure/SCORES.txt (+ PRETABLE.out, written first)\n\n    BASE        22/22  CONTROL=OK                         predicted 22/22\n    HEAD        17/22  all five CORE rows red             clause (a) met\n    M_DRDY      20/22  PWR_SLAVE, EMPTYSLAVE              predicted t6, k0\n    M_DRDYDRQ   21/22  PWR_SLAVE only                     <- the ninth seat's 19/19 escape, DEAD\n    M_DRQERR    21/22  SRST only                          <- the second cell, DEAD\n    E_EQUIV49   22/22  FAILING none                       predicted to SURVIVE as equivalent\n    RESTORE     cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN, binary FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED; all six logs end in their sentinel\n\nTHE ENUMERATION, derived before the table: every `if (odata & X)` over the 15 nonzero subsets of the\nfour settable status bits, against the five MEASURED CORE statuses {0x51 0x58 0x08 0x01 0x50}.\n**12 die; 3 survive, and all 3 are EQUIVALENT** -- they differ from the fix only at 0x00 (leak ==\ndecline) or at DSC-without-DRDY, which status_byte() cannot produce.  `E_EQUIV49` was built to make\nthat falsifiable: had it died, the reachable-status set would have been wrong.\n\n*** TWO ROWS ADDED: the 0x01 cell (absent slave AT POWER-ON -- ERR alone, since d->error = 1 at\nDEVINIT and no command has run) and the 0x50 cell (post-SRST, nearly free).  22 rows, 22 REGS, exact\nbijection -- checked structurally, not assumed.  `None` now renders as `--`, never `0x00`. ***\n\nOWED: gate_sh_rounding.sh at the new 22 pin; the closure diff's own pass (the enumeration is what\nit verifies); then commit.  SOLE CARRIERS DISCLOSED: k0 is the only killer of 3 masks, t6 of 3, k1 of\n1 -- inherent to one status per cell.\n\n---\n\n*** PASS-2 L1 DISCHARGED BY A MEASURED KILL, 2026-09-11 23:33. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/wdcaltdigin/killtable_20260911_drdy/SCORES.txt\n\n    LEG BASE      19/19  CONTROL=OK  FAILING: none\n    LEG M_DRDY    18/19  CONTROL=OK  FAILING: CORE_READ_OFF1_EMPTYSLAVE\n                         (t6 want 0x00 got 0x08 -- the leak, exactly as predicted)\n    RESTORE       cmp-vs-SEC=CLEAN  binary=FRESH  SENTINEL CLEARED (both parts)\n\nThe mutant is `odata = 0;` -> `if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`, which two seats independently\npredicted would sweep the 16-row instrument 16/16.  **It now dies on exactly ONE row -- the row\nwritten for it -- and disturbs nothing else.**  A row is not discharged by existing; it is\ndischarged by reddening under its mutant, and this is that measurement.\n\nHOW THE ROW WAS BUILT, and the design took THREE attempts because the first two were vacuous:\nthe mutant only shows where DRDY is CLEAR, and every pre-existing row drives DRDY SET\n(ST_POWERON 0x51, ST_ARMED 0x58).  A no-image session gives status 0x00, where a correctly\nDECLINED 0x00 cannot be told from a LEAKED 0x00.  `WDCC_DIAGNOSE` is unreachable without an image\n(`wdc_command()` clears d->error, then detects the missing image and RETURNS BEFORE the switch).\n**What works is the ABSENT SLAVE plus an armed write: DRQ is INDEPENDENT OF IMAGE PRESENCE**, so\n`0xb0 -> wd_sdh` between t8 and t9 gives status 0x08 -- non-zero with DRDY clear.  Three rows\n(CONTROL / ACCEPT / CORE), probe 16 -> 19, gate pin moved 16 -> 19 by hand with its reason.\n\n*** A MEASUREMENT ERROR OF MINE IS RECORDED IN THE PROBE ITSELF, because it nearly shipped as two\nfalse 'MEASURED' claims. ***  `REGS` drives the register readback; a row whose register is ABSENT\nfrom it is never read and scores 0x00 -- indistinguishable from a device answering 0x00.  t4/t5/t6\nwere missing, so two placements reported 'status 0x00' and I wrote each up as a refutation of the\nDEVICE STATE.  Adding the registers took the same probe to 19/19.  **Those placements are UNTESTED,\nnot refuted**, and the probe comment says so.\n\nREMAINING BEFORE COMMIT: re-run `gate_sh_rounding.sh` against the 19-row pin.\n\n---\n\nSplit from `wdcaltoffset` (#494) DELIBERATELY, and the split is the record that matters: #494 fixed the STORE half only, because its witness holds the read path byte-identical so the aliased store is the only variable.  Shipping a read fix under that number would have claimed coverage the witness was designed not to provide.  *** THE DEFECT IS WITNESSED AND CHEAPLY SO -- WHICH REFUTES THE RULING THAT IT HAS NO ORACLE. ***  Two adjudications held the read half unfixable for want of a bit layout (bit 7 high-impedance, write-gate representation, active-low drive/head select).  The opus5 seat refuted that by measuring, and the reason is that **the test is an INVARIANCE, not a value**: `status_byte()` never reads `d->head`, and a Drive Address register is by definition a function of head select.  On the committed build, reading 0x3f7 gives 51 at head=0 AND 51 at head=15 -- invariant -- while the SDH readback of 0xaf proves the head write landed and 0x3f6 is unchanged by it, so the invariance is not `nothing moved`.  That refutes every candidate model of 0x3f7 without naming a single expected bit.  *** WHAT IS STILL MISSING IS THE FIX'S ORACLE, NOT THE DEFECT'S. ***  An invariance proves today's value WRONG without saying what is RIGHT, and none of the fifteen PDFs under `_scratchpad/` is an ATA or ISA document -- `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` is the authority, not this note.  Pinning digin's bits from model memory is exactly the #357/#362/#365 failure this project already paid for.  AND THE OWNERSHIP QUESTION MAY DISSOLVE IT: on a PC the byte belongs to the FLOPPY controller (see `wdcaltownership`), in which case the right answer is not a wdc value at all.  Settle ownership first.  SCOPE NOTE: the model does hold the raw material -- `case wd_sdh` latches `d->sectorsize`, `d->lba`, `d->drive` and `d->head` from the stored byte -- so ACCEPT is structurally available here in a way it was NOT for the store half, where there is no discarded right answer.  ARTIFACT, added on the fable5 seat's requirement that these numbers name their log: `regress/artifacts/wdcaltoffset/mutants_20260830/TABLE_B.txt` (sentinel `TABLE_B_DONE`).  It also records a SECOND refutation the summary above omits -- a read of 0x3f7 returns 58 with DRQ raised, and **a real Drive Address register never carries DRQ**, so the read is tracking the status register on a channel independent of the head-invariance argument.\n\n*** REVIEWED 2026-09-11 (wdcaltdigin/codex_digin_20260911.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags).  THE LAYOUT IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THE TREE -- which is the honest negative the brief asked for -- AND ONE OF MY FOUR OPTIONS TURNS OUT NOT TO BE AN OPTION. ***\n\n*** MY OPTION (d), 'decline the read as the write is declined', IS LITERALLY IDENTICAL TO OPTION (a), 'change nothing'. ***  The handler computes `odata = status_byte(d, cpu)' at `:617', BEFORE it looks at `relative_addr', and the common tail at `:746-747' does `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) data[0] = odata;'.  **So an ignored offset-1 read still returns the already-computed status: a handled no-op does not release the address.**  Genuine delegation to the floppy controller would need routing and registration work outside this branch.\n\n*That is the same shape as the sketch rule this project already carries -- a proposed edit is not a fix until it has been run against the enclosing function's real control flow -- reached from the other end: my option was written from the SITE and the behaviour is decided by the TAIL.*\n\n**WHAT THE TWO PERMITTED FILES DO AND DO NOT ESTABLISH:**\n  * `wdcreg.h` carries a DISABLED register-OFFSET definition and **no Drive Address BIT definitions at all** -- so the disabled block is a placeholder, not a usable layout.\n  * the bit layout, polarity and the high-impedance bit are **not derivable from this tree**.  The seat declined to supply a remembered layout and said so, which is what was asked of it.\n  * every WDC instance this file initialises registers the region TWO BYTES wide; the machine-by-machine floppy-attachment census is outside the permitted sources.\n\n**AND OPTION (b) -- return a constant `0x80` -- IS WEAKER THAN IT LOOKS:** *\"'Undefined' does not mean 'must be one'\"*, and on the active-low premise I supplied, **zero select bits ASSERT signals**, so an otherwise-zero byte is not a neutral default.  It would also still supply no head-dependent readback, which is the very thing the witness measures.\n\n**OPTION (c) IS THE ONLY ONE THAT COULD BE RIGHT, AND IT NEEDS A SPECIFICATION FIRST.**  Encouragingly, `d->head` is **not** a variable nothing trusts -- it is used for actual disk addressing and holds the last programmed head nibble -- so a drive/head readback is honest as far as it goes.  What is unknown is whether drive and head suffice for EVERY defined bit.\n\n*** DISPOSITION: HOLD, PENDING AN ATA SPECIFICATION. ***  Preserving the current behaviour while the evidence is obtained is defensible; **declaring it hardware-correct is not**, and neither is inventing a byte.  This project fetched PowerPC Book I yesterday on exactly this pattern -- a review naming the document that would settle a question, the owner approving, and the load-bearing claims verified LOCALLY afterwards -- and it converted a dispute into a decided defect in twenty minutes.  The same move is available here.\n\n**A NOTE FOR WHOEVER RESUMES THIS: if the disposition becomes (c), the existing witness INVERTS.**  It currently passes by showing INVARIANCE across head select (51 at head=0 and head=15).  Under (c) invariance becomes the FAILURE condition, so the round owes a positive oracle asserting the specified encoding -- and a row that merely shows the value CHANGED would be satisfied by any head-dependent garbage.\n\n*** SETTLED 2026-09-11 BY FETCHING ATA-2, AND IT REFUTES ALL FOUR OF MY OPTIONS INCLUDING THE ONE THE REVIEW CALLED THE ONLY DEFENSIBLE ONE. ***\n\n`_scratchpad/ata2_x3t10_948d.pdf` / `.txt` -- **Working Draft X3T10/0948D, \"Information Technology -- AT Attachment Interface with Extensions (ATA-2)\"**, 409 KB -> 314 KB of text, 5,909 lines.  Fetched with the owner's approval on the Book I / vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent and verified locally.  *(`file` says \"6 page(s)\" and is wrong again -- third manual in a row.)*\n\n**A GREP FOR \"Drive Address\" RETURNS ZERO, AND THAT IS NOT AN ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE.**  The register is in the Control Block map by its ADDRESS, not by that name (`:1483-1495`):\n\n        CS0-  CS1-   DA2 DA1 DA0        READ (DIOR-)          WRITE (DIOW-)\n        N     A       1   1   0         Alternate status      Device control\n        N     A       1   1   1         (Note 1)              Not Used\n\n*Having been bitten yesterday by a grep-only negative, I looked for the register by ADDRESS after the name returned nothing -- which is what found it.*\n\n*** NOTE 1, `:1529-1531`, IS THE WHOLE ANSWER: ***\n\n    \"This register is obsolete.  It is recommended that a device not respond to a read of this\n     address.  If a device does respond, it shall be sure not to drive the DD7 signal to prevent\n     possible conflict with floppy disk implementations.\"\n\n**SO THE DISPOSITION IS NOT TO ANSWER AT ALL**, and every option I put to the review is wrong:\n  * **(a) keep the status alias** and **(d) decline-in-place** -- identical to each other, as the review found, and both RESPOND where the standard recommends not responding;\n  * **(b) return a constant** -- responds, and invents a value the standard declines to specify;\n  * *** (c) MODEL DRIVE AND HEAD FAITHFULLY -- which the review called the only disposition that could be right -- IS REFUTED OUTRIGHT. ***  It would implement, carefully, a register the standard marks OBSOLETE and tells devices not to answer.  **The review could not have known this: it was confined to two files that do not contain the layout, and it said so.**\n\n*** AND THE MODEL SATISFIES THE DD7 CONSTRAINT ONLY BY ACCIDENT, WHICH IS A LATENT HAZARD WORTH ITS OWN SENTENCE. ***  `WDCS_BSY` is `0x80` -- exactly DD7 -- and `status_byte()` never sets it, assembling only DRDY, DSC, DRQ and ERR.  So today's alias happens to leave bit 7 clear and happens not to conflict with a floppy controller.  **Nothing enforces that.**  An edit that made `status_byte()` report BSY -- an entirely reasonable thing for a future round to do -- would start driving DD7 at an address the standard reserves against floppy conflict, and no row anywhere would notice.\n\n**WHAT THE ROUND NOW OWES, and it is a design question rather than a documentation one:** what \"not respond\" MEANS in this emulator.  Returning 0 from a device access function is not silence -- `memory_rw.c` converts it into an architectural exception on MIPS and M88K -- so the round must choose between an unanswered access, an open-bus value, and leaving the region registered one byte narrower so the address is never claimed.  **The narrowing option is the one that matches the standard's language most closely and is the only one that also fixes the write half by construction.**\n\n**THE WITNESS SURVIVES AND ITS ORACLE INVERTS THE OTHER WAY FROM WHAT I EXPECTED.**  I noted above that under (c) the invariance test would become a failure condition.  Under the real disposition the invariance is still not the point: the row must assert that the address is NOT ANSWERED with a status byte, and a new row should pin **bit 7 clear** so the accidental compliance above becomes an enforced one.\n\n*** THE IMPLEMENTATION IS DESIGNED AND IT IS ONE NUMBER: register `wdc_altstatus` with length **1 INSTEAD OF 2**.  2026-09-11 (wdcaltdigin/codex_impl_RETRY_20260911.txt). ***\n\n**THE OTHER THREE CANDIDATES ALL FAIL, and for different reasons worth keeping:**\n  * **(b) keep two bytes, return `0` at offset 1** -- *\"It implements a FAILED MAPPED ACCESS.  It cannot be identified with ATA nonresponse without a justified bus translation.\"*  WDC still claims the address; the guest gets an architectural exception on MIPS/M88K, which asserts something about the hardware that nothing establishes.\n  * **(c) keep two bytes, answer with a constant leaving DD7 clear** -- *\"No.  WDC still supplies a SUCCESSFUL response.\"*  It removes the status alias and preserves the thing Note 1 actually recommends against: answering at all.\n  * **(d) move the `status_byte()` call inside the offset-0 branch** -- leaves offset 1 answering the initialised zero, which is still a response.\n\n**EVERY ATTACHING MACHINE REGISTERS TWO BYTES TODAY** -- `MACHINE_MACPPC` at `addr + 0x160`, `MACHINE_HPCMIPS` at `0x14000386` and via its default formula, `MACHINE_IQ80321` at `addr + 0x402` -- and **no alternate-register functionality needs the second byte**: it supplies only the read alias and the guarded write, and the header already compiles `wd_digin` out.\n\n*** THE HONEST LIMIT, STATED BY THE SEAT AND KEPT HERE: narrowing removes WDC's CLAIM to the address, but what the COMPLETE MACHINE then does at an unclaimed address is not established by the permitted files. ***  That is a measurement, not a reading -- and it may differ by architecture, since `memory_rw.c` delivers a failed-device exception on MIPS and M88K only.  **The round owes that measurement on each attaching machine before it can claim 'does not respond' rather than merely 'WDC no longer answers'.**\n\n*Note the pleasing consequence if it holds: narrowing fixes the WRITE half by construction too, since an unclaimed address cannot carry a guarded write -- so the earlier write-side fix becomes redundant rather than wrong, and the round should say which it is.*\n\n*** OWNER RULING 2026-09-11: NARROW THE REGISTRATION. ***  Put to the owner as three options (narrow / open-bus constant / hold-plus-DD7-guard); the owner chose NARROW.  The site is `dev_wdc.c:2028-2029`, a literal `2` -- **and a harvest seat verified it is a literal `2 -> 1`, NOT `1 * addr_mult`**: the alt callback never divides its offset by `addr_mult`, and all five machine cases at `:2003-2018` change the ADDRESS only, never the length.\n\n*** MY OWN PREMISE IN THE OPTION SET WAS WRONG, AND I CORRECT IT HERE BEFORE IT IS ACTED ON. ***  I told the owner narrowing gives `MIPS/M88K: architectural bus fault`.  **It does not.**  I carried the carrier's TRUE statement about the REGISTERED-device `res <= 0` path (`memory_rw.c:603-611`, which does raise EXCEPTION_DBE) into the UNMAPPED path, which does not share it.  Read at `memory_rw.c:676-712`:\n  * an unmapped READ does `memset(data, 0, len)` and returns ZEROES;\n  * an unmapped WRITE falls straight to `do_return_ok`, silently ignored -- `:709-710` literally asks *\"Shouldn't there be a DBE exception for invalid writes as well?  TODO\"*;\n  * the MIPS `EXCEPTION_DBE` block at `:698-704` is inside `#if 0` -- **disabled**;\n  * `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` (`core/memory.c:598-606`) returns false outright for `paddr < physical_max + 0x40000`, so there is no warning flood from an I/O-range address.\n\n**So narrowing is SAFER than I represented it, not riskier** -- no fault, no infinite loop, no flood. This is the fifth instance of the scope-transfer class in two days, and the first where the wrong address was carried into a question put to the OWNER.\n\n**THE `#494` MEASUREMENT DOES NOT TRANSFER EITHER, AND A SEAT REFUSED TO LET IT.**  The comment at `dev_wdc.c:655-665` records 19,793 measured `device 'wdc_altstatus' ... failed` lines and a guest looping forever in silence.  That is a REGISTERED device returning 0 -- the `res <= 0` path -- not the unassigned path narrowing produces.  The seat declined to fill the gap with a guessed per-architecture matrix and said the permitted files do not establish narrowing is safe on **every** attaching machine (MACPPC, IQ80321, HPCMIPS unverified).  *That refusal is the correct form and the residual is real: the generic path is now verified by reading, the per-machine claim is not.*\n\n*** TWO OBLIGATIONS THE NARROWING CREATES, BOTH FOUND BY THE SEAT AND NEITHER IN MY OPTION SET: ***\n  1. **A SPANNING ACCESS IS UNRESOLVED.**  The callback assigns only `data[0]`.  Nothing in the permitted files establishes whether a transaction starting at `A` and spanning the new one-byte boundary is SPLIT, ACCEPTED or REJECTED.  A 16-bit access at the ALTSTATUS address is the obvious guest shape, so **the byte-access argument for narrowing is not proof about those transactions** and this must be measured before the edit ships.\n  2. **THE DD7 ROW I PROPOSED WOULD BE VACUOUS AFTER NARROWING, AND ITS PREMISE IS WRONG ANYWAY.**  *Driving DD7 low is still driving DD7* -- bit-7-clear is not the electrical equivalent of leaving it undriven.  Once the device never answers, a conditional *\"if WDC answers, bit 7 is clear\"* row can never fire, and an UNCONDITIONAL assertion about the guest's value is simply wrong: another responder or the unassigned value may legitimately set it.  **The replacement oracle is ZERO WDC CONTRIBUTION, PROVEN BY ROUTING** -- address, direction and width observed at dispatch.\n\nKEEP the existing write-side guard as defence against a future wider registration, and correct its comment: the ATA-2 draft's write entry for this address is **\"Not Used\"**, not \"read-only\".\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- SPANNING ACCESS: SAFE.  THE BLOCKER ON THE OWNER-RULED NARROWING IS CLEARED, AND THE EDIT ADDITIONALLY RETIRES A FILED DEFECT BY CONSTRUCTION. ***\n\nThe dispatcher ACCEPTS a wide access whole against the shorter region and CLAMPS it: `memory_rw.c:495-524`.  The `#95` pre-zero at `:491-492` runs first with the caller's FULL `len`, so the guest-visible buffer is byte-for-byte `{status, 0...}` before and after the change.  **No out-of-bounds read or write in either direction.**\n\n*** AND THE SEAT CORRECTED ITS OWN FIRST ANSWER, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  It had called a wide access THEORETICAL.  It is reachable in-tree: `dev_ps2_spd.c:73-77`, `case 0x5c` (aux control), relays the guest's **own `len`, unmodified**, to `d->wdcaddr + 0x206` -- and the chain is live, not `#if 0`'d (`:44` `DEV_PS2_SPD_LENGTH 0x800`; `:103` `d->wdcaddr = devinit->addr + DEV_PS2_SPD_LENGTH`; `:111-113` auto-adds the wdc; `machine_playstation2.c:106` adds `ps2_spd addr=0x14000000`).  `MACHINE_PLAYSTATION2` is absent from `dev_wdc.c:2006-2018`'s switch, so the default arm puts `alt_status_addr` at exactly the address `case 0x5c` targets.\n\n**That STRENGTHENS the verdict rather than weakening it.**  The safety never depended on nobody issuing a wide access -- it follows from the clamp plus the handler touching only `data[0]`.  *A reachable path that is behaviourally identical is a better answer than an unreachable one.*  And it is likely rather than merely possible: the sibling arm at `:61-71` maps the command block at **2-byte spacing**, the signature of a 16-bit-only ATA host.\n\n*** THE NARROWING CLOSES `wdcaltwidth` BY ELIMINATION. ***  With `length == 1` the found-test forces `relative_addr == 0`, and the clamp then forces `len = 1` for every `len >= 2`, so **the handler can never again be entered with `len != 1`.**  `wdcaltwidth`'s named mutant (`idata = data[0]` -> `data[len - 1]`, which *\"escapes all 103 rows\"*) becomes EQUIVALENT once `len == 1`: `data[len-1]` IS `data[0]`.  **The class is dead, not merely uncovered.**  Two standing self-declared blind spots go with it -- `wdc_altoffset_probe.py:114-115` and `CHANGELOG.md:8617-8620` both describe a `len == 2` store at offset 0 that the narrowing makes unreachable.\n\n**PER THE ABSENCE-RECORD RULE, THE WARRANT IS NAMED: *the clamp at `memory_rw.c:523` forces `len == 1` for a one-byte region.*  Any future edit restoring the region's width MUST re-run the death measurement.**  This is closure by ELIMINATION, not by coverage, so the three overtaken-closure conditions do not apply -- but it does supersede the `ps2_spd` exposure, which is the one live consumer of the width the edit removes.\n\nTWO REFINEMENTS TO MY OWN CORRECTION, both accepted:\n  * the unmapped path is silent about DATA but **not about LOGGING** -- `memory_rw.c:676-679` calls `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr()`, which prints at `VERBOSITY_WARNING` while the default is `VERBOSITY_INFO`.  *(The suppression I cited, `core/memory.c:605`, applies below `physical_max + 0x40000`; whether this address qualifies is machine-dependent and is NOT settled here.)*  The `EXCEPTION_DBE` at `:701-703` is confirmed dead (`#if 0` at `:685`).\n  * **the safety is CONDITIONAL ON THE HANDLER'S SHAPE.**  It holds because `wdc_altstatus` touches only `data[0]` (`dev_wdc.c:614`, `:746-747`).  The sibling `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc)` DOES inspect width and latches warnings at `:1093` and `:1968` -- **if anyone brings `wdc_altstatus` into line with that idiom, re-derive this.**\n\nNO SHIPPED DETECTOR ROW CHANGES COLOUR: `killtable_20260830/PRETABLE.out:49-54` already measured width-1 *\"green on every row\"*.  No oracle re-pin owed -- and equally, **no coverage for the change**, which is what the round still owes.\n\n**STATUS: the edit is a literal `2` -> `1` at `dev_wdc.c:2029`, owner-ruled, blocker cleared.  It owes a panel pass 1 on the actual diff and a detector row before it lands** -- the disposition being ruled does not discharge the lifecycle.\n\n*** `wdcaltdigin` PASS 1, 2026-09-11 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 15,433 b, complete).  **APPROVED** as a withdrawal of the WDC auxiliary decode at offset 1 -- and two of my premises are refuted, one of them the round's planned oracle. ***\n\n*** THE WITNESS DOES NOT INVERT -- INVARIANCE PASSES BOTH VERSIONS. ***  I had recorded that the existing head-invariance witness INVERTS under the fix.  It does not:\n\n    observable                     two-byte (now)   one-byte (fixed)\n    A+1 read, head 0                    0x51             0x00\n    A+1 read, head 15                   0x51             0x00\n    SDH readback after head 15          0xaf             0xaf\n    EQUALITY ACROSS HEADS               True             True\n\n> **Equality across heads still passes BOTH versions, so invariance alone cannot detect restoration of the two-byte registration.**  An exact `0x51` alias witness would simply stop observing the alias.  **SDH readback establishes that the head write landed in the main bank; it does NOT establish who answered `A+1`.**\n\n**So the acceptance criterion must be replaced with ROUTING EXCLUSION**, and head selection kept only as historical context or a control.  *With another responder present, even post-fix head invariance is not an appropriate requirement at all.*\n\n*** AND I ATTRIBUTED THAT WITNESS TO THE WRONG FILE. ***  `regress/wdc_altoffset_probe.py:113` states explicitly that **no row reads either alternate-region offset**; it contains offset-1 WRITE tests.  The seat declined to authenticate the head-select measurements from it and **refused to infer another filename from the defect id** -- which is precisely the discipline my own `dev_crime.c` error broke four hours earlier, applied back to me.\n\n**THE DD7 ORACLE IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, with the reason made exact:** `status_byte()` adds only DRDY, DSC, DRQ and ERR, **so its aliased result ALREADY has bit 7 clear.  A bit-7-clear detector would accept the current behaviour.**\n\n**AND THE ANSWER TO \"IS RETURNING ZERO A RESPONSE?\" IS THE RIGHT FRAMING:** *\"The emulator completes a read with a numeric result; that does not mean WDC supplied that result.  Removing WDC's address claim is an honest implementation of device NONPARTICIPATION at this emulator's decoding abstraction.  **It does not simulate electrical high impedance.**\"*  The round may claim adoption of a draft RECOMMENDATION with the abstraction stated -- no more.\n\n=== SEVEN DETECTOR ROWS PROPOSED, each with its named mutant and the observable that differs ===\n`A1_READ_WDC_UNCLAIMED` and `A1_WRITE_WDC_UNCLAIMED` against `M_REWIDEN` (callback count 0 -> 1; **WDC state alone would miss the write mutant, since offset-1 write handling is already a no-op**); `A0_READ_ALTERNATE_STATUS` against suppression and against a constant `0x51`; `A0_WRITE_SRST_SIGNATURE` against `M_OMIT_COMMAND_RESET`; two existing P1 accept rows reused.\n\n**THE ROW I HAD NOT CONSIDERED, and it is the sharpest: `A0_READ_PRESERVES_PENDING_IRQ`.**  ATA-2 `:1535` distinguishes Alternate Status from Status precisely in that it does NOT clear a pending interrupt -- and the normal Status handler **does** clear it at `dev_wdc.c:1938`.  So a mutant adding that clearing action to the Alternate Status path is invisible to any status-byte-only oracle: **the returned bytes need not differ at all.**\n\n*** TWO DETECTOR-PACKAGING DEFECTS IN THE SHIPPED PROBE, AND THE SECOND IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL: ***\n  1. **The existing probe's \"committed\" expectations describe the OLDER offset-1 write-reset defect**, which today's callback already rejects at `dev_wdc.c:622`.  **Reusing its unchanged baseline for this new before/after comparison would MIS-SCORE THE BASELINE.**\n  2. **`ALTOFF_DET_DONE` is emitted UNCONDITIONALLY, and failed comparisons produce no failing exit status** (`wdc_altoffset_probe.py:682`).  **So neither that sentinel nor ordinary process completion is evidence of detector acceptance.**  *This project's own rule is that a preserved log is verified by its completion sentinel -- and here is a sentinel that says DONE whether or not the rows passed.  Filed as its own finding.*\n\n**THE WIDTH RETIREMENT STANDS WITH A SHARPER WARRANT:** `memory_rw.c:492` zeroes the incoming read length **before** the clamp at `:523`, the callback uses the first byte, so a wide read leaves `{status, 0...}` and a wide write retains its first-byte effect.  After narrowing, every positive-length access reaches the callback at offset zero with `len == 1`, so `data[0]` and `data[len - 1]` are equivalent there.  **`:523` is the correct warrant** -- *and it does NOT establish that wide guest accesses disappeared, nor that all width behaviour is hardware-accurate.*\n\n*Premise correction: the initializer carries **three** machine-specific case labels, not the five I implied.  No all-machine clearance is establishable from the permitted files.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- **I NEARLY FILED FIFTEEN PROBES AS DEFECTIVE AND THE DESIGN IS SOUND**.  A NEAR-MISS, RECORDED BECAUSE THE CENSUS LOOKED AUTHORITATIVE AND WAS MEASURING THE WRONG PROPERTY. ***\n\nA pass-1 seat observed, correctly, that `wdc_altoffset_probe.py` emits `ALTOFF_DET_DONE` **unconditionally** and that *\"neither that token nor ordinary process completion is sufficient evidence of detector acceptance.\"*  I verified both halves: the sentinel is outside any conditional, and **there is no `sys.exit` anywhere in the file**, so the process always exits 0 however many rows failed.\n\nI then censused all 93 probes in `regress/`: **15 emit a completion sentinel and never call `sys.exit`.**  That reads like a fifteen-instance defect class.\n\n*** IT IS NOT A DEFECT.  I CHECKED WHAT ACTUALLY CONSUMES THEM BEFORE FILING, AND THE GATE DISCARDS `rc` ON PURPOSE. ***  `gate_arm.sh` runs the probe as `... > \"$SGILOG\" 2>&1 || true`, then:\n  1. **SENTINEL FIRST**, with the reason in its own comment -- *\"a preserved log is verified by its completion sentinel, never by its existence: a killed run loses every row and looks exactly like a run that produced none\"*;\n  2. **CONTROLS BY NAME, not by count** -- *\"a green-row total lets a control go red while the sum stays acceptable, and a run whose controls died is an APPARATUS failure, which must not be read as a wall of product failures\"*;\n  3. **PER-ROW PASS, checked individually** rather than by a total, explicitly against the padded-column class;\n  4. **THE DENOMINATOR PINNED TO A LITERAL**, so a detector that loses rows cannot pass by shrinking.\n\n> **So the sentinel's contract is COMPLETION, not ACCEPTANCE, and it discharges that correctly.  Pass/fail comes from per-row content checks the gate performs itself.  The seat's sentence is literally true and describes the DESIGN rather than a defect.**\n\n**MY CENSUS KEYED ON A CORRELATE -- the absence of `sys.exit` -- INSTEAD OF ON THE CLASS: whether pass/fail is OBSERVABLE TO THE CONSUMER.**  It is, everywhere it matters, through a channel my pattern could not see.  *That is the deriver lesson for the third time today: **before writing a deriver, ask what makes a member a member** -- and if the answer is a property the regex does not test, the error is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.*\n\n*Recorded as a near-miss rather than a finding because the cost of the other outcome was real: fifteen phantom defects filed against working probes, each of which a future round would have had to disprove.  What stopped it was the two-minute check this project already mandates -- **open the consumer and ask what it does when the thing is FALSE** -- and the answer was that the consumer never asked the question I was auditing.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ASSESS (main loop, read from source at HEAD).  **#494 CLOSED THE WRITE CELL AND LEFT THE READ CELL OPEN IN THE SAME `if` -- so this is not an independent defect, it is the SYMMETRIC CELL of a repair this project already shipped, which is the `#499` shape the carrier names by its own number.** ***\n\n**THE HANDLER, `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus)` at `dev_wdc.c:609-675`, in the order it executes:**\n  * `:614`  `idata = data[0];`\n  * `:617`  `odata = status_byte(d, cpu);`  -- **UNCONDITIONAL.  The offset is not consulted.**\n  * `:619`  `if (writeflag == MEM_READ)` -> `debug(\"[ wdc: read from ALTSTATUS ... \")` and nothing else; the common tail then assigns `odata` into the caller's buffer.\n  * `:622`  `else if (relative_addr != 0)` -> the **`#494`** arm: a WRITE to offset 1 is declined silently and the whole action is refused.\n  * `:673`  `else` -> the genuine Alt Ctrl write path (`#481`'s `WDCTL_RST`).\n\n> *** THE READ PATH NEVER DECODES ITS OFFSET AT ALL.  `relative_addr` is tested in exactly ONE place, and that place is inside the `else` of `writeflag == MEM_READ`. ***\n\n**So a READ of offset 1 -- the Drive Address register, `0x3f7` on the PC layout -- returns the ALT-STATUS byte**, which is this row's filed claim, now confirmed from the source rather than asserted.\n\n=== THE CELL SPACE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE FINDING ===\nThe function's own inputs give a two-by-two: **{READ, WRITE} x {offset 0, offset 1}.**\n\n| cell | disposition at HEAD |\n|---|---|\n| WRITE, offset 0 | Alt Ctrl, correct (`#481`) |\n| WRITE, offset 1 | **CLOSED by `#494`** -- declined silently, whole action refused |\n| READ,  offset 0 | alt-status, correct |\n| READ,  offset 1 | *** OPEN -- returns the alt-status byte *** |\n\n**`#494` repaired the cell its mutant used and left the symmetric one.**  The carrier states this class in its own words -- *'the original breach, reproduced inside its own repair, by someone who had just spent an hour understanding it'* -- and prescribes the remedy: **when a class recurs, enumerate the full space from the function's own inputs and close every cell in ONE diff, then write into the contract which cells are covered.**  Four cells; three closed; one diff owed.\n\n*** AND `#494`'S OWN COMMENT CARRIES A MEASUREMENT THAT BEARS DIRECTLY ON THE REPAIR CHOICE, which is why it must be read before this round picks a disposition. ***  At `:655-665` it records, correcting an earlier wrong reason in place:\n\n> *'THE ACCESS STAYS HANDLED (`return 1`) ... this device is REGISTERED, so a 0 never reaches [unassigned-address] handling.  `memory_rw` sets `res = -1`, takes its `res <= 0` branch, and raises `EXCEPTION_DBE` -- measured, **19793 `device 'wdc_altstatus' ... failed` lines and ZERO `non-existant` ones** under `-v`.  The guest then loops forever re-executing the faulting store, and at DEFAULT verbosity it does so in SILENCE.'*\n\n**So `return 0` FROM THIS REGISTERED DEVICE IS MEASURED TO HANG THE GUEST SILENTLY.**  That is a fact about a *registered* device returning 0 -- it is NOT the same thing as narrowing the registration, which makes the address unmapped and takes an entirely different path.  *The two dispositions are easy to conflate and the tree has a measurement distinguishing them; it would be a wrong record to cite that 19793 against a narrowing.*\n\n=== THE OPTION SET FOR THE OPEN CELL, with what each actually does here ===\n  * **NARROW THE REGISTRATION** (`dev_wdc.c:2028-2029`, the literal `2` -> `1`) -- closes BOTH offset-1 cells structurally, and the handler's `relative_addr != 0` arm becomes dead code that must then be dispositioned rather than left as a comment describing a case that can no longer arrive.  **Changes 0x3f7 from HANDLED-AND-DECLINED to UNMAPPED**, so the `#494` measurement above does not transfer and the unmapped path must be measured on its own.\n  * **DECODE THE READ** -- mirror `#494` on the read side inside the handler, leaving the registration alone.  Smallest diff; keeps the address owned by wdc; makes the two-by-two symmetric.\n  * **BACKED ACCEPT / IMPLEMENT** -- model the Drive Address register.  **Refused by the tree's own evidence**: `wdcreg.h` compiles `wd_digin` out under an `#if 0` whose note reads *NOT MAPPED; fd uses this register on PCs*, and `dev_fdc.c`'s `DEV_FDC_LENGTH` carries *TODO 8, but collision with wdc*.  **The tree documents the collision from BOTH sides and resolves it from neither** -- which is [[wdcaltownership]], and it is a different question from this one.\n\n*** THE ORDERING CONSTRAINT, so a round does not ship the two in the wrong sequence: NARROWING SUBSUMES DECODING, and decoding first then narrowing leaves dead code behind. ***  [[wdcaltwidth]] is coupled to the same literal -- the region is registered two bytes wide while every existing row uses `len == 1`, so a narrowing also settles that row's unpinned width, and any detector written here should pin the width deliberately rather than inherit it.\n\n**STATUS: specified, NOT implemented.**  No edit made.  A build and a measurement are owed and could not be taken in this window -- a 24-leg regression tail holds the machine and this project's gates carry load-sensitive wall-clock oracles, so a measurement taken now would be worth nothing.  *The disposition question is put to the owner rather than assumed, because 'narrow the registration' and 'FAULT -- return 0' are different actions here and the tree has a measurement showing the second one hangs the guest.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 DESIGN (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 23,793 b, 0 cyber flags) + **OWNER RULING: DECODE THE READ, registration untouched.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: The four-cell table is correct.  The registered-device fault is live, but its measurement does not establish narrowing behavior.*\n\n**MY CELL TABLE SURVIVED THE SOURCE CHECK** -- the read path does not decode its offset, and the open cell is (READ, offset 1).  **And the transferability question I raised was answered the way I suspected: `#494`'s measured 19793 failed-access lines describe a REGISTERED device returning 0, and do NOT establish what a NARROWED registration would do.**  *Citing that measurement against a narrowing would have been a wrong record, which is why it was asked rather than assumed.*\n\n*** AND IT CAUGHT MY THIRD INVENTED PATH OF THE DAY. ***  The brief listed `src/include/wdcreg.h`; the seat reported *'The system cannot find the file specified'*, **did not guess a replacement** (as the brief instructed), and scoped its answer to the three files it could open.  The real path is **`src/include/thirdparty/wdcreg.h`**.  *Three invented paths in one session -- `dev_crime.c`, `regress/sh_tlb_probe.py`, and now this -- each caught by a seat that refused to substitute a neighbour.  The brief rule that produced the catch is the one that says VERIFY EVERY PERMITTED PATH EXISTS, and I verified four of five.*\n\n=== THE REPAIR, AS THE SEAT WROTE IT ===\n\nDecline the offset-1 read, return byte **`0x00`**, retain handler return value **1**, keep the unconditional status calculation and the common tail:\n\n    if (writeflag == MEM_READ) {\n        if (relative_addr != 0) {\n            odata = 0;\n        } else {\n            debug(\"[ wdc: read from ALTSTATUS: 0x%02x ]\" ..., (int)odata);\n        }\n    } else if (relative_addr != 0) {\n        /*  Existing #494 write-decline arm.  */\n\nThe Alt Ctrl arm follows unchanged.\n\n**ZERO'S WARRANT IS NAMED AND ITS LIMIT IS NAMED WITH IT** -- the emulator's deterministic declined-read convention: `#95` pre-zeroes registered-device read buffers and the ordinary unimplemented-address path also chooses zero.  *** 'That is an emulator policy, not proof that a real Drive Address or floppy register must read zero.' ***  *An honest warrant with its scope stated, rather than a hardware claim the files cannot support -- which is the distinction [[wdcaltownership]] turns on.*\n\n=== *** THE ORACLE TRAP, AND IT IS THE SAME SHAPE AS TONIGHT'S n6 FINDING *** ===\n\nThe clause-(d) mutant is **B-null-effect**: keep `if (relative_addr != 0)` and its branch, but remove `odata = 0`, so offset-1 reads return the status byte S again.\n\n> *** A DECLINED READ MUST USE A FIXTURE WITH **S != 0** TO KILL THAT MUTANT.  IF S IS ZERO, THE FIXED AND THE BROKEN IMPLEMENTATIONS RETURN THE SAME BYTE. ***\n\n**That is a check that cannot fail, identified before any row is written** -- the second one caught at design time tonight, after `n6`'s `r7 == 0` row would have been vacuous against a mask-returning variant for exactly the same reason (*the mask would also be zero*).  *Two independent rounds, same failure shape: an oracle asserting ZERO is worthless unless the defective path is shown to produce NON-zero.*\n\n**AND THE CONTROL IS ESTABLISHABLE, which is why this is a constraint rather than a blocker:** **disklessness does NOT imply zero status** -- initialisation sets `d->error = 1` at `dev_wdc.c:1994`, and `status_byte()` includes `WDCS_ERR` when `d->error` is set at `:594-595`.  *So a diskless fixture is a legitimate null medium AND can carry a nonzero S; the round must establish that explicitly rather than assume it.*\n\n**THE SECOND NULL-MEDIUM MEANING NEEDS ITS OWN REJECTION:** a null test vehicle that returns zero for every read without exercising wdc at all **is accepted by an offset-1-zero assertion alone**.  The detector must reject it through a **legal offset-0 read expected to return nonzero S**.\n\n*** A NOTE ON THE ROAD NOT TAKEN, recorded because the reasoning is reusable: for option A (narrowing) the seat observed that `2 -> 1` HAS NO GUARD-AND-EFFECT PAIR, so 'changing 1 back to 2' is REVERSION, not a clause-(d) mutation. ***  Its structural analogue would be **A-null-exclusion** -- keep the one-byte registration but add a SECOND one-byte registration of the released address back to this handler, defeating the exclusion while the original line still reads `1`.  **Checking only that the registration still says `1` would miss it.**  *Filed as reasoning for whoever eventually takes [[wdcaltwidth]]; not this round's problem.*\n\n**STATUS: specified and RULED.  Implementation, witness and kill table are the next unit of work.**  The `#519` regression tail has now cleared the machine, so measurement is available.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 WITNESS + KILL TABLE (agy 3.8, measuring seat).  **WITNESS BUILT AND TABLE RUN: HEAD 14/16, FIX 16/16, AND S WAS MEASURED NONZERO (0x51 / 0x58) -- so the oracle trap the design pass predicted was real and is discharged by measurement rather than by assertion.** ***\n\n**THE FIXTURE ESTABLISHES NONZERO S EXPLICITLY, which is the whole reason this round needed care.**  The clause-(d) mutant `M_NULL_EFFECT` keeps `if (relative_addr != 0)` and deletes `odata = 0`, so offset-1 reads return the status byte again -- **and if S were 0 the fixed and broken builds would return the identical byte and the row would be vacuous.**  Measured:\n  * at devinit `dev_wdc_init()` sets `d->error = 1`, so with a disk attached `status_byte()` gives `WDCS_DRDY | WDCS_DSC | WDCS_ERR` = `0x40 | 0x10 | 0x01` = **`S = 0x51`**;\n  * armed by `WDCC_WRITE` (clearing error, setting `write_in_progress`): `WDCS_DRDY | WDCS_DSC | WDCS_DRQ` = **`S_armed = 0x58`**.\n**Both values are printed in the log rather than asserted** -- so the discrimination is visible in the artifact, not just claimed in a report.\n\n=== SCORES -- AND THE SCORES ALONE ARE MISLEADING, WHICH IS THE FINDING ===\n*(`regress/artifacts/wdcaltdigin/killtable_20260911/`, `KILLTABLE_DONE` present; each score read from its own `probe_<leg>.raw`)*\n\n| leg | score | WHICH rows failed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (fix) | **16/16** | none |\n| `HEAD` (pre-fix) | 14/16 | `CORE_READ_OFF1_POWERON` (want 0x00, got **0x51**), `CORE_READ_OFF1_ARMED` (want 0x00, got **0x58**) |\n| `M_NULL_EFFECT` (clause d) | 14/16 | the SAME two core rows -- **killed by the nonzero S** |\n| `M_REINSTATE` (clause b) | 14/16 | the same two core rows |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` (clause c) | 14/16 | *** A DIFFERENT PAIR: `ACCEPT_READ_OFF0_POWERON` (want 0x51, got 0x00) and `ACCEPT_READ_OFF0_ARMED` (want 0x58, got 0x00) *** |\n| `RESTORED` | 14/16 | = HEAD -- the clean-restore proof |\n\n> *** ALL FOUR MUTANTS SCORE 14/16, AND `SCORES.txt` ALONE THEREFORE SAYS NOTHING DISTINGUISHES THEM.  OPENING THE RAW LOGS SHOWS THE NULL MEDIUM FAILS AN ENTIRELY DIFFERENT PAIR OF ROWS. ***\n\n*That is this project's own 'a count check cannot see the list landing in the wrong section' class, arriving in a kill table: **the numerator is identical and the discrimination lives entirely in the ROW IDENTITY.**  A record citing `14/16` four times would be true, useless, and would read as a detector that cannot tell a null medium from the shipped defect.*  **So the row identity, not the score, is what this record cites** -- and a future reader checking this round should read `probe_M_NULL_MEDIUM.raw`, not `SCORES.txt`.\n\n**THE DUAL REJECTION WORKS AS DESIGNED.**  A null vehicle returning 0 for every read without exercising wdc at all would pass an offset-1-zero assertion by itself; it fails BOTH accept rows (`ACCEPT_READ_OFF0_*` want 0x51 and 0x58), which is exactly the second null-medium meaning the design pass said needed its own rejection.\n\n**WRITE CELLS UNREGRESSED**, which the brief made mandatory: `#481`'s (WRITE, offset 0) SRST path and `#494`'s (WRITE, offset 1) decline both pass on every leg.  *A repair that fixed the read cell and perturbed a write cell would be worse than the defect.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME:** `PRETABLE.out` 09:44:27, first table run `probe_BASE.raw` 09:45:18 -- 51 s earlier, so the pre-table block cannot have been fitted to the answers.  Two-part restoration verified in the scratch clone `/tmp/gxaltdigin_clone`.\n\n*** DISCLOSED UNDER CLAUSE (e): `M_NULL_EFFECT` AND `M_REINSTATE` ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE TO THIS INSTRUMENT *** -- same score, same rows, same observed bytes.  That is expected (both restore the pre-fix behaviour at the read cell) and it is stated rather than left for a later pass to discover.\n\n**STATUS: the fix is MEASURED BUT NOT APPLIED TO SEC.**  Owed: the edit itself, propagation into the build trees, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 IMPLEMENTED.  **FIX APPLIED TO SEC AS `#525` -- THE FOURTH CELL IS CLOSED.** ***\n\n**Round `#525`** -- `#523` is staked by the `wdcerrfamily` design artifact and `#524` was taken by [[armbdt]] an hour earlier, both checked before claiming.\n\n**THE TWO-BY-TWO IS NOW COMPLETE:**\n\n| cell | disposition |\n|---|---|\n| WRITE, offset 0 | Alt Ctrl / `WDCTL_RST` (`#481`) |\n| WRITE, offset 1 | declined silently (`#494`) |\n| READ,  offset 0 | alt-status, unchanged |\n| READ,  offset 1 | **`odata = 0`, declined silently (`#525`)** |\n\n**STRUCTURE VERIFIED AFTER THE EDIT, not assumed:** the change converts an `if` / `else if` chain into `if { if / else } else if`, which is the shape most likely to go wrong silently.  Braces balance 6/6 across the function, and a comment-stripped read-through confirms all four arms reach their intended bodies.\n\n*** AND THE COMMON TAIL STILL RUNS FOR THE DECLINED READ, which is the half a naive decline gets wrong: *** `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) data[0] = odata;` executes, so the guest receives an explicit 0 rather than an untouched buffer.  *The design pass warned that leaving the caller's output buffer untouched would be an INCOMPLETE decline, and the source itself carries that warning for TimerClear.*\n\n**ZERO'S WARRANT IS RECORDED AT THE SITE AND ITS LIMIT WITH IT** -- the emulator's deterministic declined-read convention (`#95` pre-zeroes registered-device read buffers), **explicitly not a claim about what real hardware returns.**  Whose register `0x3f7` actually is remains open as [[wdcaltownership]]; the comment cites both sides of the tree's own collision evidence (`wdcreg.h`'s `#if 0` *'NOT MAPPED; fd uses this register on PCs'*, and `dev_fdc.c`'s *'TODO 8, but collision with wdc'*) without resolving it.\n\n**THE NARROWING IS DECLINED IN THE COMMENT, WITH ITS REASON**, so a future round meets the argument rather than re-deriving it: a `2 -> 1` at `memory_device_register()` closes both offset-1 cells structurally but moves `0x3f7` from HANDLED-AND-DECLINED to UNMAPPED -- an unmeasured path -- and leaves `#494`'s arm as dead code carrying a measured comment.\n\n**PROPAGATION:** `est/` DONE and verified byte-identical, after confirming it equalled SEC@HEAD first.  **Build trees NOT propagated** -- same deliberate hold as `#524`, for the same reason.  **Three files now owe propagate+rebuild together:** `cpu_arm_coproc.c`, `cpu_arm_instr.c`, `dev_wdc.c`.\n\n**STILL OWED: the rebuild, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.**  Witness and kill table are done (HEAD 14/16, fix 16/16, S measured nonzero at 0x51 / 0x58).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 on the real `#525` diff (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **PASS 2: NOT RATIFIED -- BRANCHING CORRECT, COMMENT OVERCLAIMED, DETECTOR COVERAGE UNVERIFIED.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: NOT RATIFIED -- branch binding is correct; full call purity and detector coverage remain unverified, and the comment overstates its hardware evidence.*\n\n**ALL FOUR CELLS VERIFIED AGAINST THE EDITED SOURCE, and the brace count independently reproduced (six and six):**\n\n| dir | ofs | body | common tail |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| READ | 0 | inner `else`: ALTSTATUS debug, status preserved in `odata` | writes status to `data[0]`, `return 1` |\n| READ | 1 | inner `if`: `odata = 0`, no debug | **writes ZERO to `data[0]`**, `return 1` |\n| WRITE | 0 | outer `else`: ALT. CTRL; `WDCTL_RST` -> `COMMAND_RESET` + reset helper | no buffer assignment |\n| WRITE | 1 | outer `else if`: DRIVE ADDRESS, ignored | no buffer assignment |\n\n**The unbraced `else` binds to the INNER `if`**, as intended.  *'A READ cannot enter either WRITE body.  A WRITE at offset 1 cannot reach either reset operation, regardless of its data byte.'*\n\n=== *** THE PURITY QUESTION I FLAGGED AS MY LEAST-CERTAIN: ANSWERED, AND BETTER THAN I HOPED *** ===\n`status_byte()` *'has no direct device-state mutation'* -- it builds a local integer and READS image existence, ring occupancy, `write_in_progress`, `error` and ATAPI command/phase, **with no assignment through `d` or `cpu`, no ring consumption, no interrupt operation.**\n\n> *** AND THE INTERRUPT ACKNOWLEDGEMENT IS OUTSIDE THE HELPER: *** the `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq); d->int_assert = 0;` pair lives in the MAIN register handler beside its own `status_byte()` call, **and is absent from `wdc_altstatus` entirely.**  *So calling the shared helper does not drag the normal-STATUS acknowledgement along with it, and a declined read acknowledges nothing.  The decline is COMPLETE.*\n\n*Honest limit kept: the seat verified the helper's body, not its complete call chain.*\n\n=== L4 APPLIED: THE COMMENT OVERCLAIMED TWICE, AND BOTH ARE NARROWED IN PLACE ===\n  * *'matches no hardware on any layout'* -> **'the value of a DIFFERENT register, from an offset this handler never decoded'**.  The header establishes auxiliary offset 0 and a disabled `wd_digin` with its PC-floppy note; *'It does not prove \"matches no hardware on any layout\"'*.\n  * *'a correct guest reads here expecting a FLOPPY effect'* -> narrowed to what the tree actually shows: **on the PC layout this byte is CONTESTED and documented from both sides**, which is reason enough to stay silent without the universal claim.\n\n*** AND I VERIFIED BOTH QUOTES MYSELF, because the seat could not: *** it did not have `dev_fdc.c` in its permitted set and said so rather than accepting my quotation.  Checked here: `dev_fdc.c:49` is `#define DEV_FDC_LENGTH 6 /* TODO 8, but collision with wdc */` and `wdcreg.h:71-73` is `#if 0 /* NOT MAPPED; fd uses this register on PCs */` around `wd_digin`.  **Both verbatim.**\n\n> *** A REFINEMENT NEITHER OF US HAD: `DEV_FDC_LENGTH` IS **6**, SO dev_fdc.c DOES NOT REGISTER 0x3f7 TODAY.  THE COLLISION IS PROSPECTIVE -- the \"TODO 8\" is what WOULD collide. ***  Added to the comment, because 'contested today' and 'contested if someone finishes the TODO' are different claims and the round was making the stronger one.\n\n**`M_NULL_EFFECT` CONFIRMED CANONICAL** -- keeping the offset guard and deleting `odata = 0` leaves the earlier status value intact, and *'the common tail then overwrites the dispatcher's pre-zeroed byte with that status'*, which is exactly why the nonzero CORE states discriminate it.  It is also *more isolated than a full reinstatement* because it preserves the new suppression of the offset-1 diagnostic.\n\n**STILL OWED: the selector audit** (the seat calls `M_NULL_EFFECT` *'not a sufficient selector audit'*) **and detector-coverage verification.**  *The `sgirandomcolour` round found six mutants conditioning a repair's effect on a selector no row varies; the same question is now open here and is carried forward rather than assumed answered.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL CONVENED on the combined `#524`/`#525` packet, and **SEAT LEDGER FOR THE #524/#525 PANEL -- THREE SEATS DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, BY NAME** ***\n\n**FIRED: the eight scriptable transports** via `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh <absolute brief>` -- codex GPT-6 Astra (max), grok 4.6 (xhigh), kimi 3, and the Ollama cloud seats glm-5.3, deepseek-v4-pro, minimax-m3 (+ the kimi-k3 substitute if configured).  **All four Ollama transports were confirmed REACHABLE by `api/tags` beforehand**, so a non-answer from any of them is a seat failure rather than a dead daemon.\n\n=== *** DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- NAMED, BECAUSE A SEAT HELD BACK AND A SEAT THAT FAILED ARE DIFFERENT THINGS AND CONFLATING THEM IS HOW A TWO-SEAT REVIEW GETS REPORTED AS A PANEL *** ===\n  * **agy 3.8** -- RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by standing owner ruling, and it is the reason both of these rounds have witnesses at all: it built the `armbdt` witness + kill table (25/25) and the `wdcaltdigin` witness + kill table (16/16) earlier today.  *Spending its bounded budget on a reading seven other seats can do is spending the roster's only independent-transport measuring capability on a fungible job.*\n  * **Opus 5** -- HELD.  It answers through the Agent tool and consumes the same Anthropic quota the owner wants preserved for the adjudication.  *Not a failure, not agreement: a budget decision.*\n  * **Fable 5** -- HELD BY EXPLICIT OWNER SEQUENCING: *'don't run fable until all other panel seats have had a say.'*  **This panel is the 'all other seats' step.**  Its availability is to be checked by a plain subagent rather than by Opus when the time comes.\n\n**WHY THE PACKET IS SHAPED AS IT IS:** every fact is INLINED -- both diffs, both full enclosing contexts, both measured kill tables, and **both first-pass NOT-RATIFIED findings** -- because the Ollama transports cannot read files and a brief containing *'check the source'* is a documented way to turn a live seat into a stall that looks exactly like a quota wall.  *The packet also asks the seats to attack the FIRST PASS'S FINDINGS, not only the code, so the corrections get adversarial scrutiny too.*\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING QUESTION PUT TO THEM IS THE SELECTOR ONE, and it is imported from a sibling round rather than invented: *** `sgirandomcolour` had **six mutants score a TOTAL ESCAPE** because every detector row probed a single position and each mutant conditioned the repair's effect on a tile or coordinate selector.  **`#524` has 25 rows and `#525` has 16, and neither has had a selector audit.**  The panel is asked to name a conditioning that a plausible edit could produce and the rows could not see.\n\n*Landing remains HELD by owner ruling until the full panel reports -- nothing is committed and no gate has run.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL (`_scratchpad/panel_20260911_103402`).  **SIX of seven scriptable seats answered substantively; the kimi CLI is WALLED and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute ANSWERED, so that seat is COVERED, not lost.** ***\n\n**Seat sizes and tails verified individually rather than by size alone:** codex 300,614 b (streams its reasoning; the tail is substantive), grok 11,840 b, `ollama_minimax` 12,053 b, `ollama_glm` 9,695 b, `ollama_kimi` 7,601 b, `ollama_deepseek` 4,219 b.  **`kimi.txt` is 350 b ending `See log: ...kimi-code.log` -- the CLI failure signature, NOT a considered non-answer.**\n\n**CONSENSUS ON THE PRODUCT CODE: NEITHER FIX IS WRONG.**  glm -- *'#524 is architecturally correct and its scope is what was overstated'*; minimax -- *'Both are correct, minimal, and correctly scoped'*; `kimi-k3` -- *'`addr & 0xfffffffc` per-access is provably equivalent'*; deepseek -- *'Neither fix is wrong as a local correction, but #524 is incomplete (combined handlers)'*, **independently reproducing the codex finding about `netbsd_memset`/`netbsd_memcpy`.**\n\n=== *** THE SELECTOR QUESTION GOT REAL ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE FLOOR-BREACH CANDIDATES *** ===\nI asked, importing the shape from [[sgirandomcolour]]: *what conditioning could a plausible edit introduce that the 25 and 16 rows never vary?*\n\n| round | proposed conditioning | seat | predicted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `#525` | **`if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`** | grok | **16/16 -- TOTAL ESCAPE** |\n| `#525` | `if (d->unit == 0) odata = 0;` -- a DRIVE-UNIT selector | deepseek | passes if every row uses the primary controller |\n| `#524` | `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096) addr &= ~3;` -- a constant property of the test map | deepseek | passes; breaks on any other page size |\n\n**grok also corrected my clause-(d) choice:** deleting `odata = 0` is NOT the discriminating mutant, *because `M_NULL_EFFECT` already scored 14/16* -- the detector sees it.  **The one that escapes keeps the assignment and gates it.**  *That is the difference between a mutant the rows catch and a mutant the rows are blind to, and I had picked the first and called it the floor test.*\n\n**PRESCRIPTION (grok):** *'Product diffs can stand.  The detectors cannot ship as the round's \"we pinned the repair\" claim until `#524` has a `+2` and a `+3` (or an anchored `& 3` mutant) and `#525` has one CORE observation with `WDCS_DRDY` clear.'*  **That is an L3 -- an undischarged obligation, where the row is the sole carrier of something the round claims.**\n\n*** AND A MEASURED-VS-PREDICTED DISCREPANCY IN MY OWN ARTIFACT, caught by grok and verified here: `PRETABLE.out:54` predicts `M_NULL_MEDIUM 4/25`; `SCORES.txt` measures **6/25**. ***  *I cited 6/25 -- the measured one -- so no record is wrong, but the PREDICTION sits in a durable artifact where a future reader can cite it as a measurement.  That is the frozen-number class with a two-row error already in the drawer.*  grok's reading of the remainder: *'PATH/ATTRIB-style controls surviving an early `return`'*.\n\n**Seats DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, by name: agy** (reserved for measurement; it built both witnesses today), **Opus 5** (held -- same Anthropic quota the adjudication needs), **Fable 5** (held by owner sequencing until every other seat has spoken -- **this panel is that step**).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 FABLE 5 ADJUDICATION (`FABLE_ADJ_20260911`).  **SEAT ALIVE -- token AND a verbatim `CHECKPOINT.md` HEAD quote returned, and it re-ran `git log --oneline -1` itself to confirm the checkpoint is not stale.**  Fired ONLY after all seven other scriptable seats had spoken, per owner sequencing, and via a subagent rather than Opus. ***\n\n=== RULING: **SHIP, after in-round RECORDS work.  Grok's L3 does NOT block it.** ===\n\n*** AND THE REASON IS A DOCTRINAL POINT I HAD WRONG: THE CLAUSE-(d) ENUMERATION HERE IS **EMPTY**, AND AN EMPTY ENUMERATION MUST BE RECORDED AS **N/A-WITH-REASON, NOT AS PASSED**. ***\n\nThe guarded body is `odata = 0;` -- **it reads nothing and tests nothing.**  The dominating predicates are `writeflag == MEM_READ` and `relative_addr != 0`, and **over a TWO-BYTE region `!= 0` admits no non-equivalent weakening.**  So DEPENDS-ON enumerates the empty set.  *This project's oldest rule about blanks applies exactly here: an unrun check and an inapplicable one must not render the same.*\n\n**Floor otherwise discharged from the artifacts, each cited:** (a) HEAD 14/16 red; (b) `M_REINSTATE` = HEAD; (c) `M_NULL_MEDIUM` fails a DIFFERENT pair -- all in `SCORES.txt` and the `.raw` files.\n\n=== *** TWO OF THE THREE PANEL SELECTOR MUTANTS DO NOT COMPILE *** ===\n  * `if (d->unit == 0)` -- **`struct wdc_data` HAS NO `unit` FIELD.**  Verified here: `unit` has **0 occurrences** in `dev_wdc.c`; the fields are `drive` (`:134`) and `base_drive` (`:57`).  *Not a mutant.*  Restated as `d->drive == 0` it is clause-(e) debt for the same reason as the DRDY one.\n  * `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096)` (this was `#524`'s) -- **`page_size` appears nowhere in `src/include/` except `thirdparty/alpha_rpb.h`**, an Alpha boot struct.  *Not a mutant.*  **DROPPED, with the field-name correction recorded so nobody rebuilds them.**\n  * `if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;` -- **clause-(e) DEBT, not floor**, on the identical test that governed [[sgirandomcolour]]'s `tile_nr == 0` today: *the body never reads or tests `odata`, it only writes it.*\n\n*** AND GROK'S OWN ROW SPEC WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, which is the sharpest catch in the adjudication: *** *'a DRDY-clear row with status 0x00 reads 0 under HEAD, fix and mutant alike.'*  **The working recipe, derived from `status_byte()` at `:584-600`:** DRDY is set iff `diskimage_exist(d->drive + d->base_drive)` and ERR iff `d->error`; a command to an IMAGELESS drive sets `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` (`:913`).  **So: SDH 0xb0, any command, read `0x3f7` -> HEAD `0x01`, fix `0x00`, DRDY-conjunct `0x01`.**  Two rows.  *`d->error` is one SHARED field (`:126`), so these rows must go LAST in their session* -- an ordering constraint that would have silently contaminated neighbours if missed.\n\n**RECORDS WORK OWED BEFORE IT SHIPS:** the L4 sibling fix (below, applied); one line citing ATA-2 X3T10/0948D Note 1, which the comment currently never mentions though `030e961` records it as governing and two seats raised it; a CHANGELOG `#525` block recording the `030e961` DD7 bit-7 row as **DISCHARGED BY SUBSUMPTION** (`CORE_READ_OFF1_*` want `0x00`) and naming the (e) disclosures.\n\n> *** AND A FINDING THAT APPLIES TO BOTH ROUNDS: NEITHER OF TODAY'S PROBES IS UNDER `regress/` PROPER, so *'shipped a detector with the fix'* IS NOT YET TRUE OF EITHER. ***  Promote per the `#494` precedent (`wdc_altoffset_probe.py`, wired into `gate_hygiene.sh:378`'s list).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 STEP 2 OF THE ADJUDICATED ORDER DONE: RECORDS WORK, INCLUDING THE ATA-2 WARRANT THE ROUND NEVER CITED. ***\n\n**(a) THE L4 SIBLING FIX -- APPLIED.**  `#494`'s write arm carried the identical claim this round's read-side comment retracts 34 lines above; the handler contradicted itself in one function.  Narrowed in place, with the provenance (*found by grepping for the sibling of a correction already called finished* -- mechanical rule 2, which I had skipped).\n\n**(b) THE ATA-2 CITATION -- APPLIED, and it is a STRENGTHENING rather than a tidy-up.**  Verified locally before writing, not taken from the adjudication: `_scratchpad/ata2_x3t10_948d.txt:1529-1531`, Note 1 -- *'This register is obsolete.  It is recommended that a device NOT RESPOND to a read of this address.  If a device does respond, it shall be sure NOT TO DRIVE THE DD7 SIGNAL to prevent possible conflict with floppy disk implementations.'*  **`dev_wdc.c` cited ATA-2 ZERO times before this** (measured), though `030e961` records the Note as governing and two seats raised it.\n\n> *** THREE CONSEQUENCES, AND THEY GIVE THE FIX A HARDWARE WARRANT WHERE IT HAD ONLY AN EMULATOR-POLICY ONE: *** **declining is the RECOMMENDED behaviour**, not merely a choice; **DD7 is bit 7 and `odata = 0` leaves it clear**, so the returned value satisfies the one constraint the standard places on a device that does answer; and **the floppy conflict the tree documents from both sides IS the reason the standard gives.**  *Zero stays an emulator policy WHERE THE STANDARD IS SILENT -- it fixes which non-DD7 value is returned -- but it is not arbitrary, and the comment now says exactly that much and no more.*\n\n**(c) THE CHANGELOG `#525` BLOCK -- WRITTEN** (106 lines, prepended; newest-first order verified, `#522` intact below).  It records the four-cell table, the measured legs, **clause (d) as N/A-WITH-REASON rather than passed**, the ATA-2 warrant, the **`030e961` DD7 row as DISCHARGED BY SUBSUMPTION** (both `CORE_READ_OFF1_*` rows want `0x00`, pinning every bit including DD7), the three clause-(e) disclosures including the two panel mutants that **do not compile**, and the seat ledger with the three held-back seats named.\n\n*** (d) STILL OWED, AND RECORDED RATHER THAN CLAIMED: THE PROBE IS UNDER `regress/artifacts/`, NOT `regress/` PROPER -- so *'shipped a detector with the fix'* IS NOT YET TRUE OF THIS ROUND. ***  The `#494` precedent is confirmed to exist (`regress/wdc_altoffset_probe.py`, referenced in `gate_hygiene.sh`).  **Deliberately NOT half-done:** a probe promoted into `regress/` that no gate executes is precisely [[probewiring]]'s defect, so the copy and the wiring land together, verified by a gate run, or neither lands.\n\n*Step 1 (the [[killtablebaseline]] repair) is complete and negative-controlled.  Step 3 -- the `M_MASK1` build for [[armbdt]] -- is RUNNING.*",
