.\regress\artifacts\wdcidselfcheck_20260925\panels\panel_20260925_174800\ollama_minimax.txt:51:**F14's disposition is the one I'd push back hardest on.** Adding one probe for a known compensating pair isn't the slippery slope the author invokes. The "unbounded growth" argument applies to "a probe per clever predicate", not to "a probe for a *named* failure mode of a *known* alternate predicate." If you accept the principle "every finite probe set admits some agreeing predicate", then by the same principle no test ever proves a comparator correct, which is too strong — the floor rules refuse that, and they should.
