.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260916_194735\ollama_minimax.txt:9:**Q3 — Per-cell disposition honest.** `23 + 8 + 10 + 5 = 46` ACCEPTS entries. The 5 HOST-DIAGNOSTIC resolve to F1 (ctl 4W 0x0201), F1b (ctl 8W 0x0201), F3 (sw +0x04), ENDIANESS 4W (ctl 4W 0x0100), ENDIANESS 8W (ctl 8W 0x0100) — clause-(e) debt per the 2026-09-04 amendment (multi-axis idata joint, ENDIANESS arm-clears-unconditionally, default-arm write line). The 23 ROWED resolve to the `p *` sessions P1-P6: REV window 4 cells (P1), value session 8 (P2), INTSTAT reassert 2 (P3), TIME pair 2 (P4/P5), residual 11 (P6), with one column already specified to overlap on REV 8R between P1 and P6 — net 23 ACCEPTS entries. The 10 EQUIVALENT resolve to the four named (REV 8W/4W/4R outside the window: prelude rewrites REV, high half is 0; CONTROL 4R: high half of `0x100` is 0) plus six unnamed; **I could not verify the six unnamed without the script — packet does not name them**. The 8 PINNED come from `opus_close7_20260915/scripts2/session_map2.py`, which is referenced but not inlined; **I could not verify the eight names — packet says "derived, not asserted"**. The `p *` rows vary under their own mutants (P1's `pc = ra` flips on accept/decline; P2's `s3`/`a1`/`a2` flip on accept/decline; P3's `cause.IP2` flips on the reassert channel; P4/P5's `v0` reflect the gap on the last 8R; P6's `s0`/`s1`/`s2`/`a0`/`a1`/`s3`/`ra`/`a2`/`t1` flip on accept/decline) — none read vacuous.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260916_194735\kimi.txt:125:      Now — **the enumeration's real soft spot for Q1**: the axes. Let me look for an axis the guard genuinely has that the product omits. The accept guard in the fixed code is `len == 8` / `len == 4` then per-access `writeflag` and `relative_addr & 4` (lane!) and the switch on relative_addr. **THE LANE BIT (`relative_addr & 4`)**: the 4-byte path branches on `relative_addr & 4` for BOTH reads and writes. Is the lane an axis of the enumeration? The enumeration: {latch} x {len 4,8} x {R,W} x {7 labels} — the lane is NOT an axis; it's folded into "label" via exact relative_addr matching in the mutants... but the mutants key on `relative_addr == CRIME_X` which only matches the LOW lane (label address). High-lane 4-byte accesses (+0x04, +0x0c, +0x14, +0x1c, +0x34, +0x3c) are all default-arm cells. So the enumeration's "default" label lumps all high lanes plus all unlabelled offsets. Is that honest? For the accept-shrink class, a mutant keyed on `relative_addr & 4` (all high lanes at once) — e.g. C-form `!(writeflag == MEM_READ && (relative_addr & 4) && latch)` on the 4-byte arm: declines ALL high-lane 4R post-latch. Post-latch high-lane 4R accesses: lw +0x04 (`p rev lw4`), lw +0x34 (`p dog rlo`), lw +0x40?? — +0x40 & 4 = 0 → low lane. +0x44 would be high. So that mutant reds `p rev lw4` and `p dog rlo` → caught. Keyed on low lanes: declines lw +0x00? (none post-latch... P1 lw +0x00 from window — red), lw +0x30 (`p dog rhi` — red), lw +0x40 (`p acc 40 lw` — red). Caught. So lane-axis mutants are caught even though the lane is not an enumeration axis. The enumeration is a generating set whose closure covers — the brief's stance: "the single-axis generating set being the floor", joints in debt.
