.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_073005\kimi.txt:341:  **Cell set (distinct (len, addr, dir) modulo value and window-pc):** The guard-growth mutants key on len/addr/writeflag (and pc for the window?). A grown conjunct could key on the pc too. The enumeration "cell" = ACCEPTS entry; value-distinguished entries share a cell for guards that don't test idata. But a guard CAN test idata (e.g. `len == 4 && !(relative_addr == CRIME_CONTROL && writeflag == MEM_WRITE && idata == 0x0201)` — decline only the 0x0201 write). Hmm — is that in the enumeration's scope? Item 6 says "an accept guard grown by a conjunct, so the cell falls into the announcing decline arm". A conjunct on idata at CONTROL would decline just one value-cell. Would latch said catch it? The latch x5 prologue contains ALL the CONTROL value cells: sw/sd × {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200, 0x0100}. A guard declining only sw 0x0201 (announced) → that prologue access announces → latch said red. ✓. So value-keyed announced declines are caught as long as the exact value cell is in ACCEPTS. A value-keyed decline of a value NOT in ACCEPTS (e.g. sw 0x02 at CONTROL)? That's not an ACCEPTS cell — it's outside item 6's "any single cell" claim (cells = ACCEPTS cells). Is it a floor gap? It would be a silent... no — announced: guard growth `len==4 && !(addr==CONTROL && writeflag==MEM_WRITE && idata==0x02)` → sw 0x02 declines WITH announce (latch path). Guest-visible? sw 0x02 accepted prints "unimplemented control 0x2"; declined announces the width text once and drops the write. Is there a row with sw 0x02 at CONTROL? No. Hmm. But wait — is this "clause (e)" territory? The generating set for CONTROL's accept side: the arm's branches are on DOG_ENABLE (0x200), HARD_RESET (0x800), leftover != 0. The values {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200} cover DOG×leftover; 0x0100 covers ENDIANESS (cleared unconditionally). A decline keyed on idata bit 1 (0x02) — bit 1 is not a branch the arm takes; it's "a conjunct on a value bit the arm never reads". Hmm — but the mutant isn't about the arm's behavior; it's about the DETECTOR. An accept guard keyed on bit 1: no ACCEPTS cell carries bit 1 in a CONTROL write value... 0x0201 = bits 0,9. 0x0001 = bit 0. 0x0200 = bit 9. 0x0100 = bit 8. So no prologue CONTROL write has bit 1 set. An announced decline keyed on (CONTROL, 4W, idata&0x02): declines nothing in any session (no row writes 0x02 at CONTROL... sw08 msg ctl is sw 0x01; sb08 refused; sh08b refused). So NO row turns red → 157/157. Is this a floor gap?
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_073005\kimi.txt:347:  Hmm, but is this "smallest edit" smaller than what I should report? Let me hold this thought and continue the systematic enumeration first. Also for Q3 I need to weigh whether this counts as "floor" or "clause (e) disclosed". The DISCLOSED list has: "a latch re-armed or pre-set by a CONTROL write's ENDIANESS bit (0x100) -- the arm clears it unconditionally and never branches on it; the two ENDIANESS writes in ACCEPTS since repair 11 are a strengthening, not a floor claim over value bits". So the project already treats value-bit conjuncts on bits the arm never branches on as... strengthening, not floor. A conjunct on bit 1 of idata at CONTROL — the arm DOES branch on leftover != 0, and bit 1 contributes to leftover. The value 0x0001 (bit 0) is in ACCEPTS and exercises leftover-nonzero. A decline keyed on bit 1 specifically... The detector's value cross covers the arm's generating set {DOG, HARD_RESET, leftover}: bit 1 only matters via leftover != 0, and leftover-nonzero is covered by 0x0001. So by the detector's own logic (generating-set over the arm's branches), a conjunct on bit 1 is equivalent to a conjunct on leftover != 0 at the level of observable arm behavior... but NOT at the level of the decline decision: the decline keyed on bit 1 declines sw 0x02 (which no row issues). Hmm, but wait — does any row issue an accepted CONTROL write with bit 1 set and check the result? sw 0x0201 has bit 1 clear. So no.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_073005\kimi.txt:359:  Disposition: I'll report it as a candidate GAP but classify it against the disclosed classes honestly: the contract's item 6 is about ACCEPTS cells; the detector explicitly disclaims value-bit floor claims (ENDIANESS bullet, "not a floor claim over value bits"). A conjunct on idata bit 1 at CONTROL 4W is exactly a value-bit conjunct the arm's generating set doesn't include... BUT the guest-visible effect (a dropped write + stolen announce channel vs the unimplemented-control text) is real. Hmm, hmm. Let me think about whether the announced form of it would even be guest-visible as "the fix undone": the fix's observable contract is "narrow widths announced once, read 0, write dropped; accepted widths handled as before". sw 0x02 handled as before = print text. Dropped + width-announce = different. So yes it undoes part of the fix's behaviour at that subcell.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_073005\kimi.txt:446:  Can a grown guard at some cell decline WITHOUT announcing while the latch is still clear? The announce decision: `if (!(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN))`. A grown guard declining a prologue cell → that decline announces (latch clear at prologue start) → sets bit → lh silenced → latch said red. Could the grown guard ALSO clear the latch? That's a conjunct on the guard, e.g. `len == 4 && (relative_addr != CRIME_CONTROL || writeflag != MEM_READ)` — single conjunct additions. A guard growth could be `len == 8 && relative_addr != CRIME_CONTROL && !(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN)`... that's growing by TWO conjuncts (a joint). Single conjunct: e.g., keyed on `d->warned`: decline only if latch already set? `len == 8 && (relative_addr != CRIME_CONTROL || !(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN))` — declines CONTROL 8 only when latch SET → in latch x5, prologue CONTROL 8 accesses happen with latch CLEAR → accepted → no steal → latch said green. Then lh announces. Any session where a CONTROL 8 access happens AFTER a refusal? rearm once: refusal lb +0x07 FIRST, then interleaved accepts (incl. ld/sd +0x08) each followed by lb +0x06. Under this mutant: the ld +0x08 in rearm once (after the first refusal, latch set) declines → announces?? The latch is SET → the announce block checks `!(warned & CRIME_W_LEN)` → already set → NO announce (normal decline path silences). Wait — the decline path IS the normal width arm; its announce logic is the latch. A declined access when latch set → no announce. So in rearm once, the CONTROL 8 declines are silent (latch set by the initial lb +0x07). Count stays 1 → rearm once green. Guest-visible: CONTROL 8R declined after first refusal → answers 0 instead of 0x100. Any row? acc ctl ld is a fresh session, no prior refusal → latch clear → ld +0x08 accepted → 0x100 ✓ green. So `|| !(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN)` conjunct at CONTROL 8R escapes?? Wait, but that's a guard keyed on d->warned — that's the RA3 class ("A d->warned-keyed shrink must be BUILT before it is disclosed"): W_8r = `len == 8 && (writeflag == MEM_WRITE || !(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN))` — keyed on 8R GENERALLY (all labels), measured KILLED by the keeps rows (they read seeded slots with accepted ld AFTER a refusal). W_8r is the whole-8R form. My variant is keyed on (CONTROL 8R AND latch-set) — a JOINT of two conjuncts (addr AND warned)... Actually W_8r's form: `len == 8 && (writeflag == MEM_WRITE || !(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN))` — declines 8R when latch clear. My variant: `len == 8 && (relative_addr != CRIME_CONTROL || writeflag != MEM_WRITE || !(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN))` — declines CONTROL 8R when latch SET. That's a 3-conjunct joint — beyond "single conjunct" growth. The enumeration is "guard grown by a conjunct". A conjunct `|| !(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN)` alone: `len == 8 && !(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN)` = declines ALL 8-byte accesses once latch set... that's the W_8w-ish form measured. W_8r measured killed. W_8w measured killed (lo keeps). So the single-conjunct d->warned form is measured for BOTH directions. The JOINT (addr × warned) is beyond single-conjunct — clause (e) by the generating-set argument. Fine — RA3 covered the single forms; joints are clause (e). OK.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_073005\kimi.txt:893:  Actually hold on — let me reconsider whether a value-keyed shrink is even guest-visible at CONTROL. Accepted sw 0x02: prints text, stores nothing (prelude resets CONTROL every access; the arm doesn't store idata to reg at all — looking at the arm: it modifies local idata and breaks; reg[1] = 0x100 from prelude. So a CONTROL write NEVER lands in reg[1]!). So the ONLY effect of accepting sw 0x02 is the host text. Declining it: announce (host text) + drop. Both host-text only. Guest-state equivalent! So a value-keyed shrink at CONTROL is guest-invisible anyway (host-text-only difference, uncounted at that value). At +0x40 (default arm): accepted sw 0x40 0x55 → lands in reg[8] high half → guest CAN read it back (lw +0x40). A value-keyed silent bypass (idata==0x55) → dropped → read-back 0. Guest-visible in principle; no row uses value 0x55. Hmm — so at the default arm, a value-keyed shrink IS guest-visible and unrowed. But again: value-bit subpartition. The detector's value discipline on the refusal side uses all-ones stores (every bit both ways across rows); on the accept side, each cell carries ONE value (mostly 0). A conjunct `idata != 0` at +0x40 4W: declines sw 0x40 with nonzero value — acc 40 sw uses 0x0a0b0c0d (nonzero) → declined → red ✓ (acc 40 sw catches the nonzero-value conjunct). A conjunct `idata == 0x55`: no row → escapes. This is the classic "can't row every value" — the generating-set argument covers the arm's BRANCH bits; the default arm has no branches on idata at all → any idata-keyed decline at a default-arm cell is beyond the branch generating set → clause (e), but not NAMED in DISCLOSED. The refusal side named it ("all-ones ... so a fall-through keyed on any bit a 1- or 2-byte store can carry shows (bits above 15 never reach a refused access, so a conjunct on them is equivalent)"). The accept side: acc rows carry ONE nonzero value per cell; a conjunct on a bit NOT set in that value escapes. E.g. at +0x40 4W, value 0x0a0b0c0d has bits {0,2,3,10,11,13,17,19,25,27} — a conjunct on bit 1 (sw 0x40 val 0x02) escapes; guest-visible (store dropped). Is THIS a floor gap? By the detector's own generating-set logic: the default arm has no idata branches, so idata is not a floor axis there — clause (e). DISCLOSED names the refusal-side analog via all-ones stores; the accept-side is not named. I'll present this as the second record note: "the accept side's value discipline is one value per cell; a shrink keyed on a value bit outside that value is guest-visible at store cells and unrowed — clause (e) by the no-branch-generating-set argument, but the DISCLOSED list doesn't say it."
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_073005\kimi.txt:895:  Hmm — wait, actually, for the SHRINK to be a "single conjunct on a guard", `len == 4 && relative_addr != 0x40` is the cell form. Value conjunct: `!(len == 4 && relative_addr == 0x40 && (idata & 2))` — that's growing the guard by a JOINT (addr AND value-bit) = two conjuncts? The guard-growth form is "grown by a conjunct" — a conjunct can be any predicate, including a compound one. The panel's lineage treats single-axis generating sets as the floor and joints as clause (e) (e.g., "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate ... clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor"; "the announce CHANNEL keyed on a JOINT of axes ... clause (e)"). So value×cell = joint → clause (e). Consistent with the project's own rules. Good — record note only.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_073005\kimi.txt:945:  Now — Q3's final answer: no floor gap (no edit that undoes the fix's contracted behaviour scores 157/157 outside the disclosed classes). The (sw,+0x04) bypass is guest-equivalent (not a behaviour undo at the guest). The value-subcell joints are clause (e) by the project's own single-axis generating-set rule; the accept-side value discipline is not NAMED in DISCLOSED (minor record note). One disclosed item possibly disclosed wrongly? Check: "a decline answering a selection of the register's bytes at CRIME_REV, CRIME_CONTROL or CRIME_TIME CONFINED to bytes the model holds at 0 there -- REV = 0x11: bytes 1-7" — a decline answering REV bytes 1-7 (all 0) answers 0 = same as the decline → equivalent ✓ true. "CONTROL = 0x100: byte 0 and bytes 2-7" — byte 0 of 0x100 is 0x00 ✓ (0x100 = byte 1 = 0x01). ✓. "TIME: the 4-byte half above the low word" — high half always 0 ✓ (uint32_t truncation) ✓.
