.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\ollama_glm.txt:7:REASONED, the floor under the joint-cell rule: 24 label x kind cells; 4 window x kind cells; 8 CONTROL write cells ({DOG set/clear} x {leftover nonzero/zero} x {4W, 8W}, with the value-0 cells realized as the plain 4W/8W at the label; RESET-true unrowable, disclosed); the default arm's six kinds in both 4-byte lanes; the two top cells. Total 44 floor + 2 strengthenings = 46 -- exactly the claim, and 43 + 3 = 46 matches the diff's single added line. The pass-5 hole (window x 8R) was the only missing floor cell; it is now present. No visible listed kind is untakeable (I checked all 20 inlined entries; `ldP' is takeable because the REV arm never tests direction -- READ). No fed row has a dead channel: each of the three added kinds has a measured keying that flips exactly the row it feeds (P1/P1b/P2/P2b at 140 -- READ from POSTTABLE 9).
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:20:  1. **Verify the enumeration.** Derive from `DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)`'s branches the (branch side × accepted kind) cells that are floor under the joint-cell rule, and say whether `ACCEPTS` (46 kinds) is exactly that set plus the two named clause-(e) strengthenings. Name any floor cell missing, any kind listed that the function cannot take, and any row it feeds whose channel cannot vary.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:52:  **The enumeration question (Q1)**: The floor for the latch pre-set/re-arm cross (ACCEPTS) is: every (branch side × kind) cell where an accepted access can go, such that a latch re-arm/pre-set keyed on that cell would be caught. The joint-cell rule: "a dominating predicate crossed with the body-tested kind partition".
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:66:  - CONTROL arm: body acts only on MEM_WRITE. Within the write: three predicates — DOG_ENABLE (0x200), HARD_RESET (0x800), ENDIANESS (0x100) — each cleared; then leftover != 0 → fatal. The generating set over value bits for CONTROL writes: {DOG, HARD_RESET, leftover} per the repair-11 comment. The brief says CONTROL's writes at both widths × {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200}: 0x0201 = DOG|leftover(0x01); 0x0001 = leftover only; 0 = nothing; 0x0200 = DOG only. Wait, where's HARD_RESET? HARD_RESET's true side is disclosed (stops CPUs, unrowable). So value-bits tested: {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200}. Plus repair 11 adds 0x0100 ENDIANESS at both widths — clause-(e) strengthening. But hold on: is {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200} a correct cross of the branch sides? The DOG branch: true side needs idata & 0x200. HARD_RESET true side: stops CPUs (unrowable). After clearing DOG/RESET/ENDIANESS, `if (idata != 0)` fatal — leftover branch. So the body-tested partition for CONTROL writes: DOG set (0x200), leftover nonzero (e.g. 0x01), both (0x0201), neither (0). That's 4 cells × 2 widths (4W at 0x08 = high lane... wait CONTROL is at 0x08; a 4-byte write at 0x08 targets the HIGH half of reg[1]. Hmm! So `sw 0x08 0x0201` writes 0x0201 << 32 into the register? And idata for a 4-byte write is memory_readmax64 of 4 bytes = the raw 4-byte value 0x0201. The switch's CONTROL arm reads `idata` raw — so `idata & 0x200` etc. work on the raw value regardless of lane. But `d->reg[relative_addr/8]` gets idata<<32 for the high lane. OK so the arm sees idata = 0x0201 raw. Fine.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:176:  - **Re-arm keyed on `extra`/`d` identity or cpu** — joint/unnamed, clause (e) disclosed? "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate ... or on one unnamed offset" — the generating set is single axes: width, direction, address bits, label, pc window, value bits {0x200,0x800,leftover}. Re-arm keyed on... hmm, what single axes exist that aren't crossed? Value bits at OTHER labels: e.g. re-arm keyed on idata & 1 at DOG write (accepted sd 0x30 with data 1). The DOG arm has no body — does the floor include DOG write value bits? The joint-cell rule: "a dominating predicate crossed with the body-tested kind partition". DOG's arm tests nothing. So a re-arm keyed on DOG-write value bit 0... ACCEPTS at DOG: what values? Probably all 0 (the tuple shows value 0 mostly). If all DOG accepted writes carry 0, a pre-set keyed on `idata & 1` at DOG would never fire in the prologue → and a re-arm keyed on `idata & 1` at DOG would never fire in rearm once → 141/141! Is that a floor gap or disclosed?
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:178:  Hmm wait — pre-set/re-arm keyed on a value bit at a non-CONTROL label. The enumeration's generating set: CONTROL's value bits are crossed {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200} because the CONTROL ARM branches on them. At other labels the arm doesn't branch on idata, so value bits are NOT floor — analogous to ENDIANESS (the arm never branches on it → strengthening, not floor). By the same rule, a latch keyed on `idata & 1` at DOG is outside the floor — a clause-(e) item. Is it disclosed? The ENDIANESS bullet covers "a latch re-armed or pre-set by a CONTROL write's ENDIANESS bit (0x100) -- the arm clears it unconditionally and never branches on it". It doesn't mention value bits at other labels. The general principle "the arm never branches on it" → not floor seems to be the stated rule. So by the joint-cell rule it's not a floor gap; it's an unrowed strengthening-class cell. But is it disclosed? Not explicitly! Hmm. The DISCLOSED list has "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset..." — a value-bit key at DOG is neither a joint of axes nor an unnamed offset. It's a value axis at a label whose arm doesn't test it. Not clearly disclosed. But also — would the rows catch it anyway? rearm once: every ACCEPTS kind between two refusals. DOG kinds carry value 0 (probably — need to check the actual ACCEPTS values at 0x30). If all zero, `idata & 1` re-arm never fires → passes. Also `d->warned = 0` keyed on `idata == 0` at DOG WOULD fire (values are 0) → caught. So the specific gap is value-bit-true at non-CONTROL labels. By the stated floor rule (arm doesn't branch → not floor), it's not floor. Consistent with ENDIANESS treatment. But the ENDIANESS case got rows anyway as strengthening, and got a disclosure. The DOG/INTSTAT/TIME/etc. value-bit case is the same class, undisclosed — I'd call it a Q3 nit or a Q2 non-floor survivor. Hmm, actually wait: INTSTAT/INTMASK writes — the arm calls crime_interrupt_reassert(d), which doesn't read idata. TIME arm: crime_update_crime_time(d) — doesn't read idata. REV arm reads pc only. Default arm: prints data bytes (debug) — doesn't branch. So idata value bits at non-CONTROL labels: never branched on → not floor by the rule. OK.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:184:  B. **A re-arm keyed on the REV-window pc predicate with a REFUSED access**: e.g. `if (pc in window && len == 1) d->warned = 0;` placed... where? If placed in the decline arm — that's a latch keyed on pc — the latch rows refuse from various pcs? The latch x5 rows: warm once (sb, write, odd) etc. Do any refused accesses come FROM the PROM window pc? Probably not — refused probes run at normal pcs. A latch re-arm keyed on pc-in-window on the REFUSAL path... hmm, but re-arm means clearing warned before a refusal announces. Scenario: first refusal announces (warned was 0... wait if pre-set keyed on pc-window-refusal... this is getting deep. The latch tests: first refusal announces once, subsequent refusals silent. A pre-set keyed on (pc in window AND refused) would only matter if a refusal comes from the window — probe rows don't do that (rev pc rows are ACCEPTED from the window? No wait — "CRIME_REV (+0x00), reads AND writes, from pc 0xbfc05180: the PROM-probe hack sets pc = ra (it does not test the direction) lb/lh/sb/sh rev pc" — those are REFUSED (lb/lh/sb/sh) from the window! So refused accesses DO come from pc 0xbfc05180 in the rev pc rows. A latch keyed on pc-window on the refusal path: in the rev pc session, the first refusal is from the window → a pre-set there would silence... but each session's first refusal must announce (said rows: rev said). Session structure: rev pc session: refused lb/lh/sb/sh from window, each... hmm, if the latch pre-sets on the first window-refusal, the announce is skipped → rev said row catches it? "rev said" = the FIRST refusal's text in the rev label-first session. If pre-set keyed on pc-window fires at that first refusal... the pre-set must fire BEFORE the announce check. If the mutant is `if (pc in window) d->warned |= CRIME_W_LEN;` at function entry, then the first refusal from the window doesn't announce → rev said catches. But careful: rev said session — is the FIRST refusal in that session from the window? The rev pc rows are refused from the window; rev said is at +0x00... from what pc? Probably a normal pc. Different sessions. Ugh — without the probe file I can't be sure. But this is a latch keyed on the pc axis on the refusal path — the single-axis generating set includes... hmm, the pc axis is a property of the access, like address bits. Item 4's keyed-latch class: "width 1 or 2 and direction R or W ... each label first ... address parity ... address bits". The pc axis: is a latch keyed on pc (window vs not) at the refusal in the generating set? The said rows: "every said row sat at an offset with bit 1 set -- rev said is at +0x00, every address bit clear" — that was about address bits and suppression. The pc of refused accesses: most rows run at some probe pc; the rev pc rows run at 0xbfc05180. A latch keyed on pc-in-window: first refusal in latch x5 session is at... +0x07 (warm once? no — latch x5 is lh, read, even...). Argh.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:404:  So strictly: ACCEPTS is NOT exactly "the floor set + 2 strengthenings" — it contains 4 additional duplicate-kind entries (0x04 sw/lw, 0x278 sd/ld). But wait — are those duplicates really redundant? Think about what rearm once / latch x5 prologue do: each ACCEPTS entry is executed between refusals (rearm) or before the first refusal (pre-set). A re-arm keyed on `relative_addr == 0x278` (an unnamed offset) is in the disclosed clause-(e) class ("one unnamed offset"), so the 0x278 entries test that class by measurement, not floor. Actually they're the far-block representatives — the brief's history mentions "in the middle block (an accepted sd between two refusals there) mid once" separately. The 0x278 pair presumably guards a re-arm/pre-set keyed on the far block (bit 9, >= 0x200) — which is an address-bit axis, part of the single-axis generating set... The latch axis analysis says every address bit 0-9 carried both ways by refusals; for ACCEPTS the far-block entries carry bit 9 on accepted accesses. A pre-set keyed on `relative_addr >= 0x40` was found on the closing pass (per item 4: "keyed on CRIME_CONTROL, on `relative_addr >= 0x40', on address parity"). So address bits on the accept side matter; 0x40 entries carry bit 6; 0x278 carries bits 3,4,5,6,7,9. Hmm — but by the joint-cell rule (dominating predicate × body-tested kind partition), the default arm has no address-bit branches, so 0x278 is not floor; it's strengthening/extra-evidence. Fine — but then the answer to "is ACCEPTS exactly the floor set plus the two named strengthenings" is: NO, not exactly — it also carries 4 extra entries beyond floor+2 (0x04 pair, 0x278 pair), which are defensible as strengthening (they pin address-bit axes / the window-end accept cells item 6 uses), but the brief's own framing ("exactly the ... floor ... with the contract saying which cells are floor and which are clause-(e) strengthening") records only the ENDIANESS pair as strengthening. The 0x04/0x278 extras are NOT recorded as strengthening in the contract text — the contract says "and the default arm's six kinds in both 4-byte lanes" (that's 6; the list has 8 default-ish entries... plus the 2 far-block = 10 non-label entries). Hmm wait, let me recount from the contract's own enumeration text: "{8W, 8R, 4W, 4R} at every case label" (6 labels × 4 = 24), "REV also from the PROM-probe window at all four kinds" (+4 = 28), "CONTROL's writes at both widths × {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200}" (+8 = 36), "plus 0x0100 ENDIANESS at both widths" (+2 = 38), "and the default arm's six kinds in both 4-byte lanes" (+6 = 44). That's 44. Plus... the contract says 46 with the ENDIANESS pair... 44 includes the 2 ENDIANESS? 24+4+8+2+6 = 44. But ACCEPTS has 46 entries. Discrepancy of 2!
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:414:  OK here's the cleaner statement for Q1: The floor derived from the function's branches is 40 cells. ACCEPTS realizes all 40 (including, since repair 11, the 8R window cell ldP). It then carries 6 beyond-floor entries: the 2 named ENDIANESS strengthenings AND 4 more ((sw,0x04),(lw,0x04),(sd,0x278),(ld,0x278)) that duplicate already-rowed default-arm kinds at second addresses. Those 4 are not floor under the joint-cell rule and are not recorded as strengthening in the contract — the contract's own enumeration ("the default arm's six kinds in both 4-byte lanes") covers 6, but the list carries 8 default-arm entries + 2 far-block entries. So the statement "ACCEPTS, 46 kinds" as "the CROSS ... plus the two named strengthenings" is arithmetically off: the cross is 40 cells + 2 strengthening = 42; the extra 4 entries are unrecorded duplicates/strengthening-by-address. Hmm wait — am I sure the 0x278 pair isn't floor? The far block >= 0x200: the switch's default arm. relative_addr 0x278 is within DEV_CRIME_LENGTH 0x280. No branch distinguishes it from 0x40. Under the joint-cell rule: the dominating predicate for the default arm is "not any label"; the kind partition gives 6 kinds. Address bits are not body-tested. So 0x278 8W/8R = same cells as 0x40 8W/8R. Not floor. Right.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:475:  - **The `is_32bit` branch inside the redirect** (mips32_pc_to_pointers vs mips_pc_to_pointers): the machine is 64-bit (R5000-ish? O2 = R5000/Rm7000, 64-bit) → is_32bit false → only one side reachable on this probe. A re-arm inside the is_32bit side would never fire here — unrowable on this machine, like use_fb. Is it disclosed? Hmm! The DISCLOSED list mentions machine-configuration lvalues (use_fb, flag_for_128MB) and HARD_RESET's true side. The `cpu->is_32bit` branch side is NOT mentioned! A re-arm/pre-set inside `if (cpu->is_32bit) mips32_pc_to_pointers(cpu);` — e.g. `if (cpu->is_32bit) { d->warned = 0; mips32_pc_to_pointers(cpu); }` — on this probe (64-bit ip32) never fires → 141/141. Is that a floor gap? The branch IS one of "the function's own branches", and the redirect arm IS rowed (lwP takes the window branch). But the is_32bit sub-branch's true side is unreachable on a 64-bit machine — same status as use_fb=1 (graphics). The disclosed machine-config bullet covers "a decline answering a MACHINE-CONFIGURATION lvalue (`d->use_fb`, `flag_for_128MB`)". is_32bit is also machine configuration... but the bullet is specifically about ANSWERING (odata source), not about re-arm/pre-set keyed on machine config. Hmm. A re-arm keyed on is_32bit: where would it fire? Only when the redirect branch fires (REV window accepted) AND is_32bit. On this probe: never. So `if (cpu->is_32bit) d->warned = 0;` inside the REV window branch passes 141/141. Floor or not? By the joint-cell rule: the is_32bit branch is body-tested? No — on this probe it's a constant. Like HARD_RESET's true side (unrowable, disclosed) and use_fb (unrowable on one machine, disclosed). The is_32bit case is NOT disclosed explicitly. Is it covered by "Unrowable on one machine, like the function-static latch below"? That sentence is inside the machine-configuration-lvalue bullet about ANSWERING. A strict reader: the enumeration claim "is ACCEPTS now exactly the (branch side × kind) floor the function's own branches define" — the is_32bit true side is a branch side the function defines, crossed with the REV-window kinds, and it's NOT in ACCEPTS and CANNOT be (64-bit machine). So the honest answer: the enumeration has one more unrowable cell that the DISCLOSED list does not name: the `cpu->is_32bit` true side within the REV redirect branch. Also the RAM-check branches above the width arm (`>1024`, `% mb_per_bank`) — those exit(1), and they're "filed, not here" (disclosed). The bank-loop branches: prelude, not kind-dependent.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:529:  (hh) **The ENDIANESS strengthening**: P2/P2b measured red — re-arm/pre-set keyed on `idata & CRIME_CONTROL_ENDIANESS` at CONTROL write. The 0x0100 cells fire it. ✓ — but note: those cells only catch a latch keyed on ENDIANESS *at CONTROL writes*. A latch keyed on ENDIANESS bit at a NON-CONTROL accepted write (e.g. `idata & 0x100` at DOG sd)? All DOG ACCEPTS writes carry 0 → bit clear → pre-set never fires / re-arm never fires → 141/141! Is that floor? The DOG arm doesn't branch on idata → not floor (same rule as ENDIANESS at CONTROL). Disclosed? The ENDIANESS bullet is specifically about CONTROL writes. A value-bit-keyed latch at a non-CONTROL label is the same class (arm never branches on the value) → not floor → clause-(e) strengthening territory, undisclosed but consistent with the stated rule. Hmm — is it? The rule says the single-axis generating set is the floor; value bits at arms that test them (CONTROL's {DOG, RESET, leftover}) are the generating set; value bits elsewhere are not. So not floor. But not "disclosed" either — it's just outside the floor by rule. OK.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:545:  (oo) **A re-arm/pre-set keyed on `relative_addr & 4` for len==8?** len 8 ignores the lane bit... the predicate `relative_addr & 4` on 8-byte accesses: ACCEPTS 8-byte entries at 0x00,0x08(!),0x10,0x18,0x30,0x38,0x40,0x278 — 0x08, 0x18, 0x38, 0x278 have bit 3 or... `& 4` = bit 2: 0x08 & 4 = 0, 0x278 & 4 = 0... hmm 0x278 = 0b1001111000 → bit 2 = 0? 0x278 & 4: 0x278 = 632; 632 & 4 = 0 (632 = 512+64+32+16+8 → 0b1001111000; bit 2 (value 4) = 0). So all sd/ld ACCEPTS offsets have bit 2 = 0! A pre-set keyed on (len==8 && (relative_addr & 4))? No 8-byte ACCEPTS entry has bit 2 set → never fires → 141/141! Floor? The function's width arm for len==8 does NOT branch on relative_addr & 4 → not floor (the kind partition for len 8 has no lane split). Address-bit-keyed pre-sets on accepted accesses: is there a row? The generating-set for accepted accesses: kind × label. Address bits within an arm: not body-tested → not floor. Same class as 0x04/0x278 extras (which DO carry some bits). Wait — but do the ACCEPTS entries collectively carry every address bit both ways for each kind? For len 8: offsets {0x00,0x08,0x10,0x18,0x30,0x38,0x40,0x278}: bit 3: set at 0x08,0x18,0x38,0x278; clear at 0x00,0x10,0x30,0x40. bit 4: 0x10,0x18,0x30,0x38,0x278 set; others clear. bit 5: 0x30,0x38,0x278. bit 6: 0x40,0x278. bit 7,8: 0x278 (0x278 = 0b1001111000 → bits 3,4,5,6,9). bit 9: 0x278 only. bit 2: NEVER set for 8-byte kinds! bit 1, 0: never (8-aligned). So a pre-set keyed on bit 2 of an accepted 8-byte access is invisible. Floor? No — the function doesn't branch on bit 2 for len 8. Not floor. Fine — consistent.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:668:  So: is "ACCEPTS = the floor + the two named strengthenings" exactly true? No — there are 4 unrecorded duplicate-kind entries. Is any of them doing work? (sw,0x34,0x0badcafe): the SEED value 0x0badcafe — hmm, does the seed matter for the rearm/pre-set rows? The rows only count announces. The seed doesn't matter there. But wait — the 0x34 write with a nonzero seed: in latch x5's prologue, before the first refusal... any effect? It writes DOG's low half via default arm. Harmless. As a pre-set trip: same kind as sw 0x04 (4W-low). Redundant. The 0x278 pair: 8W/8R at the far block — same kinds as sd/ld 0x40. Redundant as kinds; valuable as far-block address-bit coverage (bit 9) — a pre-set keyed on `relative_addr >= 0x200`! Is that floor? Not by the joint-cell rule (default arm doesn't branch on address). But the axis analysis for the LATCH (item 4) says address bits are carried both ways by refusals; for the ACCEPT side (pre-set/re-arm), bit 9 set only by the 0x278 pair. A pre-set keyed on bit 9 would be caught by the 0x278 pair (prologue includes sd 0x278 → fires → latch x5 = 0). Without the pair: invisible. So the 0x278 pair DOES close a real (single-axis: address bit 9) pre-set/re-arm cell! Hmm — is address bit 9 a floor axis for accepted accesses? The brief's own floor rule for ACCEPTS: "a dominating predicate crossed with the body-tested kind partition". The function's branches don't test address bits for accepted accesses (only the switch's exact labels). So bit-9-keyed pre-set is NOT floor — it's a strengthening of the same standing as ENDIANESS, but UNRECORDED as such. Similarly the 0x04 pair: bits... 0x04 has bit 2 set; the 0x34 pair has bits 2,4,5 (0x34 = 0b110100). The other default entries (0x40) have bit 6. Collectively accepted-access address bits: covered variously. A pre-set keyed on bit 2 (`relative_addr & 4`) on an accepted 4-byte LOW-lane write — fires at sw 0x34/0x04 → caught. At bit 2 with len 8: never (no 8-byte entry at a bit-2 address — 8-aligned never has bit 2... 8-aligned means bits 0-2 clear! So bit 2 with len 8 impossible). OK.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:680:  CONTROL reads both widths ✓ (lw, ld). CONTROL writes: the generating set {DOG, RESET, leftover}: cells that return: DOG×leftover 4 combos × 2 widths = 8 ✓. RESET-true: stops CPUs → disclosed unrowable ✓. But hmm — RESET × DOG × leftover: the cell (RESET set, DOG set/clear, leftover) — all stop CPUs → disclosed as one side. ✓ ENDIANESS: no branch → strengthening ✓ named.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:1003:  5. Now, the enumeration question once more. Q1 asks specifically: "Derive ... the (branch side x accepted kind) cells that are floor under the joint-cell rule (a dominating predicate crossed with the body-tested kind partition), and say whether ACCEPTS (46 kinds) is exactly that set plus the two named clause-(e) strengthenings. Name any floor cell missing, any kind listed that the function cannot take, and any row it feeds whose channel cannot vary."
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:1009:  Hmm — hold on, let me reconsider: maybe the intended reading of "the default arm's six kinds in both 4-byte lanes" is 6 kinds realized by 8 entries: the low-lane kinds deliberately at TWO sites (+0x04 = REV's low lane, +0x34 = DOG's low lane) because those sites are semantically distinct (adjacent to named labels)? Under the joint-cell rule they're the same cell. The enumeration sentence says "six kinds" — 6 ≠ 8 entries. So the text/list mismatch is real regardless of interpretation: the list carries 46 entries; the floor per the text's own enumeration is 40 cells (realizable in 40 entries: 24 label + 4 window + 8 CONTROL-write + 2 CONTROL-read + 6 default... wait that's 44. Hmm! Let me recount: 24 label kinds includes CONTROL's 4 (2 reads + 2 writes at value 0). Then "CONTROL's writes at both widths × {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200}" = 8 — the value-0 cells are shared with the label 24. So distinct floor cells: 24 (labels) + 4 (window) + 6 (CONTROL write cells beyond value-0: 0x0201, 0x0001, 0x0200 × 2 widths) + 6 (default) = 40. Yes 40 distinct floor cells. Entries to realize them: 40. Plus ENDIANESS 2 = 42. Actual 46 → 4 extra entries.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:1013:  Hmm wait, actually — I want to double check the bit-9 claim. A pre-set keyed on `relative_addr & 0x200` on an ACCEPTED access: latch x5's prologue contains sd 0x278 and ld 0x278 → fires → pre-set → first refusal silent → latch x5 red. Without the 0x278 pair: no accepted access in the prologue has bit 9 set → never fires → green. So yes the 0x278 pair closes a real cell — but under the joint-cell rule that cell is NOT floor (the function branches nowhere on bit 9). It's strengthening. The brief records only the ENDIANESS pair as strengthening. So the contract under-records. Fine — that's my Q1 answer with a named imprecision, not a missing floor cell.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260915_011138\kimi.txt:1021:  And **the is_32bit branch inside the redirect**: true side unrowable on this machine (64-bit ip32) — undisclosed unrowable cell. Q1: "name any floor cell missing" — the is_32bit true side is a branch side the function defines; under the joint-cell rule it's a dominating predicate... but it cannot be crossed on this probe (like HARD_RESET's true side, which IS disclosed). The DISCLOSED list does not name it. I'll name it: not a rowable gap on this machine, but a disclosure-completeness miss — same class as HARD_RESET-true (disclosed) and use_fb (disclosed), so by the brief's own standards it belongs on the list.
