.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\ollama_glm.txt:19:READ (header, DISCLOSED): "a decline answering a selection of the register's bytes at CRIME_REV, CRIME_CONTROL or CRIME_TIME that the model holds at 0 there -- **the ADDRESSED lanes (bytes 0-1 at a label)**, or the 4-byte half above the low word -- is EQUIVALENT." READ (same header, item 9, and the inlined prelude): REV = 0x11, CONTROL = 0x100, TIME = 66-990. REASONED: the addressed halfword lanes are 0x0011, 0x0100, and 66-990 -- byte 0 at REV and byte 1 at CONTROL are nonzero, so answering them is *not* equivalent, and item 9's own v0 rows (lb/lh rev v0, ctl v0, time sd lb/lh v0) exist to kill exactly that. The zero-held lanes are bytes 1-7 at REV, bytes 0 and 2-7 at CONTROL, bytes 2-7 at TIME; the "half above the low word" alternative is right, the "addressed lanes" alternative is false at all three registers named -- a drafting slip for "above the addressed lanes," which item 9's bracket states correctly. Secondary: the same list's "the single-axis generating set being the floor" is not a true description of the 141-row floor (items 4/8 row the two-axis cross because measured joint survivors were ruled floor breaches).
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:219:  Hmm wait, but actually — is "8R from window" really distinguishable from "8R not from window" as a KIND? The function's pc test is `if ((pc & 0xffffff00) == 0xbfc05100)` — it doesn't test width. A mutant keyed on `len==8 && writeflag==READ && pc-window` is a JOINT predicate (width × direction × window). The contract's DISCLOSED list includes "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME)... clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." Hmm, that disclosure is about latch keyed on joint predicate over REFUSAL axes. For pre-set/re-arm over ACCEPTED kinds, the cross is KIND x LABEL — and the "kind" axes are width(4) × direction(2) × window(in/out for REV). A re-arm keyed jointly on {8R, REV, in-window}... the enumeration covers lwP (4R in-window), sdP (8W in-window), swP (4W in-window), and ld/sd/lw/sw at 0x00 out-window. The cell (8R, in-window) is missing. A single-axis mutation `pc in window → re-arm` would fire on lwP/swP/sdP → caught. `len==8 → re-arm` fires on sdP/sd → caught. `direction==R` fires on lwP/ld → caught. But the joint {8R ∧ in-window} escapes — that specific joint cell has no row. Under the "single-axis generating set is the floor" clause (e) doctrine, joint predicates are disclosed debt. But wait — the disclosure says joint predicates are clause-(e) debt for the LATCH keyed on refusal axes. For the accepted-kind cross, the brief claims the cross is complete: "is `ACCEPTS` exactly the function's accepted-access kinds". If kinds are width × direction × window-side, then 8R-in-window IS a kind the function can take (an ld at 0x00 from pc 0xbfc05180 is a real accepted access that takes the redirect branch), and ACCEPTS omits it. Actually hmm, does it even make sense: lwP, swP, sdP are included because... the redirect branch is width-agnostic. If you include lwP, swP, sdP, why not ldP? The set {lwP, swP, sdP} covers window-in × {4R, 4W, 8W} — missing 8R. Since the window branch ignores direction AND width, one might argue any one kind suffices for the branch side; they included 3 for direction × width coverage. The full cross would be 2 widths(4/8... and 4-hi only since 0x00 & 4 == 0... actually ld, sd, lw, sw = 4 kinds) × in-window = 4 more: ldP missing.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:232:  But — is a joint-predicate re-arm within the contract clause (e)? The contract item 8 says "an accepted access between two refusals does not re-arm the announce... closed over every place an accepted access can go". If the place is "8R from the PROM window at REV" and no row goes there, the closure claim fails. However, the DISCLOSED joint-predicate bullet might cover it: "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." This is about THE LATCH (item 4), keyed on refusal axes. The re-arm/pre-set closure claims the KIND x LABEL cross. The mutant I propose keys on a joint predicate {width=8, dir=R, window=in} — the generating-set doctrine says single axes generate the floor; joints are debt. A review seat could argue it's disclosed by that bullet. But the bullet's example and framing are about the latch on refusals, not about re-arm on accepted kinds. Adversarially, I should NAME it and let the verdict reflect it. Actually wait — is it even necessary to be joint? Consider `if (writeflag == MEM_READ && len == 8)` — that's joint too. Any single-axis key is caught by the cross. Hmm, what about a re-arm keyed on the redirect branch being TAKEN (pc changed)? Same as window-in single axis — caught by lwP/swP/sdP.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:242:  Hmm wait, actually there's a subtlety — what about `sw 0x0100` at 0x08 — would rows "sw08 msg" catch it? The mutant doesn't print; it re-arms. The row that counts announces is rearm once. If no accepted CONTROL write in the rearm loop has 0x100 set, and no other session does an accepted 0x100 write between refusals, it's a survivor. THIS IS A CANDIDATE GAP. But hold on — is keying on `idata & 0x100` "narrowing by ADDRESS/width/direction"? No, it's keyed on the VALUE. The contract's enumeration for CONTROL writes is explicitly DOG × leftover (four cells) — it treats the value space as generated by {DOG, leftover}. The ENDIANESS bit (0x100) set-side is a value cell not in the generating set. Given "CONTROL's writes at both widths x {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200} (DOG x leftover, all four)" — the brief asserts these four suffice. Adversarially: a pre-set/re-arm keyed on `idata & CRIME_CONTROL_ENDIANESS` (or on `idata == 0x100`, or on any bit outside {0x200, low-byte leftover}) — hmm wait, leftover nonzero covers bits other than 0x200/0x800/0x100... no! leftover is idata AFTER masking DOG, RESET, ENDIANESS. The four cells: 0x0201 (DOG set, leftover nonzero), 0x0001 (DOG clear, leftover nonzero), 0 (DOG clear, leftover zero), 0x0200 (DOG set, leftover zero). The RESET bit: 0x0800 — its set side stops CPUs (disclosed). The ENDIANESS bit: 0x0100 — its set side is NOT in any cell! 0x0100 → after mask: 0 → (DOG clear, leftover zero) cell = same cell as 0. So the enumeration treats 0x0100 as equivalent to 0. For the ARM's actual behavior it is equivalent (masked, no fatal). But for a MUTANT keyed on the 0x100 bit, 0x0100 differs from 0 — and no row writes 0x0100. So: mutant `case CRIME_CONTROL: if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) { if (idata & 0x100) d->warned |= CRIME_W_LEN; ...` — pre-set on the 0x100 cell. Under latch x5 prologue: CONTROL writes are 0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200 (sw and sd) — none sets 0x100. So the latch is NOT pre-set → announce counts normal → 141/141. This is exactly analogous to the c1_deepseek mutant (keyed on idata == 0x0200 after the DOG block — which was EQUIVALENT because the DOG block already cleared 0x200, making it dead code). But `idata & 0x100` is NOT dead — real accepted writes can carry 0x100 (e.g., a guest writing 0x0100 or 0x0101, 0x0300, etc.). The probe just never does it.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:248:  Hmm wait, wait. Let me double-check the claimed equivalence: "bits above 15 never reach a refused access, so a conjunct on them is equivalent" — that's about REFUSED accesses (sb/sh carry ≤ 0xffff). But ACCEPTED writes (sw/sd) carry all 32/64 bits. The value cells for accepted CONTROL writes only span {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200}. Bits outside {0x0201} ∪ ... any bit other than 0x200, 0x1 — e.g. 0x100, 0x400, 0x1000, 0x10000... — a key on any of them survives. 0x100 is the most natural (it's a named mask in the arm). Also 0x400, 0x8000, etc. — but those are "any other bit" — same class. The contract item 3 rows refused stores with all-ones (sb 0xff, sh 0xffff) to catch fall-through keyed on any bit a narrow store can carry; but ACCEPTED CONTROL writes don't carry all-ones anywhere. So the class "latch keyed on an accepted CONTROL write carrying bit X" for X ∉ {0x200, 0x1} is unrowed. Unless disclosed: DISCLOSED has "a re-arm or pre-set inside the HARD_RESET branch... Unrowable". The 0x100 cell is rowable but unrowed. Is it covered by "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate... clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor"? The generating set for CONTROL values is {DOG × leftover} — bit 0x100 is an axis outside the named generating set. Hmm. The brief's item 4 says "CONTROL's writeflag x DOG x leftover sides" — explicitly three axes: writeflag, DOG, leftover. ENDIANESS-bit-set is not a side of any named axis. So I can name this as a gap: the accepted-write value enumeration at CONTROL omits the ENDIANESS-bit-set cell (sw/sd 0x0100 or 0x0101), and a pre-set/re-arm keyed on that bit scores 141/141.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:335:  But WAIT — is this within the contract's clause (e)? The contract item 4/8 claim the cross is {kinds at labels} with CONTROL's writes at the four values. The mutant's key (0x100 bit) is a value cell outside the enumerated four. The enumeration's own rationale: the four values cross DOG × leftover. The `if (idata != 0)` leftover test happens AFTER `idata &= ~CRIME_CONTROL_ENDIANESS` — so for the FUNCTION's behavior, 0x100-carrying writes ≡ non-carrying. But the mutant key isn't about the function's behavior; it's about test observability. The test cannot distinguish a pre-set keyed on 0x100 because no row feeds 0x100. This is a genuine floor gap relative to the claim "closed over every place an accepted access can go" — well, actually, is it? "Every place an accepted access can go" — the place is the CONTROL arm write path; the cross rows it. But the MUTANT only fires for a subset of values the probe never uses. Hmm. The contract claims closure over branch sides; the 0x100 bit is not a branch side in the FIXED function (the clear is unconditional). So under the contract's stated floor (single-axis generating set from the function's own branches), is a key on a masked bit inside the floor or not? The contract item 3 explicitly worried about this for REFUSED stores: "plus ALL-ONES refused writes, sb 0xff and sh 0xffff (kimi CLI, closing pass), so a fall-through keyed on any bit a 1- or 2-byte store can carry shows (bits above 15 never reach a refused access, so a conjunct on them is equivalent)". So the doctrine: a conjunct keyed on a bit that no row carries is EQUIVALENT — but that doctrine was applied to refused accesses where bits are physically limited by width. For ACCEPTED CONTROL writes, bits above 15 CAN reach the arm (sw/sd carry them) — the probe just chooses not to carry them. By the contract's own logic ("keyed on any bit a store can carry"), an accepted sw/sd CAN carry 0x100, so the enumeration should include a CONTROL write with 0x100 set (e.g. 0x0101 or 0x0300... wait 0x0300 has DOG; 0x0101: ENDIANESS + leftover). The natural cell: sw/sd 0x0100 (ENDIANESS only → leftover zero after clear) and/or 0x0101. The omission means: pre-set/re-arm keyed on `idata & 0x100` survives. I'm fairly confident this is a REAL GAP. It mirrors c1_deepseek (keyed on == 0x0200 post-DOG-block → dead code → equivalent) — but 0x100 is NOT dead code; it's a live value cell the probe never feeds.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:553:  - One more possible omission: "REV also from the PROM-probe window in both directions" — directions covered: 4R (lwP), 4W (swP), 8W (sdP); 8R missing. Is 8R-from-window a distinct accepted kind? The pc-window if doesn't test width, so branch-wise {in-window × 8R} is not a separate branch side — the same `if` side is taken by lwP/swP/sdP. Under the "sides of every if" doctrine, the window's IN side is covered 3 times; the cross with width is the KIND x LABEL doctrine — the fourth pass's own complaint was marginals-not-crossed: kinds {8W,8R,4W,4R} crossed with window {in,out}: in-side has 8W(sdP), 4W(swP), 4R(lwP) — missing 8R. A re-arm keyed on `len == 8 && writeflag == MEM_READ && pc-window` (a JOINT predicate, three axes) survives. The DISCLOSED list has "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate ... clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor". A re-arm is not exactly "a latch keyed on" — but pre-set/re-arm are the latch's two error directions, and the bullet's intent covers keyed-on-joint-predicate latch manipulations. Hmm, borderline. The 0x100 finding is single-axis (one bit of the write value), so it's the cleaner gap. Note the joint-predicate bullet speaks of the latch "keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME)" — the example is refusal-side; for the accepted side, the contract claims the full KIND x LABEL cross, and the brief asks "is ACCEPTS exactly the function's accepted-access kinds". Answer: no — ldP (8R from the window) is an accepted kind the function takes and ACCEPTS omits; and CONTROL-write values carrying 0x100 (or any bit outside {0x200,0x1}) are accepted kinds omitted. Also arguably 4-byte accesses at 0x0c (CONTROL+4) hit default — covered by the default-arm low-lane kinds.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:629:    Actually hold on, let me steelman the defense: the enumeration's purpose is "every side of every if an accepted access takes". The ifs in the CONTROL arm test DOG, RESET, leftover. 0x100 is not an if-side. A latch keyed on 0x100 is a latch keyed on a predicate that is NOT one of the function's branch sides — is it in the contract's clause (e) floor? Clause (e) is "the single-axis generating set being the floor". The generating set here = the if-sides. `idata & 0x100` is a single axis, but not a member of the generating set... The generating-set doctrine says: the floor catches mutants keyed on single axes of the enumerated set; joints of them are debt. A key on 0x100 is a single axis OUTSIDE the enumerated set — the enumeration simply doesn't span the value space it claims ("CONTROL's writes at ... {0x0201, 0x0001, 0, 0x0200} (DOG x leftover, all four)"). The brief's own Q1 asks "is ACCEPTS exactly the function's accepted-access kinds" — an accepted CONTROL write carrying 0x100 is a kind the function takes (and treats distinctly from 0x0201 in one respect: the ENDIANESS clear is a no-op for the others... well, behaviorally the arm treats 0x100-carrying writes the same as their cleared twins — but the KIND space for mutation keying includes value bits). The fourth closing pass's own standard was: "a pre-set on ... CONTROL's 0x0200 cell each scored 131/131" — i.e., a value-cell keying is exactly the class the cross was built to close, and 0x0200 was ADDED to the list for it. The 0x100 cell is the same class and is missing. Also c1_deepseek targeted exactly "an accepted CONTROL write of exactly 0x0200" — value-keyed latches at this arm are an acknowledged live class (it survived only because dead code). So YES — this is a real, undisclosed, same-class-as-acknowledged gap.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:887:  9. What about 8W at 0x00 with a nonzero value? ACCEPTS has sd 0x00 with val 0 — the prelude rewrites REV anyway; a latch keyed on the VALUE of an accepted write at REV — value-keyed at non-CONTROL arms: e.g., pre-set keyed on `idata == 0x0123456789abcdef` at DOG? Value-keyed latches outside CONTROL: is that a class the enumeration addresses? Hmm! The cross rows kinds (op × offset), but the VALUE axis is only enumerated at CONTROL (because the CONTROL arm branches on idata). Other arms don't branch on the value. A pre-set keyed on `relative_addr == CRIME_DOG && idata == 0x1122334455667788`... that's keying on a specific value — a single value-cell among 2^64 — the generating-set doctrine can't row every value; the class is "keyed on one value at one label" ≈ joint/unnamed-cell — I'd put it in the same clause-(e) debt family as "one unnamed offset". But CONTROL is different: the brief ITSELF elevated CONTROL's value space into the floor (four named cells), because the arm branches on idata bits. The bits it branches on: DOG (0x200), RESET (0x800), leftover (post-mask != 0). The 0x100 bit is masked-not-branched-on — the defense would say: "a latch keyed on a bit the arm does not branch on is the unnamed-cell class, clause (e) debt." Hmm. That's actually a defensible counter! Let me weigh it carefully.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_064016\kimi.txt:991:  Hmm, one more consideration on whether 0x100-keying is really "single-axis" vs the generating set: the contract's CONTROL enumeration is (writeflag × DOG × leftover). `idata & 0x100` is NOT expressible as a joint of those axes (leftover is post-mask; 0x100 is masked away before leftover is tested) — it's an axis outside the generating set, like an unrowed address bit. The address-bit doctrine says "every bit 0-9 carried both ways by a refused access of each kind that can carry it" — the suite closes address bits by carrying them. For the CONTROL write value, the bits the suite chose to carry: 0, 9, 11 (0x0800 via sw08 — but that session has no refusals), and 0-7 via 0xff/0xffff REFUSED stores (wrong side — refused never reaches the arm). Bits 8, 10, 12-63 of an ACCEPTED CONTROL write: never carried anywhere with a refusal in the session. So the gap is: value bits of accepted CONTROL writes other than {0, 9} are not carried both ways (or at all) in any latch-counting session. The minimal witness: bit 8 (ENDIANESS) — the arm's own named bit.
