.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_012622\ollama_glm.txt:130:A pre-set keyed on a JOINT predicate is disclosed (clause (e) debt: "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate ... single-axis generating set being the floor"). So the pre-set enumeration only needs to cover single axes? Hmm — but wait. The disclosed item says: "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor."
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_012622\ollama_glm.txt:145:What about a pre-set keyed on (DOG set AND leftover zero)? That would be a joint predicate on accept-path attributes. Hmm — the single-axis generating set for pre-set: the axes are the branch tests. A pre-set inside the DOG_ENABLE true branch is keyed on one branch (DOG test). A pre-set keyed on "leftover zero" — mutant `pre_ctl0` (TXT + else d->warned |= ...) — keyed on leftover-zero branch. Covered by sw 0. ✓. A pre-set keyed on DOG-set×leftover-zero would need to be inside both branches — i.e., in the code after the DOG clear but... no, the leftover test happens after; a pre-set placed at the point where both conditions hold would be an edit like `if (was_dog && leftover==0)` — a NEW compound condition, which is a joint predicate, disclosed under "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate ... clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor". OK so I think DOG-set×leftover-zero is fine under the disclosure, since the single-axis members (DOG set via 0x0201; leftover zero via sw 0) are covered. Wait, but careful: the pre-set axis coverage requires that each single-axis key be exercised. DOG set: sw 0x0201 hits the DOG true branch. Leftover zero: sw 0 hits the leftover-zero branch (implicit else — the `if (idata != 0) fatal` not taken). So any single-branch pre-set is hit. A joint pre-set (DOG set AND leftover zero) is disclosed. OK.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_012622\ollama_glm.txt:756:A re-arm placed on a specific side is caught if some KIND hits that side. The "DOG set AND leftover zero" combination is not an elementary side — it's a path. An edit keyed on that joint condition (`if (idata == 0x0200) d->warned = 0;`) would be a joint-predicate re-arm, which is disclosed? The disclosure says: "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." So joint predicates are disclosed clause-(e) debt. The single-axis enumeration is: both sides of every if. The four elementary tests in CONTROL: writeflag, DOG, HARD_RESET (disclosed), leftover. Both sides of writeflag: R (lw) and W (sw). Both sides of DOG: set (0x0201) and clear (0x0001, 0). Both sides of leftover: nonzero (0x0201, 0x0001) and zero (0). So the single-axis generating set IS covered. The missing combo (DOG set + leftover zero) is a joint predicate (DOG-true AND leftover-false) — disclosed.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_012622\kimi.txt:274:  Hmm, wait — but is this within the disclosed "joint predicate" debt? "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." — That's item 4's disclosure, about the LATCH (set/test keyed on refusal axes). A re-arm is item 8. Item 8's disclosure: only "a re-arm or pre-set inside the HARD_RESET branch" is disclosed. So a re-arm keyed on (write × {INTSTAT,INTMASK}) is NOT disclosed. And it's not caught. Also simpler: re-arm keyed on write@REV (+0x00) — accepted write at +0x00 never happens at all (even pre-refusal) → also survives; and pre-set keyed on write@+0x00: never fires anywhere → survives trivially. Hmm — pre-set that never fires is... it never changes any behavior in-session, but for an arbitrary guest it changes behavior (guest writes at +0x00 accepted — wait, prelude overwrites REV each access; writes at +0x00 are accepted and do reach case REV). A guest doing sw +0x00 then its first narrow access gets NO announce — violates "first refused access announces". Guest-reachable, unrowed, undisclosed (the +0x00-write disclosure is about round trips/accept-shrink, not about latch effects keyed there).
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_012622\kimi.txt:306:  - "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." — latch = the warned bit set/test. A re-arm `d->warned = 0` IS a latch-affecting edit keyed on a joint predicate (writeflag × label). Hmm — "keyed on" — the example given is refusal-keyed. The phrase "single-axis generating set being the floor" suggests joint predicates are debt. If the panel judges this disclosure to cover accept-side joint predicates too, my candidate is "disclosed". But the example and context are refusal-side, and item 8 separately claims closure "over every place an accepted access can go" — derived from branches. The accept-side enumeration (KINDS) is presented as COMPLETE ("each accepted kind"), and rearm once is the row. The derivation's own completeness claim ("both sides of every `if` an accepted access can take") — an accepted write at REV takes: entry-write side, width write side, case REV, hack in/out. All those SIDES are rowed. The mutant adds a NEW if (writeflag) inside REV — not a side of an existing if. So by the derivation's literal standard, it owes nothing. By the floor's actual behavior, it survives. This is a legit adversarial finding: the derivation is complete for branches that EXIST, but the accept-side floor has unrowed direction×label conjunctions at REV/INTSTAT/INTMASK where an additive mutant hides. Whether the panel calls it GAP or debt is the verdict question.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260914_012622\kimi.txt:308:  Given the brief's verdict protocol: "CLOSES (no floor gap remains in the 131-row test)" vs "GAP: <exact source edit>". A floor gap = an edit that undoes fix behaviour and passes 131/131 and isn't disclosed. My edit A qualifies unless the joint-predicate disclosure is read broadly. The disclosure for joints says "clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor" — it's explicitly scoped as the latch disclosure under item 4. Item 8 (re-arm) has no such disclosure. So I'll call it a GAP, and name the row that should catch it (rearm once) and why it can't (KINDS has reads only at +0x00/+0x10/+0x18; no accepted write at those labels ever occurs between refusals — or at +0x00, anywhere).
