.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\fable_pass1_20260913.md:61:Clauses (a)-(c) are carried by the committed witness: 3/15 pre-fix (`witness_committed.out`), 15/15 on the fix, kills a verbatim `exit(1)` reinstatement, and a comment-only file still carries the kill. The (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration over the edited else arm: dominating predicates `len == 8`, `len == 4` -> operand `len`, reachable partition **{1, 2}** (MIPS GPR widths are 1/2/4/8; `lwl/lwr/ldl/ldr` issue the aligned full width; `0x280` is 8-aligned so no end-of-device clamp -- Opus/codex READING, I concur); the reached body (tail) tests `writeflag` -> partition **{R, W}**. Floor cells: **{1,2} x {R,W} = 4**, plus the repair's own effect.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_203439\ollama_glm.txt:558:**Issue B: item 4's "each label first" enumeration.** Labels: rev once, sh08b once (CONTROL), istat once, imask once, dog once, time once, latch x5 (default-arm, low), hi once (far block), mid once. Is every switch label a "first refusal" somewhere? Labels: REV (rev once), CONTROL (sh08b once), INTSTAT (istat once), INTMASK (imask once), DOG (dog once), TIME (time once), default arm (latch x5 at low default offsets, hi, mid). Complete for width/direction axes: warm once (sb, write, odd), latch x5 (lh, read, even). Widths 1 and 2, R and W: sb (1,W), sh? Hmm — the direction/width enumeration: "width 1 or 2 and direction R or W — warm once (sb, write, odd), latch x5 (lh, read, even)". Is there a session whose first refusal is sh (2-byte write)? And lb (1-byte read)? latch x5's first refusal is lh at +0x06 (read, 2-byte, even). warm once's first refusal is sb (write, 1-byte, odd). What about a first refusal that is lb (1-byte, read) or sh (2-byte, write)? For a latch keyed on a single axis — e.g. keyed on (len==1 && writeflag==MEM_READ) — the claim is the single-axis generating set covers width×direction. Hmm, a latch keyed on "len != 1 || writeflag == MEM_WRITE"? I.e., sets the bit only for 1-byte writes. First refusals: warm once's first refusal is sb (1-byte write) → sets. latch x5's first is lh (2-byte read) → doesn't set → announce printed again → "latch x5" count row red? Wait, latch x5 counts notices across 5 refusals expecting 1. If the latch only sets on 1-byte writes, then the lh first refusal doesn't set... but wait, the latch x5 session's refusals are all lh at +0x06? "latch x5 (lh, read, even)" — first refusal lh; subsequent refusals at other places? "latch x5 (default-arm, low)" — probably 5 refusals all in the default-arm low block. If the latch is keyed on len==1 (only 1-byte accesses set the bit), then the FIRST lh refusal doesn't set the bit → notice printed at first refusal; subsequent refusals also don't set... so every refusal prints → count = 5 ≠ 1 → red. OK so a len-keyed latch is caught by latch x5 as long as its refusals are 2-byte. And a writeflag-keyed latch (only writes set): warm once's first refusal is sb (write) → sets; then? warm once: "refused, accepted, refused: one notice" — the warm session: refusal, accepted access, refusal. If the second refusal is also a write... hmm. Actually "warm once (sb, write, odd)" — the session's refusals. A latch keyed only on writes: first refusal sb (write) sets; later refusals — if they include a read refusal, that read refusal prints again → count 2 → red. Does the warm session include a read refusal? Not stated. Hmm.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_203439\ollama_glm.txt:885:Disclosed items: "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." — INTSTAT is a NAMED label. The pre-set at the INTSTAT case label: keyed on a single named label. Not joint, not unnamed. "a re-arm or pre-set inside the HARD_RESET branch" — no. So not disclosed. And the header's item 4 pre-set enumeration claims the class closed. If no session reaches INTSTAT-first, the claim is false at label granularity. BUT the header's claim is at ARM granularity ("in EVERY switch arm an accepted access reaches first in some counted session... in the INTMASK arm, the IRQ sessions' setup"). Hmm — is the header's claim FALSE, or is the INTSTAT-label pre-set outside the claimed class? The class as claimed: pre-sets in arms reached first. The INTSTAT/INTMASK arm IS reached first. A pre-set textually inside that arm — even attached to one label — is "in the arm". So the header claims it caught. If it isn't → the enumeration claims a cell no row can see → Q1 answer.
