.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_164716\ollama_glm.txt:439:(b) The lb/lh rev pc hunk: adds `more = ld(A, "lb", CRIME + 0x07, "v1")[0] if op == "lb" else []` — a second refusal after the REV refusal, and `["print v0"]`. Rows: rev pc (pc == PROBE_PC+4 — the refused access must NOT set pc=ra), rev v0 (answer 0), and for lb: "rev once" (first refusal at REV then +0x07: one notice). Wait — for op == "lh", `more = []` — no second refusal. So "lh rev once" doesn't exist — but "latch x5" (lh, read, even) covers... the header says "each label first — rev once, sh08b once (CONTROL), istat once, imask once, dog once, time once, latch x5 (default-arm, low), hi once". "rev once" is lb only. Is a latch keyed on "first refusal is a 2-byte read at REV" (i.e., doesn't set for lh-at-REV-first) caught? The lh rev pc session has ONE refusal (lh at +0x00) — no count row for it... The "said" rows: "lh said" — the lh session presumably counts its full text once. Hmm — the "said" rows exist per width (lb/lh/sb/sh said) from the 85-row test. If "lh said" is a single-refusal session counting the notice, a latch keyed on `len==2 && rel==REV && read` (declines but doesn't LATCH) would... announce at the lh session (count 1 ✓)... then a SECOND refusal in the same session would announce again — but if the lh said session has only one refusal, no double. Hmm — the keyed-latch class: the latch doesn't SET for lh-at-REV; the symptom is a SECOND notice at a later refusal. To show it, a session needs first refusal = lh at REV, then another refusal. The lh rev pc session: one refusal only. Is there any session whose FIRST refusal is a 2-byte read at REV followed by another? "latch x5" is lh, read, even — at +0x06 (even, default arm), not REV. Hmm — so a latch keyed on (width=2, direction=R, label=REV) jointly — JOINT predicate → disclosed. And single-axis: keyed on label REV → "rev once" (lb) catches (first refusal lb at REV, then +0x07 → if the latch doesn't set for... wait, keyed on label REV means the latch sets only when rel==REV; the lb-rev-then-+0x07 session: first refusal at REV → latch SETS (key satisfied) → +0x07 refusal → no second notice → count 1 ✓ GREEN?! Hold on — the keyed-latch detection logic: "A keyed latch shows only when the first refusal in a session falls where it does not set" — i.e., the session's first refusal is OUTSIDE the key → latch never set → EVERY refusal announces → count > 1 → RED. So to catch a latch keyed on label==REV, you need a session whose first refusal is NOT at REV, followed by more refusals → count would be >1. Hmm, so "rev once" (first AT REV, then +0x07) catches a latch keyed on... a latch that does NOT set at REV but sets at +0x07: e.g., keyed on `relative_addr != CRIME_REV` or keyed on default-arm offsets. And it also catches: latch keyed on "odd address" (first refusal at +0x00 even? +0x00 is even... hmm "rev once": lb at +0x00 (even), then lb at +0x07 (odd) — a parity-keyed latch (sets only on odd) → first refusal even → no set → +0x07 announces (2nd... wait the count would be 2 → RED ✓ — that's lt_par, and POSTTABLE says lt_par's red rows: warm/rearm/hi once 2 each — hmm, "rev once" not listed in lt_par's reds? "lt_par warm/rearm/hi once 2 each" — so rev once stayed green for lt_par? If lt_par is keyed on odd addresses SETTING the latch: first refusal at +0x00 (even) → no set → announce (1); +0x07 (odd) → sets, no announce → count 1 → GREEN. And warm once (sb at odd?) → 2 → RED. OK consistent.) Fine — the once-rows each pin one axis-value-as-first-refusal; the generating set is claimed closed. The header's enumeration of "each label first" — REV (lb), CONTROL (sh08b), INTSTAT, INTMASK, DOG, TIME, default-low (latch x5), high block (hi once). Each followed by another refusal with count 1. The JOINT keyings are disclosed. OK.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_164716\ollama_glm.txt:500:Now, is there any single-axis latch the rows would miss? The claim in the header: "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate ... clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." So joint predicates are disclosed.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_164716\ollama_glm.txt:547:So: is there a single-cell re-arm edit that escapes? A re-arm is a behavior-undoing edit: e.g., adding `d->warned = 0;` at the top of some arm. For the test to catch it, some session must have: refusal (announce), accepted access through THAT arm, another refusal — with the accepted access matching the re-arm's keying. The rearm session covers: sd/ld/sw/lw at +0x30, +0x34, +0x38(ld), +0x18(lw), +0x10(lw), +0x00(lw), +0x08(sw), +0x04(lw), +0x278(sd/ld). A re-arm keyed on e.g. `relative_addr == CRIME_TIME && len == 8 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE` — joint, disclosed-ish. A re-arm keyed on a SINGLE axis: e.g., in the TIME arm unconditionally `d->warned = 0;` — the rearm session's ld at +0x38 passes through the TIME arm (crime_update_crime_time is called for the accepted ld... wait, does an accepted 8-byte access at +0x38 go through the switch? Yes — the width dispatch handles data, then the switch runs for ALL accepted accesses. So an accepted ld +0x38 hits CRIME_TIME arm). So a re-arm in the TIME arm (unconditional) is caught by rearm once (the ld +0x38 → re-arm → subsequent lb +0x06 refusal announces again → count 2 → red). Good. A re-arm in the INTMASK arm: caught by lw +0x18. A re-arm in the default arm: caught by lw +0x04 and sd/ld +0x278. A re-arm in the 4-byte lane (unconditional): caught by any 4-byte accepted access in the session. A re-arm in the 8-byte lane: caught by sd/ld +0x30. A re-arm in the tail: caught by latch x5 (all refusals pass through the tail — if the tail re-arms, every refusal announces → latch x5 red). A re-arm in the prelude (before the width dispatch): every access re-arms → warm once red (refused, accepted, refused → count 2). So single-axis re-arms are covered. Joint re-arms (e.g., only 8W at TIME) — disclosed as joint-predicate class? The disclosure bullet says "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." That's about the latch; a re-ARM keyed jointly is arguably the same clause (e) class. Hmm, but strictly the disclosure speaks of "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate" — the re-arm is the inverse latch operation. I'd say the generating-set argument covers it symmetrically: single-axis re-arms are covered; joint re-arms are clause (e). OK.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_164716\ollama_glm.txt:693:OK so the latch-axis coverage is a genuine generating set for single axes. Joint predicates disclosed. Fine.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_164716\ollama_glm.txt:707:Hmm wait — but actually, is a pre-set in the INTSTAT/INTMASK arm really "undoing part of the fix's behaviour"? Yes: it would silence the notice in sessions where an accepted INTMASK/INTSTAT access precedes the first narrow access. That's a real behavior change (the notice is part of the fix). Is it a SINGLE-CELL change? Adding `d->warned |= CRIME_W_LEN;` inside the INTSTAT/INTMASK case is a single-line insertion — but is an INSERTION a "single-cell edit"? The standard: "any single-label or single-cell change". The mutants in the tables include things like `d->warned = 0;` in the 4-byte arm (codex's finding — an insertion). And "pre4" (`d->warned |= CRIME_W_LEN;' in an accept arm) — the Opus closing seat's mutant. So insertions count. The test's claimed coverage: "the announce's ... pre-set (4-byte access, a second latch on bit 0)" — the denominator statement says pre-set by 4-byte access and second latch on bit 0 — hmm, it does NOT claim pre-set coverage for every switch arm! Let me re-read the denominator: "the announce's text, channel, presence, pre-set (4-byte access, a second latch on bit 0) and re-arm (every accepted kind, the TIME arm)". So pre-set coverage claimed only for: 4-byte access, second latch on bit 0 — and "by an 8-byte one, warm once (sd first) and time once (ld first)" per the CONTRACT item 4. So the contract claims 8-byte pre-set via sd (DOG — 8W) and ld (TIME — 8R). It does NOT claim pre-set at INTMASK/INTSTAT arms. And the re-arm claims "every accepted kind ... and an accepted access into each switch arm". So the pre-set at INTMASK/INTSTAT arms — is it disclosed? The DISCLOSED list: "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at". A pre-set in the INTMASK arm is not a latch "keyed on" a predicate in the sense of the set-expression — hmm, actually a pre-set in a switch arm IS expressible as a latch keyed on a joint predicate? No: `if (!(d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN)) { d->warned |= CRIME_W_LEN; fatal(...); } goto crime_declined;` plus, in the INTMASK arm, `d->warned |= CRIME_W_LEN;` — that's an ADDITIONAL set. The overall latch state now sets on: any refusal, OR any accepted INTMASK/INTSTAT access. As a predicate: "keyed on" = the set condition is (refusal) OR (access at INTMASK/INTSTAT). That's a disjunctive/joint predicate. Hmm. Is it caught by the single-axis generating set? The latch's set-predicate now includes "relative_addr ∈ {INTMASK, INTSTAT} AND len ∈ {4,8} accepted" — for the latch to be pre-set before a session's first refusal, the session must do an accepted 4/8-byte access at INTMASK/INTSTAT first. Sessions that do: imask/istat sessions (the ctl lw comes after the refusals — so their FIRST refusal precedes it → the notice prints → once rows count 1 → green), the gate session (no count row), rearm (latch already set). Hmm — so is there any row that would go red? The once-rows all stay green: rev once (session has no INTMASK/INTSTAT accepted access before... the rev session: ra=..., lb at +0x00, then lb +0x07 — no accepted CRIME accesses at all before the refusals), sh08b once (no accepted accesses), istat once/imask once (setup sd at +0x20 — SOFTINT, default arm; the lw at +off comes AFTER the four refusals — so first refusal announces → count 1 → green; hmm wait, actually let me double check the imask session order: from the diff context, the visible lines: `st(A, "sd", CRIME + 0x20, BIT)`, print cause, `ld(A, "lb", CRIME + off, "a0")`, print, `st(A, "sb", CRIME + off, 0xff)`, print, `ld(A, "lh", ...)`, print, `st(A, "sh", ...)`, print, `ld(A, "lw", CRIME + off, "v0")`, print + prints. So the first refusal is the lb at +off — before it, only the sd at +0x20 (default arm). So an INTMASK-arm pre-set doesn't silence the imask session's first refusal → imask once green.), dog once (lb at +0x30 first — no accepted access before → green), time once (first ld at +0x38 — TIME arm, not INTMASK → the pre-set at INTMASK doesn't trigger → first refusal announces → count 1 → green), warm once (sd at +0x30 — DOG arm → green), latch x5 (prologue: sw +0x08 CONTROL, sw/lw +0x34 default → green), hi once (no accepted before first refusal → green), rearm once (first refusal first → announce → then the accepted lw +0x18/+0x10 are no-ops → count 1 → green), free said (session: free-running lb +7 — is there an accepted access before? "free said" checks the announce text in a free-running session; a pre-set at INTMASK — the free session probably has no accepted INTMASK access → green), top said (session: ld +0x210, lw +0x214, lw +0x210 — all accepted (REV? no wait +0x210 is far block — default arm; +0x214 default; +0x210 default) → the first refusal lb +0x27b announces → green — wait, top said checks "said(t, 1, 0x27b, clean) == 1" — the first notice is at 0x27b. An INTMASK pre-set doesn't affect this session (no INTMASK accesses) → green).
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_164716\ollama_glm.txt:759:OK so the candidate GAP: `d->warned |= CRIME_W_LEN;` inserted in the CRIME_INTSTAT/CRIME_INTMASK arm (or the CRIME_REV arm). But wait — I should double-check: does this edit "undo part of the fix's behaviour"? The fix's notice would be silenced in real guest scenarios: e.g., NetBSD enabling the interrupt mask (an accepted 4/8-byte write to INTMASK) before any narrow access → the narrow-access notice never prints. Yes — that undoes the "announced once per device instance" behavior for realistic boots. Is it a "single-cell" edit? It's an inserted line — comparable to codex's `d->warned = 0;` in the 4-byte arm (which the closing pass treated as a gap and the repair rowed). The repair rowed re-arm "closed over every place an accepted access can go" — the re-arm axis was closed over every place — but the PRE-SET was NOT closed the same way. The denominator statement says pre-set only "(4-byte access, a second latch on bit 0)". So the standard the brief sets for itself: "the notice's text, channel and latch (the latch keyed on any single axis: width, direction, label, block)" — hmm, the STANDARD paragraph (SETTLED section): "the notice's text, channel and latch (the latch keyed on any single axis: width, direction, label, block), the answer at every named label, state carried across calls, and the decline holding in the device states the tail reads". Pre-set per-arm is not in the standard's enumeration! The standard says the LATCH keyed on any single axis — the pre-set is a different defect class (the latch pre-set by accepted accesses). Hmm. The standard also says "state carried across calls" — the pre-set is arguably "state" behavior... The contract item 4 (the test's own contract) claims pre-set coverage for 4-byte accesses, 8-byte accesses, and the bit-0 collision — not per-arm. So by the test's own contract, an INTMASK-arm pre-set is... not claimed. Is it disclosed? The DISCLOSED list doesn't mention arm-specific pre-sets. Hmm. The question is whether the panel's floor ("does any single-cell edit in the fix's own enumeration score 111/111?") — "the fix's own enumeration" — the enumeration of what? "the FLOOR question (does any single-cell edit in the fix's own enumeration score 111/111?) is asked of the whole test". Hmm, "the fix's own enumeration" — maybe the enumeration of behaviors in the standard paragraph. The standard paragraph: "...the notice's text, channel and latch (the latch keyed on any single axis: width, direction, label, block)..." — a pre-set in the INTMASK arm is the latch keyed on a single axis? The set-predicate "relative_addr == INTMASK" is a single axis (label)! Hmm — is the INTMASK-arm pre-set equivalent to "the latch keyed on label INTMASK"? No — a label-keyed latch (in the decline arm) sets only on REFUSALS at INTMASK; an arm pre-set sets on ACCEPTED accesses at INTMASK. Different trigger (accepted vs refused). The latch-axis generating set covers refusals-keyed predicates. The pre-set class is "the latch set by accepted accesses" — the contract covers 4-byte lane, 8-byte lane, and via those, the arms the rowed sessions' accepted accesses pass through: CONTROL (sw 0x01), default (+0x34), DOG (sd +0x30), TIME (ld +0x38). Missing: INTSTAT, INTMASK, REV arms.
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_164716\ollama_glm.txt:785:So the mutant scores 111/111. Now: is it disclosed? Scanning the DISCLOSED list: (1) return 1 equivalence; (2) DOG/CONTROL-read fall-throughs; (3) interior accept slots; (4) effect keyed on one unnamed offset (incl. time_land); (5) accepted writes at +0x00/+0x08 shrunk; (6) joint-predicate latch or unnamed-offset latch; (7) SOFTINT fall-through; (8) function-static latch; (9) widths 3/5/6/7; (10) RAM-size checks; (11) independence between instances. None covers "a pre-set in the INTSTAT/INTMASK (or REV) arm". The latch-axis disclosure says "a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate (e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME), or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor." — Could the panel argue the INTMASK pre-set is "a latch keyed on a single axis (label)" and hence covered by the generating set? The generating set for the SET-predicate: the set now happens on (refusal at any width/direction/label) OR (accepted access at INTMASK). The first disjunct alone already sets on every refusal — the added disjunct only matters pre-refusal. The single-axis latch rows cover set-predicates RESTRICTED to refusal-triggered keying (the set expression inside the decline arm). An arm pre-set is a set OUTSIDE the decline arm — the latch rows' logic ("first refusal falls where it does not set") can't see it because the trigger isn't a refusal. So no, the generating-set claim doesn't cover it, and the disclosure doesn't name it. 
.\regress\artifacts\crimewidthkill\panel_20260913_164716\ollama_glm.txt:801:So the uncovered pre-set cells: the CRIME_REV arm, the CRIME_INTSTAT/CRIME_INTMASK arm. Both are single-cell insertions. Also — what about a pre-set keyed on accepted accesses at a single UNNAMED offset (default arm)? Covered by the prologue's +0x34 accesses (default arm) — well, a pre-set at a DIFFERENT unnamed offset (e.g., only +0x100): the rowed sessions' accepted accesses before first refusals: +0x08, +0x30, +0x34, +0x38, +0x20, +0x210/+0x214 — a pre-set only at +0x100 wouldn't trigger in any rowed session → 111/111 — but that's the "one unnamed offset" disclosed class ("a latch keyed on ... one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at: clause (e)"). Hmm — that disclosure covers latch keying on unnamed offsets; a pre-set at an unnamed offset is arguably the same class (the set keyed on one unnamed offset). The disclosure's parenthetical: "(e.g. only 2-byte refusals at CRIME_TIME)" — about refusals. And "or on one unnamed offset other than the default-arm cells the latch rows refuse at". A pre-set at unnamed offset +0x100: the set is keyed on one unnamed offset — I'd say that falls under the disclosed clause-(e) class (barely). But the INTMASK/INTSTAT/REV arms are NAMED labels — observable arms — and the panel's standard says "at every OBSERVABLE switch label". The pre-set at a NAMED, OBSERVABLE label is squarely the class the floor is supposed to cover ("the latch keyed on any single axis: width, direction, label, block" — label axis!). Hmm, wait — is the pre-set "the latch keyed on label"? The set-predicate includes label == INTSTAT (and accepted). As a single-axis... the trigger has TWO axes: label AND acceptedness (len ∈ {4,8} + the switch reach). Hmm — so is it a JOINT predicate (label × width)? The set fires on (relative_addr == INTSTAT) AND (len ∈ {4, 8}) — that's a joint (label × width) predicate! And joint predicates are disclosed ("a latch keyed on a JOINT predicate ... clause (e), the single-axis generating set being the floor"). Hmm!! That's the counter-argument: an arm pre-set is inherently joint (address × accepted-width), so it's clause (e) debt.
