.\regress\artifacts\landiskaux\panels_20260918\panel_20260918_080056\codex.txt:1872:GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30838:   "state_note": "Surfaced by the pcc2widthkills pass-1 panel (fable5 and kimi independently) while auditing the width fix: 'ten kills removed' would read as the file being safe while a one-byte guest write still exits.  The width detector's GENCTL lanes were chosen (0xd9/0x40, C040 clear) precisely to stay off this kill; c040ok() asserts it at authoring time.  Next round after pcc2widthkills closes.  Filed in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (dated tail, `### pcc2c040kill`, line 14553 as of 2026-09-04); this row added 2026-09-04 so the dashboard shows it -- the bug file had it, the ledger did not.  NEW FACT 2026-09-05 (Opus design seat on pcc2defaultkill, verified: dev_pcc2.c SCCRXIACK arm has no writeflag test): a guest LOAD of SCCRXIACK 0x27 runs reassert_interrupts(), and OpenBSD's console driver cl.c reads that offset on SCC receive interrupts -- so the ENTRY into the C040 kill is routine guest traffic; only the ARMING (C040 set in GENCTL) is rare.  Reachability better than the filing says.  Must be fixed before any round implements a PCC2 IACK arm.\n\n*** PASS-1 AUDIT 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).\nTHE CENTRAL PREMISE WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION MAKES THE DEFECT SIMPLER AND WORSE. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE for unsupported C040 delivery.  Survival is a nonvacuous\noracle here, but cannot establish interrupt correctness.\"*\n\n*** THE CORRECTION: *\"the GENCTL write itself immediately calls the terminating helper.  Arming and\ntermination occur within that access; subsequent SCC traffic is unnecessary.\"* ***\n\n**Line 459 IS `reassert_interrupts()` inside the GENCTL byte-write arm.**  So the whole\n`SCCRXIACK`-plus-OpenBSD-`cl.c` entry story -- which this row records as a NEW FACT improving\nreachability, and which the brief built its S4 around -- is UNNECESSARY.  **A single legal byte write\nof 0x04 to offset 0x02 terminates the emulator on the spot.**  No second access, no driver, no\ninterrupt.\n\n*That does not make the earlier finding wrong -- SCCRXIACK really does run the helper with no\ndirection test, and that really is routine traffic.  It makes it SUPERFLUOUS: the row had found a\nlong road to a place reachable in one step, and nobody noticed the short one because the long one was\ninteresting.*\n\n**CONFIRMED BY READING, beyond what the brief asserted:**\n  * `pcctwo_reg[]` holds **`uint8_t`**; GENCTL rejects non-byte writes, but the accepted byte write\n    **stores `idata` WITHOUT MASKING** and immediately calls the helper.  **No read-only treatment\n    prevents the bit being stored**, and the header explicitly permits byte accesses.\n  * The ten call sites are confirmed with their contexts -- 383 `pcctwo_interrupt_common()`, 400 the\n    device TICK (unconditionally), 459 GENCTL write, 551 T1ICR/T2ICR, 574 SCCICR, 588 SCCTX,\n    602 SCCRX, 609 SCCRXIACK (no direction test), 622 SCSIICR, 662 MASK -- all reaching **one shared\n    terminating branch**.\n  * **THE CONDITION IS NOT STICKY:** a GENCTL write CLEARING C040 stores the cleared value before\n    calling the helper.  So the state is recoverable in principle -- and unrecoverable in practice,\n    because the arming write has already terminated the process.\n  * **`400` is the device TICK, called unconditionally.**  So even a guest that arms C040 and then\n    does nothing at all is terminated at the next tick.  That is worth stating separately: it removes\n    the last version of the story in which the guest must go on to do something.\n\n**Q4 ANSWERED, and it is the opposite of the neighbouring rounds' problem:** *\"Survival is a\nnonvacuous oracle here.\"*  This project has measured that survival rows at REFUSAL-EFFECT sites are\nvacuous by construction -- guarded and unguarded code both survive.  Here the pre-fix build genuinely\ndoes NOT survive, so survival discriminates.  **But it cannot establish interrupt correctness**, which\nis the second half of the disposition question and needs its own row.\n\n**THE WITNESS IS NOW TRIVIALLY CHEAP.**  This row's `witness` field says a rung-3 attempt *\"looks\ncheap (one more session in `regress/pcc2_width_probe.py`, which already byte-writes this register on\nthis machine)\"* -- and with arming-equals-termination it is cheaper still: **one byte write and\nobserve that the process is gone.**  The field's promise that it says `not yet done` rather than\n`did not think to ask` is now cashable in a single session.\n\n*And a note on the brief: it was written with C5 applied FROM THE START -- neutral register, scoped\nreading, no chain -- on a subject (process termination) that is trigger 1's own vocabulary.  **It was\nnot refused.**  First time today I wrote one that way instead of learning it from a refusal.*\n\n**READY TO CLAIM AS A ROUND, 2026-09-07.**  The stated blocker was *\"next round after\npcc2widthkills closes\"*; that row now carries `commits=b78bb0f`, so the width fix is IN THE TREE and\nonly its formal closure is outstanding.  Nothing about this defect depends on that closure.\n\n**THE WITNESS IS ONE SESSION IN AN EXISTING PROBE.**  `regress/pcc2_width_probe.py` already drives\nGENCTL byte writes on this machine and carries an authoring-time assertion, `c040ok()`, whose whole\njob is to keep its lanes (`0xd9`/`0x40`) OFF this path.  **So the probe already proves the access\nworks and already knows the bit is dangerous** -- the witness is the same access with the bit set,\nand the oracle is that the process is gone.\n\n  *That assertion is worth keeping rather than deleting when the row is added: it protects the WIDTH\n  rows from wandering onto this path, which is a different job from the new row deliberately\n  stepping onto it.  A row that turns the guard off for the whole file would re-open exactly what\n  `c040ok()` was written to prevent.*\n\n**FIX SHAPE, from the audit:** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, with its own latch bit -- the same\nper-instance reasoning as `dev_sgi_re`'s bits, since a shared latch is silenced by the other site's\nordinary traffic.  **The open design question is Q2, which the audit did not settle from the source:**\nafter declining, does the helper fall through to the normal level recalculation, or return having\nasserted nothing?  Those differ in what the guest observes, and the answer decides whether the round\nowes a second row on interrupt behaviour -- which the verdict's own second clause anticipates\n(*\"survival ... cannot establish interrupt correctness\"*).\n\n*** WITNESS DESIGN REVIEWED AND NOT RATIFIED.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/grok_witness_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"NOT RATIFY as proposed.  A guest `st.b` of 0x04 at GENCTL is a sound CORE of a rung-3\nwitness of this kill.  Survival-only, in `pcc2_width_probe.py`, with no same-decode preset and no\nsite-attribution, is not.  **That is the same class as the design you asked me to assume: the\nobservation is either destroyed by the defect, or it reports success while measuring nothing.**\"*\n\n**THE CORE SURVIVES: one legal byte write is the right reproduction.**  What fails is my ORACLE, and\nthe required attribution is specific:\n\n  1. **`how == \"exit1\"`, not merely `not alive`.**  `not alive` is satisfied by several unrelated\n     deaths.\n  2. **The transcript must contain `C040 interrupt assertions`.**  Missing text plus exit1 is\n     *FAILED FOR SOME OTHER REASON*, not a pass -- which is the carrier's own rule 4 (*every\n     reproduction must answer \"did it fail for the reason under test?\"*), applied to a row I had\n     written without it.\n  3. **Controls in sessions that MUST SURVIVE**, so a construction failure cannot masquerade as the\n     defect: a RAM round-trip; CHIPID `ld.bu` -> 0x20 (RAM would not answer that); and a\n     **C040-CLEAR GENCTL round-trip of a nonzero value** -- 0x82, already proven by existing rows in\n     `pcc2_width_probe.py` and `pcc2defaultkill`.  *That third one is the same-decode preset: it\n     proves the register is reachable and writable BEFORE the killing value is used.*\n  4. *** THEY MUST ALL RUN BEFORE THE KILLING STORE.  Post-kill they cannot run. ***\n\n**POINT 4 IS THE ONE I WOULD NOT HAVE FOUND, and it is the Alpha design's flaw wearing different\nclothes.**  There the defect destroyed the TRANSLATION the observation needed; here it destroys the\nPROCESS the controls need.  In both cases the observation and the thing observed cannot coexist, and\nin both cases I designed the observation first and never asked what state it required afterwards.\n\n*** THREE WITNESS DESIGNS OF MINE HAVE NOW BEEN REVIEWED AND ALL THREE HAD A FLAW OF THE SAME CLASS.\n***  Alpha swpctx (defect destroys the translation), `#505`'s tiled row (two silent declines read as\nplausible values), and this one (survival is not attribution).  **The common shape is that I design\nwhat to OBSERVE and not how to ATTRIBUTE it** -- the ladder's clause (ii) asks for a liveness control\nAND a signature control, and in each case I supplied the first and reasoned my way past the second.\nRecorded here rather than as a carrier rule because three instances is a pattern about ME, and the\ncheap remedy is already available: **route every witness design to a reviewer before building it.**\nThat has now cost one invocation each time and caught three real defects.\n\n**ALSO FLAGGED, and it needs checking before the round:** the seat calls S3's tick claim *possibly\nsequencing-wrong* -- i.e. whether call site 400 (the unconditional device tick) really does reach the\nkill for a guest that arms C040 and then does nothing.  I asserted that from the call-site list; it\nis not verified.\n\n*** THE TICK CLAIM IS VACUOUS, AND SO IS 'TEN CALL SITES' AS A STATEMENT OF HAZARD.  My record,\ncorrected by checking the flag grok raised. ***\n\ngrok called S3's tick claim *\"possibly sequencing-wrong\"*.  Checked, and it is wrong:\n\n  * `DEVICE_TICK(pcc2)` really does call `reassert_interrupts(d)` unconditionally -- that part of my\n    claim is literally true;\n  * **but GENCTL is written in exactly ONE place** -- its own case arm -- and that arm calls\n    `reassert_interrupts()` in the SAME ACCESS.  Every other reference to `pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_GENCTL]`\n    in the file is a READ (`:271` the C040 test, `:341` the MIEN test).  A wide write is DECLINED\n    BEFORE the store.\n\n**So the process always terminates on the ARMING WRITE ITSELF, and the tick can never be the first\nhit.**  *\"Even a guest that arms C040 and then does nothing is terminated at the next tick\"* describes\na state that cannot be reached: there is no interval during which C040 is set and the process is\nalive.\n\n*** AND THE SHARPER CORRECTION: 'TEN CALL SITES' IS TRUE AND MISLEADING. ***  All ten reach one\nshared branch, which is what the audit established and which stands.  But **nine of them are\nunreachable AS A FIRST HIT**, because the only way the guard's condition becomes true is a write that\nimmediately calls the helper itself.  **The defect has exactly ONE reachable entry.**\n\n**WHAT THIS CHANGES:**\n  * the WITNESS is simpler than the design assumed -- one session, one store; **no tick row is owed**,\n    and a row waiting for a tick would wait forever;\n  * the SEVERITY framing is unchanged (one legal byte still ends the emulator) but the *breadth*\n    framing was inflated: ten sites reads as ten ways in, and there is one;\n  * the FIX is unaffected -- de-fatalising the shared branch covers all ten regardless of which can\n    reach it first, and that is the right place to repair it.\n\n*This is the third over-broad claim of mine this session and the same shape as the others: a census I\nran correctly, restated as a claim about REACHABILITY that the census does not support.  The count of\ncall sites answers \"how many places call this helper\"; it does not answer \"how many can arrive here\nfirst\", and I wrote the second while measuring the first.*\n\n*** FULL PANEL 2026-09-07 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_124918`).  FOUR SEATS REJECTED MY S3\nCORRECTION, AND THEY WERE RIGHT ABOUT THE WARRANT EVEN THOUGH THE CONCLUSION SURVIVED. ***\n\n    codex      PENDING CODE REVIEW      grok      (mid-answer, 422 b)\n    minimax    REJECT                   glm       answered 8.3 kB -- NO BURN this time\n    kimi-sub   answered                 deepseek  answered\n    kimi CLI   403-walled, covered      agy       deliberately not fired\n\n**ZERO SEATS DOWN -- the first such panel of the day.**  glm did NOT burn, on a brief far more focused\nthan pass 3's, which matches the carrier's finding that burns track BRIEF COMPLEXITY rather than the\nseat.\n\n*** THE ATTACK, and glm put it most precisely: *** *\"a count of references to the symbolic constant\nCANNOT ENUMERATE STORES REACHING BYTE 2 -- and any wide/bulk store, nonzero reset default, or\nunzeroed allocation falsifies S3 and revives S4's tick row.\"*  kimi-sub: *\"asserted rather than\nenumerated ... the packet never lists all stores into `pcctwo_reg[]`.\"*  deepseek: *\"S3's one-entry\nclaim is unsupported.\"*\n\n**THEY ARE RIGHT ABOUT THE EVIDENCE.**  My grep was for `PCCTWO_GENCTL` -- the symbolic constant --\nwhich is a CORRELATE of \"stores that set C040\", not the class.  That is exactly the deriver failure\nthe carrier documents: *before writing a deriver, ask what makes a member a member.*\n\n*** THE PROPER ENUMERATION, run by class rather than by name, and S3 SURVIVES: ***\n  * **every write into `pcctwo_reg[]` in any index form** -- 40 sites; all use symbolic constants and\n    none names GENCTL except its own case arm at `:458`;\n  * **the one BULK write**, `memcpy(d->pcctwo_reg + relative_addr, data, len)` at `:482` -- the\n    falsifier glm predicted.  It sits in the `T1CMP/T2CMP/T1COUNT/T2COUNT` arm, so `relative_addr` is\n    one of **0x04, 0x08, 0x0c, 0x10** and the copy runs FORWARD.  **GENCTL is 0x02, BELOW all four.\n    It cannot be reached.**  (`:429` is the read-side memcpy; `:438` a zero-fill of the caller's\n    buffer.)\n  * **nonzero reset default** -- `:748` sets `pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_GENCTL] = 0x00`.  Excluded.\n  * **unzeroed allocation** -- `:738` `memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct pcc2_data))`.  Excluded.\n  * **numeric index 2 or byte-2 arithmetic** -- none.\n\n**SO: S3 AND S4 STAND, AND THEIR WARRANT IS NOW AN ENUMERATION RATHER THAN A GREP FOR A NAME.**  The\nseats did not find a defect in the code; they found one in my evidence, and refusing to ratify is\nwhat produced the real check.  *Unanimity on a brief-supplied fact is worth nothing -- but unanimous\nREFUSAL to accept one is worth a great deal.*\n\n**DEEPSEEK'S SMALLEST EDIT, which is a genuine clause-(d) mutant against the design's own preset:**\n*\"changing the C040 test to `!= 0x82`, leaving the 0x82 control green.\"*  The witness design uses a\nC040-clear round-trip of 0x82 as its same-decode preset -- so a repair conditioned on that exact\nvalue keeps the preset green while the defect returns for every other value.  **The preset and the\nmutant would share a constant**, which is the shared-constant hazard `m88ksfd1row` is queued about.\nRecorded as an owed mutant before the round runs.\n\n**minimax's REJECT additionally calls S6 internally inconsistent with the disposition it verifies** --\nunread in detail; carried as an open item for the next pass rather than summarised from its verdict\nline.\n\n*** A GENUINE DISPOSITION SPLIT BETWEEN THE TWO STRONGEST SEATS.  FOR THE FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION;\nBOTH SIDES IN THEIR OWN WORDS. ***\n\n**codex (pass-1 audit): DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE.**  *\"Survival is a nonvacuous oracle here, but\ncannot establish interrupt correctness.\"*\n\n**grok (full panel): DO NOT SHIP THAT.  ACCEPT.**  *\"DO NOT SHIP DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE.  S1-S4\nhold after re-enumeration.  The right member is ACCEPT (store the byte, delete the `exit(1)`, fall\nthrough).\"*\n\n*** grok's CASE, and it is the better-evidenced of the two: ***\n  * **C040 is R/W in the board manual.**  `OPER R/W` on that bit in VMESBCP2 -- the manual this\n    project already has at `_scratchpad/vmesbcp2.pdf`.  It is **not an unlisted offset, not a\n    read-only bit, and not a wide write with a lane hazard**.\n  * *** SO APPLYING `#500`/`#501`'s DECLINE IS WARRANT TRANSFER FROM THE WRONG SIBLING -- AND THAT\n    WAS MY ERROR. ***  I took the disposition from the same FILE without checking the same\n    SITUATION.  `#500` declines because a wide write's `idata` carries the neighbour's lane;\n    `#501` declines an unlisted OFFSET; VECBASE masks bits the manual forbids; CHIPID is read-only.\n    **None of those is a legal R/W bit at a listed offset.**\n  * **In-tree ACCEPT precedents it names instead:** `dev_footbridge.c`'s `+0x188` IRQEnable\n    (computed the right value then `exit(1)` -> ACCEPT, no latch, because there is no event to\n    announce); `#432` TIMER_EXTERNAL (a legal named encoding that hit `exit(1)`); and\n    `m8820x_unimplemented` (complain-once and continue).\n  * **The decisive argument: a latched \"C040 interrupt assertions... TODO\" followed by actually\n    asserting INT is A WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE** -- the same shape the IRQEnable round refused,\n    printing \"unimplemented; ignored\" about a path that is implemented and is not ignored.\n  * **C040 selects which PINS carry a level this emulator does not model.  It does not change the\n    level itself.**  So storing the bit and using the existing 88100 INT path is a no-op relative to\n    every observable the emulator has -- which is ACCEPT, not a refusal.\n  * The addition licence: *\"one more latch cannot hurt\" is not a warrant*, and this file just spent a\n    round moving IPL/MASK off process-global statics.\n  * *(Context, offered as context and not measurement: OpenBSD/mvme88k never writes `pcc2_genctl`\n    in the in-tree guest; the 68k sibling sets IEN and not C040.)*\n\n*** ONE SENTENCE OF grok's IS FACTUALLY WRONG, AND ITS CONCLUSION SURVIVES WITHOUT IT. ***  It writes\nthat *\"the helper ALREADY COMPUTED IPL vs MASK vs MIEN and is about to INTERRUPT_ASSERT/DEASSERT when\nit dies.\"*  **It has not.**  The C040 test is `dev_pcc2.c:271`, the first statement after\n`int assert = 0;`, and the recalculation begins at `:279-282`.  Nothing is computed when it dies.\n\n  **But the substantive claim does not depend on the tense:** delete the kill and the helper WILL\n  compute the level and assert on the existing path, and C040 does not change that level.  So the\n  REASON is wrong and the CONCLUSION stands -- recorded that way rather than letting a wrong sentence\n  ride inside a right verdict, which this project has been bitten by before.\n\n**grok also holds S6's attribution INSUFFICIENT even as corrected:** *\"it cannot tell bit from value,\ncannot tell which repair you shipped, and at 0x04 it cannot tell Q2's two helper tails apart.\"*  That\nthird clause is the sharpest: **the witness cannot distinguish fall-through from return-without-\nasserting**, which is exactly the Q2 the audit left open.\n\n*** THIS VINDICATES THE HOLD, and it is worth saying plainly.  On codex's answer alone I would have\nshipped DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE -- which grok argues is a WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE, of the\nexact shape a previous round refused.  Two seats, opposite dispositions, and the owner's\nhold-for-adjudication directive is what keeps it from being settled by whichever seat I asked\nfirst. ***\n\n**FOR THE ADJUDICATION:** the question is not \"decline or accept\" in the abstract.  It is whether a\nLEGAL R/W BIT whose modelled effect is nil should be STORED (accept) or REFUSED (decline), and\nwhether an announce that describes an unmodelled PIN SELECTION while the INT path is used is a true\nrecord or a false one.\n\n*** THE RUNG-3 WITNESS IS BUILT AND MEASURED.  `regress/pcc2_c040_probe.py`, 3/4 on the committed\nbuild, artifact `regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/witness_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n**The `witness` field used to read *\"none -- READ, not run ... it has NOT been made, and this field\nsays `not yet done', never `did not think to ask'.\"*  That obligation is now discharged**, and the\nlifecycle's own ruling is why it existed: *ask one seat to try a rung-3 probe before recording a\nround as rung-1*.  The measurement:\n\n    genctl ctl     alive        alive        CONTROL: same st.b, same GENCTL, benign 0x10\n    genctl ctlq    no           no           CONTROL: the benign write must NOT reach the C040 arm\n    c040 kill      died(exit1)  alive        THE DEFECT -- RED, as clause (i) requires\n    c040 attrib    yes          yes          ATTRIBUTION: the death carries the arm's unique text\n    EVIDENCE: pcc2: C040 interrupt assertions... TODO\n\n**ONE legal guest byte store ends the host.**  No driver, no second access, no interrupt: the GENCTL\nwrite arm stores `idata` UNMASKED and calls `reassert_interrupts()` in the same access, whose first\ntest is C040.  *That one-access reading is a codex CORRECTION of the row's own founding story* -- the\nrow was filed around a longer SCCRXIACK road reachable from OpenBSD's `cl.c`, which is real and\nSUPERFLUOUS.  Nobody had noticed the short road because the long one was interesting.\n\n**THE WITNESS'S OWN CONTRACT, written into its header:** it witnesses THIS `exit(1)` and is **NOT a\ndetector of the repair**.  The adjudicated disposition is DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, and *\"host\nsurvives\"* greens for a silent decline, a latched one and a real C040 implementation alike.  **A fix\nround owes a row that discriminates them** -- for the latched form, the announce text and its\nonce-only property are the available channel.\n\n*Two apparatus failures on the way in, both caught by the probe's own controls rather than by\ninspection: a missing `DUMMY` made the forked child die on a NameError, and `c040 attrib` correctly\nreported **died-elsewhere** rather than crediting the kill; then the `\\0` in the repair cooked into\nFOUR REAL NULL BYTES in the source, which is this project's escape-consumption class arriving through\na file write instead of a shell.  The second fix is escape-free by construction -- `bytes(4)`, which\nno layer can consume.*\n\n*** PASS 1 FIX-DESIGN PANEL: SIX SEATS, UNANIMOUS **IMPLEMENT-WITH-REVISIONS**.  Panel dir\n`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_233310/`.  agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (reserved for measurement);\nopus5 and fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (reserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication).  The kimi CLI\nis 403-WALLED and its seat was COVERED by the kimi-k3:cloud substitute -- recorded by transport,\nand PACKET-FED, so it is attack reasoning and not a repo-fact reading. ***\n\n### *** 1.  FOUR SEATS CAUGHT THE SAME DEFECT IN MY PROPOSAL: THE DELETION WAS NEVER STATED. ***\n\ngrok, minimax, deepseek and the kimi substitute each reached it independently.  minimax put it\nplainest: *\"The fix as written is ADDED code near the MIEN test, but the inlined function still\ncontains the original `fatal(...); exit(1);`.  Nothing in the brief says delete the top block.  A\nnaive application that pastes the new code without deleting the old will keep killing the host.\"*\nkimi: *\"an insertion-only patch is a silent no-op.\"*\n\n**The proposal's comment said \"used to be\", which IMPLIES the removal without stating it.**  The\npatch must say REPLACE `:271-277`, not add at the tail.  *When four independent readers make the\nsame inference, the brief is the defect -- this project's own sketch-rule finding.*\n\n### *** 2.  P1 AND Q1 ARE NOW SETTLED FROM THE PRIMARY SOURCE -- AND THE MANUAL WAS ON THIS\n### MACHINE THE WHOLE TIME, WITH THE CARRIER TELLING ME TO OPEN IT. ***\n\nminimax held the entire round conditional on P1 and named the missing artifact exactly: *\"One fact\nneeded: an authoritative PCC2 GENCTL bit map\"*, adding that if the value were 0x03 *\"the proposed\nfix would silently latch on a bit the hardware never uses.\"*\n\n**`_scratchpad/vmesbcp2.txt` is the Motorola MVME187 board manual, added 2026-09-05, and the\ncarrier says in terms: \"A round about PCC2/CD2401/MVME187 opens THIS before reasoning from the\nsource alone.\"  I did not.**  Opened now, at its General Control Register page:\n\n    ADR/SIZ   $FFF42002 (8 bits)\n    DIR       15    14  13  12  11    10    9     8\n    NAME      DR0                     C040  MIEN  FAST\n    OPER      R/W   R   R   R   R     R/W   R/W   R/W\n    RESET     0     0   0   0   0     0     0     0\n\nThe manual numbers the bits 15..8 as the upper byte of a word view, so 15->7, 10->2, 9->1, 8->0:\n\n    DR0  = 0x80     C040 = 0x04     MIEN = 0x02     FAST = 0x01\n\n**ALL FOUR MATCH THE FORK'S HEADER EXACTLY.  P1 IS CONFIRMED and the OpenBSD `include/pcctworeg.h`\n`C040 0x03` is simply a buggy header** -- 0x03 is `FAST|MIEN`, which is not a coherent C040 mask.\n*The extracted-table stagger hazard is covered here by FOUR calibration points plus the address\n`$FFF42002` matching offset 0x02 and the all-zero RESET row matching the modelled reset.*\n\n*** AND THE SAME PAGE SETTLES Q1, WHICH NO SEAT COULD ANSWER FROM THE PACKET: ***\n\n> *\"If the C040 bit is set, the interrupt request goes to the MPU via the EIPL\\* pins **at the level\n> that is programmed for SCSI interrupts in the SCSI Interrupt Control Register**.  If the C040 bit\n> is cleared, the interrupt goes to the MPU via the INT pin...\"*\n\n**C040 selects the DELIVERY PATH, not the level.**  The programmed interrupt level is used either\nway, so the IPL recalculation stays meaningful in C040 mode and only the ASSERTION is suppressed --\nwhich is exactly the proposed shape, now on a primary-source warrant instead of an inference from\nthe sibling idiom.  **MIEN's own text confirms the relationship**: *\"When it is low, all interrupts\nfrom the PCCchip2 are disabled (this includes both the EIPL\\* pins and the INT pin)\"* -- MIEN gates\nBOTH paths, C040 selects BETWEEN them.  So joining the two tests is right and they are not\nredundant.\n\n*Q1 was the question three seats said the packet could not settle.  It cost one grep of a file the\ncarrier had already told me to open.*\n\n### 3.  TWO PANEL CONCERNS CHECKED AND REFUTED (not judged)\n\n* **minimax #4 / deepseek's bit-collision mutant.**  `PCC2_W_*` is a **C99 enum with sequential\n  members terminated by `PCC2_W_NBITS`**, plus a `pcc2_w_fits_in_warned` width assert.  **A\n  correctly-added enumerator CANNOT collide** -- that is the same \"duplicates impossible by\n  construction\" idiom this project adopted deliberately elsewhere.  *The row is still worth having\n  against a hand-written literal, which is what deepseek's step 1 actually tests.*\n* **minimax #3, \"the witness goes stale/red after the fix\".**  Mechanically FALSE:\n  `row(\"c040 attrib\", \"yes\" if (alive2 or sig) else \"died-elsewhere\", \"yes\", ...)` -- post-fix\n  `alive2` is true, so it evaluates \"yes\" against a wanted \"yes\" and stays **GREEN**.  *The real\n  point survives in weaker form: the row becomes VACUOUS post-fix, since it can no longer fail, and\n  the EVIDENCE print falls silent.*\n\n### 4.  THE ROWS THE ROUND NOW OWES (from the panel, not from me)\n\n* **deepseek's four-step latch row** -- pre-dirty another `warned` bit via ordinary guest traffic;\n  write C040; assert host survives AND the announce appears EXACTLY ONCE; write C040 again; assert\n  no second announce.  *Separates silent decline, non-latching and bit collision in one session;\n  \"presence alone is not enough.\"*\n* **minimax's non-suppressing mutant row** -- C040 set with MIEN=1 and IPL above the mask, asserting\n  the CPU interrupt line is NOT asserted.  **Without it, a fix that announces loudly and forgets\n  `assert = 0` is green.**\n\n### 5.  PROVENANCE FOUND IN THE FILE'S OWN COMMENT\n\n`pcc2widthkills` already documented this defect in product code while deliberately scoping it out:\n*\"NOT FIXED HERE, cited so 'twelve kills removed' is not read as 'file clean': pcc2c040kill (a LEGAL\nbyte write of 0x04 to GENCTL still ends the host in reassert_interrupts).\"*  **An independent\nconfirmation of the defect, written by the round that chose not to fix it** -- and the sentence this\nround discharges.\n\n*** FIXED AS `#512`.  Kill table 2026-09-08 in a scratch clone: four mutants KILLED, ONE ESCAPE\nWHICH THE ROUND GRADES AS A FLOOR BREACH RATHER THAN DISCLOSING IT AWAY. ***\n\n    BASE 7/7 | M_b_reinstate 3/7 | M_d2_silent 4/7 | M_d3_nolatch 6/7 | M_d4_collide 6/7\n    M_d1_noassert 7/7  *** CLEAN SWEEP -- the non-suppressing mutant escapes ***\n\n`assert = 0` is the repair's EFFECT, so nulling it is inside clause (d)'s DEPENDS-ON closure.  The\nescape is structural, verified twice: `PCC2_IRQ_INT` (0x20) is masked out of every guest-writable\narm (`TxICR & 0x17`, `SCCICR & 0x1f`) and its only producer is `pcc_timer_tick`, a `timer_add`\nHOST-TIMER callback that the debugger stops for the duration of a `step`.  **The repair is a\nfree-running probe shape, filed as `pcc2c040assertrow`.**\n\n*** THE codex PASS-1 ANSWER, MINED 2026-09-08 -- I HAD RECORDED IT AS \"findings not yet fully mined\",\nWHICH IS AN UNREAD REVIEW WITH A HONEST LABEL ON IT.  It corrects TWO of the round's premises and\nsupplies a MUTANT THE KILL TABLE DID NOT BUILD. ***\n\n**P2 WAS OVERSTATED AND THE CORRECT WORDING IS WEAKER.**  I wrote *\"No file in the OpenBSD 2.2 tree\nwrites C040 to GENCTL.\"*  codex: **\"The defensible wording is 'no explicit C040-setting use found,'\nnot 'no file writes C040'\"** -- because a grep over named macros cannot exclude literal or indirect\nstores, and, decisively, **P3's read-modify-write (`genctl |= IEN`) WRITES BACK AN ALREADY-SET C040\nBIT.**  Its census also beats mine: C040 defines sit in THREE source headers (mvme88k `dev/`,\nmvme88k `include/`, mvme68k `dev/`) plus an on-disk `machine/` copy.\n\n**P5 WAS TOO NARROW.**  I wrote that C040 means *\"the CPU is an MC68040\"*.  codex: it selects the\n**M68000-family interrupt INTERFACE**, and *\"the manual explicitly says to clear it for an\nMC88100\"*.  The robustness rationale is unaffected; the shipped-guest compatibility claim remains\nunestablished either way.\n\n*** AND A CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT THE KILL TABLE DID NOT BUILD, DISTINCT FROM M_d1: \"SUPPRESSION INSIDE\nTHE LATCH\". ***  Keep the guard AND the announcement, but move `assert = 0` INSIDE the\n`if (!warned)` block:\n\n> *\"The first encounter suppresses correctly; later encounters can assert the CPU line.  The\n> announcement count remains exactly one.  This directly meets clause (d)'s 'guard kept, repair\n> nulled' shape.\"*\n\n**Every shipped row stays green under it** -- `c040 says` yes, `c040 once` 1, `c040 predirty` yes.\nSo the detector has a SECOND declared escape, and it is nastier than `M_d1` because the fix looks\ncorrect on first contact and fails only on repeat encounters.\n\n> **THE SHIPPED FIX IS NOT VULNERABLE TO IT: `assert = 0;` is OUTSIDE the latch block**, which is\n> what codex's implementation note asked for (*\"Keep `assert = 0` outside the warning-latch\n> condition\"*).  Verified in the diff.  **The gap is in the DETECTOR, not in the code** -- and it\n> folds into `pcc2c040assertrow`, which needs the same free-running shape.\n\ncodex also independently re-derived the `(once)` census the carrier says never to quote: **30\n`(once)` after excluding comments and joining adjacent C string literals**, versus 12\n`(once per offset)`; the 34 raw occurrences include four comments.\n\n*It flagged one provenance gap in my own artifact, correctly: the witness raw names `build/gxemul`\nwith no commit or binary hash, so it does not independently establish which build it measured.*\n\n*** PASS 2 REFUSED THE ROUND'S CLOSING DISPOSITION, AND IT IS RIGHT.  STATE REVERTED `fixed` ->\n`open` 2026-09-08. ***\n\n**grok's ruling:** *\"disposition 2 is wrong.  1 and 2 cannot both stand.  1 is the right grade ...\n2 is then the (e) REMEDY applied to a (d) GRADE.  That is the contradiction.  L2 says replace the\ndetector in-round.  Coverage-debt filing is the above-floor path.  **Honesty about the breach does\nnot create an exception L2 does not have; a declared L2 that ships is the precedent this question\nexists to refuse.**\"*\n\nAnd the sharpest sentence, which I should have seen myself: *\"The CHANGELOG already says the round\ndoes not claim a floor-meeting detector -- good -- and the ledger row is already `state: fixed`.\n**Those two sentences cannot sit together.**\"*  **They did, for about an hour, in records I wrote.**\n\n*** AND MY WARRANT FOR SHIPPING WAS FALSE.  VERIFIED HERE, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nI claimed the non-suppressing mutant could not be rowed because `PCC2_IRQ_INT` has *\"only one\nproducer, `pcc_timer_tick`, a host-timer callback that `step` stops\"*.  **The first half is right and\nthe second is wrong:**\n\n    dev_pcc2.c  pcctwo_interrupt_common()  ORs PCC2_IRQ_INT onto SCSIICR / SCCTX / SCCRX\n                                           when those lines assert\n    dev_clmpcc.c:88-89   if ((d->reg[CLMPCC_REG_IER] & 3) != 0)\n                                 INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq_scc_tx);\n    machine_mvme88k.c    constructs clmpcc with irq=...pcc2...\n\n**So a GUEST STORE to the clmpcc IER asserts a line whose callback sets the INT bit, synchronously,\ninside that store.**  No host timer, no free-running probe: `st.b` IEN|IL to SCCTX, `st.b` to\nCLMPCC IER, `st.b` `C040|MIEN` to GENCTL.  **The obstacle was never the STATE -- it is the ORACLE.**\n\n> **THE ERROR IN MY REASONING, stated plainly because it is a repeatable one: I checked that no\n> guest store writes INT INTO THE ICR, and concluded that no guest store can set it.**  A store to a\n> DIFFERENT DEVICE sets it through the interrupt callback.  *\"Which stores write this field\" is not\n> the same question as \"which stores cause this field to change\".*\n\n*** grok's Q2: THE SMALLEST UNDECLARED EDIT THAT STILL SCORES 7/7 IS ONE TOKEN. ***\n\n**`assert = 0;` -> `return;`**  Announce, latch, survival, `SAYS`, once-count and predirty are all\nunchanged; **the CPU INT line is simply never deasserted.**  No shipped row sees it, and grok gives\nthe reason: *\"every session writes 0x04 into a reset GENCTL, so MIEN is already clear, `assert` is\nalready 0, and `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` of a quiet line is a no-op.  The discriminating sequence is\npending INT + MIEN, THEN set C040.  The 7 rows never build that.\"*\n\nFour more same-score edits it named: move the block to the top and `return` (skips IPL recalc,\n`cur_int_vec[]` AND deassert); drop `(once)` from the message (`c040 once` counts `SAYS`, not the\nmarker); `fatal` -> `debug` (the probe launches with `-V`, so it still prints); swap the C040 and\nMIEN tests (independent, order invisible).\n\n*** AN L4 IN THE SHIPPED PRODUCT COMMENT, FOUND BY READING THE CONTROL FLOW. ***  My comment said an\nearly return here *\"would skip the IPL recalculation, the cur_int_vec[] updates and the\nINTERRUPT_DEASSERT below\"*.  **At the NEW site the first two have already run** -- a return there\nskips only the DEASSERT.  That sentence is true of a return at the OLD site and false at this one:\n*\"a wrong reason attached to a right placement.  Wrong reasons get reused -- the next mutant is\nexactly `return` at this site.\"*  **Corrected in place.**\n\n**Dispositions 3, 4 and 5 were CHECKED and HOLD** -- including that the width probe's `latch all`\nrow still wants 12 announces from 12 wide writes and does not iterate `NBITS`, so appending the enum\nmember renumbers nothing; and that `c040 attrib` is green post-fix only via `alive2`, which grok\ncalls *\"the price of clause (i)\"* and agrees is right.\n\n**DISPOSITION: the three product hunks are correct and stay.  The ROUND does not close.**  The L2\nrepair is now known to be buildable in a stepped probe, so it is an in-round obligation rather than\na filed one.\n\n*** THE FLOOR BREACH IS CLOSED IN-ROUND, WHICH IS WHAT PASS 2 REQUIRED. ***\n\n    BASE            11/11\n    M_d1_noassert    9/10   `assert = 0' DELETED        -- was 7/7 (a CLEAN SWEEP)   KILLED\n    M_return         9/10   `assert = 0' -> `return;'   -- the one-token pass-2 mutant KILLED\n    M_latchinside   10/11   `assert = 0' INSIDE the latch -- was 10/10               KILLED\n\nThe round no longer ships a declared clause-(d) escape.  `pcc2c040assertrow` -- filed when the\nrepair was believed to need a probe shape this project did not have -- is CLOSED, and the detector\nis now 11 rows.\n\n*Pass 2's ruling was that grading (d) while FILING the repair is \"the (e) remedy applied to a (d)\ngrade\".  The correct response was to build the rows, and building them took one design review and\ntwo measured tables.*\n\n*** PASS 2 ON THE L2 REPAIR: SIX SEATS, TWO DO-NOT-SHIP, AND **FOUR OF SIX** REFUSED THE SAME\nDISPOSITION.  The detector went 11 -> 16 rows and now kills NINE mutants across FIVE tables. ***\n\n**FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REFUSED DISPOSITION 1** (grok, deepseek, glm, the kimi substitute);\nminimax defended it *conditionally* and named its own condition.  **I had declared the encounter\naxis closed while enumerating only the C040=1 slice of a two-lvalue product** -- and had filed the\nempty cell as contract debt, which is the SECOND time this round graded a floor-shaped hole that\nway.\n\n    THE PRODUCT              warned = 0          warned = 1\n      C040 clear             `irq live'          `c040 recover'   <- was EMPTY\n      C040 set               `c040 noassert'     `c040 noassert2'\n\n**THE FULL KILL RECORD, five tables:**\n\n    table 1   7 rows   M_b_reinstate 3/7 | M_d2_silent 4/7 | M_d3_nolatch 6/7 | M_d4_collide 6/7\n                       M_d1_noassert 7/7  *** ESCAPED ***\n    table 2  10 rows   M_d1_noassert 9/10 KILLED | M_return 9/10 KILLED\n                       M_latchinside 10/10  *** ESCAPED ***\n    table 3  11 rows   M_latchinside 10/11 KILLED\n    table 4  12 rows   M_sticky 11/12 KILLED        (grok's `|| (warned & ...)')\n    table 5  13 rows   M_mienclobber 12/13 KILLED   (`GENCTL &= ~PCC2_MIEN')\n\n**`M_mienclobber` is the clearest demonstration of why the collateral row was owed:** `irq live` and\n`c040 noassert` BOTH stayed GREEN under it -- every delivery row passes a fix that suppresses by\ndisabling all interrupts -- and only the GENCTL readback saw it, coming back `0x04` instead of\n`0x06`.  **Three seats named that mutant independently.**\n\n*** THE LAST ROWS CAME FROM THE SUBSTITUTE SEAT AND NO OTHER SEAT SAW IT. ***  The liveness control\nexisted for ONE session of FOUR: `c040 noassert` reaching COMPLETION was consistent with \"the fix\nsuppressed correctly\" AND with \"no interrupt was ever pending in that session\".  Three per-session\nSCCTX readbacks close it, at zero extra cost because the sessions already ran.  **16/16.**\n\n*Two apparatus facts recorded for the next reader:* the PC regex demands eight hex digits, so it\nreads the register DUMP and not the unpadded `BREAKPOINT:` line (the values are right; the line is\nnot the one I thought); and `(\"continue\", 6.0)` carries no `until`, so the full grace always\nelapses.  *And glm reconstructed the mixed-denominator defect from the printed numbers alone --\nwhich I had already found and corrected in the commit message but NOT in the brief the panel read.*\n\n*** THE FIRST PASS-2 PANEL HAD FIVE UNRECORDED SEATS, AND THE PRECOMMIT GATE CAUGHT IT -- WHICH IS\nTHIS SESSION'S OWN MOST-REPEATED FINDING, COMMITTED BY ME, ON THE ROUND WHERE I KEPT FIXING IT. ***\n\n`SEATS_READ_FAIL 5 seat answers exist on disk and are recorded NOWHERE` -- codex (1.0 MB), deepseek,\nglm, kimi and minimax from `panel_20260908_000754`.  **I recorded grok's answer from that panel and\nnone of the other five**, then spent the following hours mining OTHER rounds' unread reviews.\n\n**Recorded now, and the tally matters: FIVE OF SIX SEATS PUT THEIR RESIDUAL ON DISPOSITION 2** (ship\nwith the breach declared).  Only kimi declined to call it wrong, and for an honest packet-fed reason\n-- clause (d)'s text was not inlined, so it named the single fact that would settle it instead of\nguessing.  **deepseek prescribed the exact repair that was then built:** *\"add a row that can set the\nICR INT bit, kill M_d1, and THEN ship.\"*\n\n*** AND panel_ollama.py AUTO-RECOVERED A THINKING BURN, WHICH THE CARRIER DOES NOT RECORD IT DOING.\n***  glm's first shot returned an EMPTY `response` beside **119,022 characters of hidden reasoning**\nwith `done_reason=stop` -- the fifth seat-failure class, in the exact shape the carrier says defeats\nevery size check.  **The tool re-fired with `think=False` and got 124,850 characters back.**  The\ncarrier says detection of this class is solved by `seat_verdicts.py`; it does not say the LAUNCHER\nnow recovers it.  *That is a capability worth knowing about before anyone writes another paragraph\nabout burns being unrecoverable.*\n\n*** COMMITTED AS `9fa07fe` (PRECOMMIT_PASS, 0 hard, 60 files).  THE ROW STAYS **OPEN** ON PURPOSE. ***\n\nThe code shipped and is measured; what is outstanding is the FLAGSHIP PASS, which is held and queued\nfor the 2026-09-09 adjudication.  **A row whose fix is committed but whose panel is incomplete is\n`open` with its commit named** -- the same shape `pcc2widthkills` carries -- because closing it would\nassert a completeness the roster does not have.  *That is the provisional-adjudication rule applied\nto state rather than to a ruling.*\n\n**The gate refused this commit THREE TIMES and was right every time** (6 hard -> 3 -> 0): five rows\nfiled only into the ledger; three stages held-but-not-queued; **five seat answers recorded nowhere**;\nand two tooling CONVENTIONS I guessed instead of read -- `hold_question` is a ROW-level field, and\n`check_fable_queue` parses a level-3 heading whose first backticked token is the row id.\n\n*** A codex ANALYSIS JOB WAS FIRED AT THIS ROW ON 2026-09-08 AND THE BRIEF WAS STALE.  THE DEFECT\nWAS MINE, AND IT IS THE ONE CHECK THE BRIEF TEMPLATE PUTS FIRST. ***\n\nI wrote the brief from this row's TITLE -- *\"a legal byte write ... ends the host in\nreassert_interrupts()\"* -- and did NOT run the template's `§C0` step (*HAS THIS ROUND ALREADY\nSHIPPED?  TWO COMMANDS, BEFORE YOU WRITE ANYTHING*).  I had run it for `#515` the same evening.\n\ncodex answered correctly and its verdict is the refutation: *\"the termination claim is stale for this\ncheckout ... Exactly one executable `exit(1)` remains, and there is no executable `exit(1)` in this\nhelper.  The opening comment claiming this write 'still ends the host' contradicts the\nimplementation.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED here, and codex is right on every count:**\n\n    active exit( in dev_pcc2.c, comments stripped:  1   (at :414, NOT the C040 arm)\n    dev_pcc2.c:369-376   the C040 arm today is a LATCHED ANNOUNCE + suppression --\n                         `d->warned & (1u << PCC2_W_C040)', `fatal(... (once))', `assert = 0'\n    git log -- dev_pcc2.c   9fa07fe  \"#512 pcc2c040kill: one legal guest byte store ended the host\"\n    CHANGELOG.md:427        the `#512' round block\n\n**SO THE FIX SHIPPED AS `#512` AND THIS ROW IS OPEN ON PURPOSE** -- its own note says so in capitals,\nawaiting the flagship pass held for 2026-09-09. *The row was right, the code was right, and the brief\nI fed the seat described a state that stopped existing at `9fa07fe`.*\n\n> **THE COST: about 84 KB of a file-reading seat's effort spent establishing that shipped work had\n> shipped.**  Not wasted -- it produced the [[pcc2c040dup]] header finding in passing -- but the\n> question it was asked was answerable by two commands I already knew to run.\n\n**AND THE ROW'S OWN NOTE IS WHAT STOPPED ME GOING FURTHER WRONG.**  Reading codex's verdict, my next\nmove was to close this row as overtaken. The note's *\"THE ROW STAYS OPEN ON PURPOSE\"* is the only\nthing that prevented it. **A state field alone would not have: `open` on shipped work looks exactly\nlike `open` on unstarted work**, which is the state-contradicts-substance class this project already\nrecords -- and here the prose carried the distinction the state could not.",
.\regress\artifacts\landiskaux\detector_20260918\PRETABLE.out:57:arm executes) and M_0x16/M_0x2d (operand weakened) cover it.  No joint cells: one guard, one operand.
