es once: field accumulates: after block i, field = bits 0..i. For block 0 (GENCTL): field 0 == bit_0? No \u2192 TEST true \u2192 announce; set \u2192 field = bit0. Block 1: field == bit1? No \u2192 announce. ... all twelve announce. Count 12. \u2713 stays green (contract says `latch all` catches it... wait no).\n\nThe contract says: \"The TEST line's operand swap (`&' -> `==') is caught by `latch all': with the field accumulating, equality fails from the second block on and it announces 12 either way; `latch aba' DOES distinguish it (A=1, then the field is 3, and 3 == 1 is false, so A re-announces -- MEASURED 100/101 by a pass-5 seat).\"\n\nWait, that's self-contradictory as written: \"is caught by `latch all'\" \u2014 but then says it announces 12 either way, so `latch all` does NOT catch it; `latch aba` does. Let me re-read: \"The TEST line's operand swap (`&' -> `==') is caught by `latch all': with the field accumulating, equality fails from the second block on and it announces 12 either way; `latch aba' DOES distinguish it\".\n\nHmm. \"caught by `latch all'\" then \"announces 12 either way\" \u2014 those contradict. Unless the sentence means the mutation is caught by `latch aba`, and the clause about `latch all` explains why `latch all` does NOT catch it. The wording \"is caught by `latch all'\" followed by an explanation of why `latch all` can't distinguish... Actually re-reading: I think the sentence is sloppy: it should say \"is NOT caught by `latch all'\". The structure: \"is caught by `latch all': with the field accumulating, equality fails from the second block on and it announces 12 either way\" \u2014 \"either way\" means mutant and fix both give 12, so `latch all` is green under the mutant \u2192 NOT caught by `latch all`. Then \"`latch aba' DOES distinguish it\". And the measured 100/101 with `latch aba` red confirms: M_TEST_EQ \u2192 117/118 latch aba (part 5). So the contract sentence \"is caught by `latch all'\" is a wording error \u2014 it should be \"NOT caught by `latch all'\". Wait, actually let me re-read once more:\n\n\"The TEST line's operand swap (`&' -> `==') is caught by `latch all': with the field accumulating, equality fails from the second block on and it announces 12 either way; `latch aba' DOES distinguish it (A=1, then the field is 3, and 3 == 1 is false, so A re-announces -- MEASURED 100/101 by a pass-5 seat).\"\n\nHmm \u2014 \"is caught by `latch all'\" \u2014 maybe it means \"is caught [by rows other than...]\"... No. The most natural reading: the author wrote \"is caught by `latch all'\" but then the colon-clause explains it is NOT. This is an internal contradiction in the contract text. Actually wait \u2014 maybe I should parse it as: \"is caught by `latch all'\" is the earlier (wrong) version, and the amendment... no, the contract also says \"An earlier version of this sentence called it indistinguishable; the instrument was stronger than its contract said.\" So the sentence was amended. The amendment appears to have been applied sloppily, leaving \"is caught by `latch all'\" contradicted by its own explanation. Let me check the measurement: part 5 M_TEST_EQ 117/118, red row `latch aba`. And pass-5 fable5 measured 100/101 `latch aba`. So `latch aba` catches it; `latch all` does not (announces 12 either way \u2014 under the fix, all twelve announce once; under the mutant... let me verify: block 0: field 0, 0 == bit0 false \u2192 announce; set field = bit0. Block 1: field = bit0; bit0 == bit1? false \u2192 announce; field |= bit1 \u2192 bits 0,1. Block 2: field == bit2? false \u2192 announce. All twelve announce. Count 12 = same as fix. So `latch all` green under mutant. \u2713 NOT caught.)\n\nSo the contract's sentence has an internal defect: it opens \"is caught by `latch all'\" when its own derivation and measurement say `latch all` cannot distinguish it and `latch aba` is the catcher. This is a record-honesty defect (Q4) and worth naming. Actually hold on, let me re-read the diff versio