t was `break' -> `return 0'` where the whole guard body is `pcc2_width_decline(...); return 0;` \u2014 same thing); (c) the mutation tool replaced the first `break;` in the FILE (which might be a different block)... Hmm, that's a real possibility: mutation at \"the break following the VECBASE guard\" \u2014 the VECBASE arm's guard break. That's what I assumed.\n\nHmm, actually WAIT. Let me recount the red rows. 116/118 means TWO reds. The listed: \"latch all fail,rb2 vecbase\". But ALSO \u2014 does `op vecbase dcl` change? Under RET0: put h at VECBASE in the `op vecbase` session: announce once (bit clear), return 0 \u2192 \"failed ]\" once. `op vecbase dcl` counts DECL == 1 \u2192 still 1 \u2192 green. \u2713 (Section 9 predicted \"op vecbase dcl stays 1\".) And `rb vecbase own`: put h 0xaab8 once \u2192 announce, return 0 \u2192 VECBASE stays 0x50 \u2192 green.\n\nSo only `latch all fail` should be red... and the table says also rb2 vecbase. Hmm, unless \u2014 OH WAIT. I see it now! In rb2 vecbase's session, the TWO put h writes: under RET0, the first put h announces AND returns 0. But the second put h: the guard is `if (len != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` \u2014 TRUE (len 2, write). Then pcc2_width_decline: TEST bit set \u2192 no announce. Then `return 0`. So memory_rw prints \"failed ]\" \u2014 TWICE in this session. But that doesn't affect the rb2 row's count...\n\nUNLESS the rb2 row is not what I think. Let me re-read the rb2 loop:\n\n```python\nfor nm, off, pre, want, guest in ((\"genctl\", GENCTL, \"0x82\", \"0x82\", True), ..., (\"vecbase\", VECBASE, \"0x50\", \"0x50\", False), ...):\n    if guest:\n        ...st.h twice...\n    else:\n        t, alive, how = session(setup(r5=pre) + program([ST_B(5, off)])\n                                + [\"put h 0x%x, 0xaab8\" % (PCC2 + off)] * 2\n                                + program([LD_BU(4, off)], at=CODE + 0x100) + [\"print r4\"])\n    row(\"rb2 \" + nm, printed(t, \"r4\") if alive else \"died\", want, ...)\n```\n\nSo rb2 vecbase = readback after two declined put h. Under RET0: 0x50 \u2192 green.\n\nHmm, so the part-5 measurement says rb2 vecbase red. Something's off. Let me think about `return 0` semantics ONE more time. In gxemul, DEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2) \u2014 the access function's return value: `memory_rw` in gxemul: for device accesses, the return value indicates exception. In dev_pcc2.c the final `return 1;` typically means OK/no exception! Let me recall GXEMUL source: `#define DEVICE_ACCESS(name) int dev_ ## name ## _access(...)` and returns 1 = success, 0 = exception? In gxemul's memory_rw.c: \n\n```\nif (!devaccess(...)) ... exception?\n```\n\nHmm. In gxemul, memory_rw does something like:\n```\nif (devaccess(..., writeflag, ...)) { ... } else { fatal(\"...failed...\") }\n```\nThe contract says: \"memory_rw's own line, '[ writing to device 'pcc2' addr ... failed ]', once per block whose guard returned 0\" \u2014 so return 0 \u2192 \"failed\" printed. That's GIVEN in the packet. So my reading is right: RET0 \u2192 failed printed per wide write at that block.\n\nSo under M_RET0_VECBASE, `latch all fail` = 1 (red, expected 0) \u2713, and rb2 vecbase... hmm.\n\nOHHH WAIT. I bet I see it: in rb2 vecbase's session, the debugger does TWO put h. Each prints \"failed ]\". Fine. But the row value: printed(t, \"r4\") \u2014 the guest program runs `LD_BU(4, VECBASE)` then I_LD_SCR, then `print r4`. Hmm wait, program([LD_BU(4, off)], at=CODE + 0x100) \u2192 insns = [LD_BU] + [I_BR_SELF, 0]; cmds = put w of each word, then pc=..., step 1. Fine.\n\nUnless... the second put h, having its latch bit set, skips the announce, returns 0, and memory_rw \u2014 wait, does memory_rw print \"failed\" and then ALSO do something else, like not store the word? The debugger put h \u2014 store_16bit_word \u2192 memory_rw(len 2) \u2192 device dispatch \u2192 access returns 0 \u2192 \"failed\" \u2192 the debugger prints an error. The debugger might then... hmm, the debugger's command loop might ABOR