orrect code does not. REFATAL_EXPECT[\"trapfrozen10\"] = False, so presence of the line \u2192 bad=refatal \u2192 BADREGS. Consistent.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety: under the mutant, `update_shadow_regs = false` still happens (it's outside the inner if). For trapfrozen10, shadows should be frozen anyway (SFRZ set \u2192 update_shadow_regs = false). Actually in the correct code, the outer `if (PSR & SFRZ)` sets update_shadow_regs = false regardless of trap status. So the shadows are frozen either way. Fine.\n\n**Check 3: M_ERRORFRZOFF and trapfrozen10.**\n\nM_ERRORFRZOFF: switch 1's ERROR arm `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)`. For trapfrozen10: the trap names vector 10 \u2192 class 10 \u2192 ERROR arm. With the mutant, implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ) = !1 = 0 (SFRZ is set \u2014 frozen). So it goes to the fallback: vector < 128 && !implemented \u2192 fallback fires, prints `unimplemented vector=0x00a`, stops all. Result: HALT. The table says \"trapfrozen10 HALT (naming 0x00a)\". Consistent \u2014 0x00a = 10. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 does the fallback fire BEFORE the re-route? Premise 2: order is switch 1, THEN fallback, THEN SFRZ re-route. Yes. So the fallback catches it. HALT naming 0x00a. Consistent.\n\nHmm, but one question: in the correct code, what is `implemented` for the ERROR arm? Presumably `implemented = true` unconditionally for all classes 1..10? Actually for trap vectors... Let me think about what the ERROR arm is. The class switch maps vector -> class; for vectors 1..10ish, implemented = true. The mutant conditions it on SFRZ. Fine.\n\n**Check 4: The err* rows' refatal expectations and the fallback ordering.**\n\nREFATAL_EXPECT: error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl \u2192 True (must see the line). These are non-trap second faults with SFRZ set \u2192 re-route fires (PSR & SFRZ, !is_trap) \u2192 prints. Good.\n\ntrapfrozen* \u2192 False. Traps with SFRZ set \u2192 exempted, no print. Good.\n\nBut wait: what about the BADREGS check for err* rows? The err* rows expect DELIVER to 0x50 (re-routed to ERROR vector 10). The re-route sets vector = ERROR before the common path. So the entry is 0x50. And the REFATAL check: `if name in REFATAL_EXPECT and ((REFATAL in tr) != REFATAL_EXPECT[name]): bad.append(\"refatal\")`. For err* rows, REFATAL must be in tr. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 a subtle issue: the row `error` itself. Row \"error\" is a second fault naming... Let me look: the PSR_WANT / want dict for the frozen set includes \"error\". The row `error` is presumably: first fault sets SFRZ, then handler faults again naming ERROR (non-trap)? Actually \"error\" row: second fault with SFRZ set, non-trap, class... hmm, if it names ERROR as a non-trap? How does a non-trap fault name ERROR? Maybe an actual ERROR exception (vector 10 is \"reserved\"/ERROR in m88k). The re-route: PSR & SFRZ, !is_trap \u2192 vector = ERROR (already 10), fatal printed. So error row: expects the line. OK.\n\n**Check 5: The REFATAL string and the `tr` variable.**\n\n`REFATAL = \"SFRZ already set in PSR\"`. The fatal prints `[ SFRZ already set in PSR => ERROR ]`. Substring match in transcript `tr`. Fine. Note premise 3: fatal is a plain print, cannot be silenced by -q, lands in pty transcript. OK.\n\n**Check 6: trapfrozen128 and M_SFRZTRAPUSER.**\n\nM_SFRZTRAPUSER: re-route `if (!is_trap || vector >= 128)`. trapfrozen128: frozen trap naming 128 \u2192 vector >= 128 \u2192 re-route fires (inner condition, similar shape) \u2192 vector = ERROR = 10 \u2192 delivered to 0x50 instead of 0x400. The expected entry for trapfrozen128 is 0x400. So the breakpoint at 0x400 doesn't fire; instead... the table says \"trapfrozen128 STOP (re-routed to 0x50 instead of 0x400)\". STOP = anything else (machine stopped for another reason). Why STOP and not BADREGS? Because the entry breakpoint at 0x400 never fired, so no register assert; the probe sees... the machine eventually stops (0x50 has `br .` planted? 0x50 = 8*10, V_ERROR is in VECTORS, so `br .` is planted there. Wait, then it would hit the br . at 0x50 and just... The probe plants `br .` at every entry of every vector in VECTORS. The breakpoint is at the expected entry (0x400). If it lands at 0x50 instead, execution sits at the br . loop at 0x50? That would be a hang/timeout, not a stop. Hmm, but the table says STOP. Maybe the probe has a timeout and classifies. Or maybe the re-route to ERROR with frozen shadows causes something else. Actually \u2014 hmm, wait. Under the mutant, vector = ERROR = 10, but is the tail switch's ERROR arm... The common path would deliver to 0x50 with update_shadow_regs = false. The probe's expected entry 0x400 never reached; the probe continues and eventually the emulator... Actually STOP = \"the machine stopped for another reason\". Maybe the probe runs with a timeout and any stop. This is a detail I can't fully verify from the packet; it's a measurement claim anyway. I should not challenge measured claims per se, but I can check consistency.\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 why would the machine stop? Under M_SFRZTRAPUSER, when the frozen trap naming 128 is re-routed, fatal() is a plain print... it doesn't stop. Deliver to 0x50, which has `br .` planted... the probe would wait at the breakpoint for 0x400 forever? Unless the probe's `continue` returns when the machine halts for any reason. If the guest sits at br . (0x50), the emulator keeps running (br . loops). The probe probably has a timeout. The classification \"STOP\" = \"the machine stopped for another reason\". I can't verify; it's their measurement. Fine.\n\n**Check 7: The gate's claim list vs the diff.**\n\nThe gate pins: M88KEXC_RESULT=41/41, seven HALT rows (resid50, resid127, resid11, resid50ind, resid50c, sfu1h, usfu1) \u2014 that's 7, matches EXPECT_RESULT which has exactly those 7. Eight frozen-trap rows `trapfrozen(4|5|6|8|9|10|128)?` DELIVER \u2014 hmm: trapfrozen, trapfrozen4, trapfrozen5, trapfrozen6, trapfrozen8, trapfrozen9, trapfrozen10, trapfrozen128 = 8 rows. The regex `trap