ve err* rows exist (error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl per REFATAL_EXPECT), but the gate pins \"four frozen-handler rows at 0x50\". Hmm, wait \u2014 is `error` a \"frozen-handler\" row? error's shape: first fault, then handler faults with ERROR... Actually what makes a row \"frozen-handler\"? The PSR_WANT/want dict hunk: `if name in (\"error\", \"trapfrozen\", \"errovf\", \"errdiv0\", \"errpriv\", \"errunimpl\", \"trapfrozen4\", ...)` \u2014 \"the second fault runs with SFRZ set: the shadows stay FROZEN at the first fault's values\". So all these have handlers that fault again. err* = error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl = 5 rows with frozen shadows delivered at 0x50. So \"four frozen-handler rows at 0x50\" appears to be off by one (should be five), OR the gate counts differently (e.g., excluding `error` because its original vector is already 10? \"frozen-handler rows at 0x50\" \u2014 all five land at 0x50). Hmm, but wait \u2014 actually, maybe \"frozen-handler\" is a term of art in this gate for the rows whose handler words are planted at the expected entry and which expect FROZEN shadows... trapfrozen10 also has a planted handler and lands at 0x50 with frozen shadows. That'd make six.\n\nHmm, alternatively \"the four frozen-handler rows\" could be error/errovf/errpriv/errunimpl where errdiv0 is grouped elsewhere? I can't settle this. It's a discrepancy worth naming: the gate narration says four, the composite has five err* rows (all at 0x50). But the brief also says the gate pins M88KEXC_RESULT=41/41, so even if the per-row pin missed one, the 41/41 aggregate would catch a wrong row. Actually would it? M88KEXC_RESULT=41/41 means all 41 rows green. So a mutant that breaks errdiv0 would make it 40/41 and fail the gate. The per-row pins are redundant narration. So even if \"four\" is a miscount, the gate still functions. This is cosmetic. But the panel asked: \"of the pass-7 dispositions... WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 the dispositions are: five row closure