.\regress\artifacts\ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2648:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass6_panel_20260906_102851\ollama_glm.txt:1:{"model": "glm-5.3", "created_at": "2026-09-06T15:35:03.158648302Z", "response": "", "thinking": "Let me carefully parse this brief.\n\nThe setup: I'm a \"packet-fed seat\" \u2014 I cannot read files or run commands. I must reason from the inlined text alone. The verdict line must come first. Each claim must be labeled READ or REASONED. Never MEASURED. If a premise cannot be settled from the packet, I must say so, name the premise, and say what single fact would settle it. Do not pad.\n\nThe pass: PASS 6 on the PASS-5 REPAIR (probe v5 -> v8, 36 fault rows). The question: does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the shipped composite? If yes, give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it; if none, CLOSE with my own denominator. Second question: of the pass-6 dispositions (listed: three class closures incl. the fallback's MODE cell, one harness extension, one disclosed equivalent (class 10), one interval correction, one driver-listing note, one PRETABLE-order disclosure, one count correction (seven -> five), three grok records), WHICH IS WRONG?\n\nSo I need to audit the premises as stated, find self-contradictions, and answer the two questions. Let me go through the packet carefully.\n\nFirst, the repair diff: probe v5 -> v8. Changes:\n\n1. Docstring changes: m88kexc comments about Table 6-3 / FIP definitions (a record fix).\n2. New rows trapfrozen4/5/6/9 (naming classes 4, 5, 6, 9), trapfrozen8 (naming DIV0=8, with handler planted at 0x48=OVF entry).\n3. New row resid127: tb1 0,r3,127 \u2014 pins the fallback's far bound.\n4. New row usfu1: stcr r5,cr1 with r5=0x0000000a (USER mode, SFD1 set) then legal divu r6,r5,r4 -> SFU1 (114) raised from user mode reaches the fallback.\n5. HARLT classifier now includes resid127, usfu1 in expected-HALT rows.\n6. CONTRACT clause (e) updated.\n7. PSR_USERSFD1 = 0x0000000a constant.\n8. TB1_0_R3_127 = 0xf003d87f constant.\n9. V_RESID2 = 127; VECTORS tuple extended.\n10. ROWS extended with the new rows; handler_at extension: run_row gains handler_at parameter, default V_DIV0; only trapfrozen8 sets it (to V_OVF).\n11. The frozen-asserts name list extended with the new trapfrozen* rows.\n12. EXPECT_RESULT and EXPECT_VECTOR extended.\n13. main loop: unpack rows with row[:7] and handler_at from row[7] if present.\n\nNow let me check details for internal consistency.\n\n**Check the vector numbering.** From the diff and premises:\n- V_MISALIGN = ? The trapfrozen4 row says \"frozen tb1 naming 4 -> its own vector 0x20\". 8*4 = 32 = 0x20. OK, class 4 = MISALIGNED. So V_MISALIGN = 4.\n- trapfrozen5 -> 0x28 = 8*5, class 5 = UNIMPL (UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE). OK.\n- trapfrozen6 -> 0x30 = 8*6, class 6 = PRIVILEGE. privsuptb: \"tb1 0,r3,6 ... the trap route into PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION... 0x30\". OK.\n- Class 7 = BOUNDS? trapfrozen: \"div0 whose handler at 0x40 is tb1 0,r3,7 ... it delivers to 0x38\". 0x38 = 8*7 = 56. So class 7 = BOUNDS (BOUND_VIOLATION). Hmm wait \u2014 trapfrozen row: first fault is DIV0 (handler planted at 0x40 = DIV0's entry 8*8=0x40). Wait, 8*8 = 64 = 0x40. So DIV0 = class 8 = ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE. The row's vector field says V_BOUNDS \u2014 that's the expected second-fault vector? Let's see: (\"trapfrozen\", [DIVU_R2_R3_R0], {\"r3\": 0x11}, V_BOUNDS, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, [TB1_0_R3_7]). Hmm, the row's \"vector\" field is V_BOUNDS. The comment says: \"handler at 0x40: tb1 0,r3,7 with SFRZ set -> a TRAP is not re-routed to ERROR\". So the first fault is DIV0 (divu with r3=0x11 nonzero divisor... wait r3=0x11, so divu r2,r3,r0 divides r3 by r0? Actually DIVU_R2_R3_R0 = divu r2, r3, r0 \u2014 the dividend is r3, divisor is r0. r0 = 0 presumably, so divide-by-zero -> DIV0 (class 8). Handler planted at 0x40 (8*8) = tb1 0,r3,7 \u2014 trap naming vector 7 (BOUNDS). Under SFRZ-set frozen semantics, the trap is delivered to its own vector 7 -> entry 0x38. So the expected DELIVER vector... the row's vector field is V_BOUNDS. Hmm, wait: the row tuple's \"vector\" field is the expected entry for the breakpoint: \"a breakpoint at the expected entry\". V_BOUNDS = 7 -> entry 8*7 = 0x38. Yes: \"it delivers to 0x38\". OK consistent.\n\nWait, but hold on. Earlier the docstring says \"trapfrozen div0 whose handler at 0x40 is tb1 0,r3,7 (r3=0x11): a TRAP with SFRZ already set is NOT re-routed to ERROR ... it delivers to 0x38 with the shadows still frozen. `if (!is_trap)' -> `if (true)' was a 10/10 escape (Opus, pass 2)  0x38\". OK.\n\nNow the class numbering:\n- 0..3: hardware-event tails (m88kexchwtraps) \u2014 presumably RESET etc.\n- 4 = MISALIGNED_ACCESS (0x20)\n- 5 = UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE (0x28)\n- 6 = PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION (0x30)\n- 7 = BOUND_VIOLATION (0x38)\n- 8 = ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE (0x40)\n- 9 = INTEGER_OVERFLOW (0x48)\n- 10 = ERROR (0x50)\n- 114 = SFU1_PRECISE (0x390)\n- 115 = SFU2? (0x398)\n- 128 = USER_TRAPS_START (0x400)\n\nCheck against the diff rows: trapfrozen8: (\"trapfrozen8\", [ADD_R2_R3_R4], {\"r3\": 0x7fffffff, \"r4\": 1}, V_DIV0, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, [TB1_0_R3_8], V_OVF). First fault = OVERFLOW (add of 0x7fffffff + 1) \u2014 wait, ADD_R2_R3_R4 with r3=0x7fffffff, r4=1 -> overflow -> class 9 (OVF, entry 0x48). Handler planted at 8*V_OVF = 0x48. Handler = TB1_0_R3_8 \u2014 trap naming vector 8 (DIV0). Under frozen semantics, delivered to its own vector 8 -> entry 0x40. The row's vector field = V_DIV0 (8) -> expected entry 0x40. The comment: \"first fault add 7fffffff+1 (handler planted at 0x48), handler tb1 0,r3,8: the frozen trap naming DIV0 delivers to 0x40 -- observable only because the handler is NOT at 0x40 for this row.\" Yes, consistent. And why: if the handler were planted at 0x40 (DIV0's entry, the default handler_at), then... hmm, the comment in the header says: \"[, handler_at: the vector whose entry the handler is planted at -- only trapfrozen8 sets it, to OVF (0x48), because its frozen trap names DIV0 and a handler AT 0x40 would make the first fault's breakpoint mask the second event]\". Wait \u2014 \"a handler AT 0x40 would make the first fault's breakpoint mask the second event\". Hmm, let me think. The probe plants a breakpoint at the expected entry. For trapfrozen8, expected entry is 0x40 (V_DIV0). If the handler were also planted at 0x40, then... the handler words at 0x40 would overwrite... no wait. The breakpoint at 0x40 would fire when the handler executes at 0x40 (the handler IS at 0x40), which would be before/at the same address as the expected delivery. Actually the issue: the first fault is OVF, delivered to 0x48. If handler_at = 0x40 (default), the handler (tb1 0,r3,8) would be planted at 0x40, and the trap names vector 8 -> entry 0x40. So the handler would be at the same address as the delivery target. The breakpoint at 0x40 (expected entry) would fire when the handler code at 0x40 executes its first word \u2014 i.e., the tb1 instruction at 0x40 would hit the breakpoint before delivering. Actually wait: the breakpoint is planted at the expected entry 0x40. The handler is also at 0x40. So when the handler's first word (the tb1) executes at 0x40, the breakpoint fires \u2014 indistinguishable from the delivery breakpoint. So the probe couldn't tell whether the trap delivered to 0x40 or the breakpoint fired on the handler's first instruction. Hence handler_at = OVF (0x48): the OVF handler (first fault) is planted at 0x48, and the breakpoint at 0x40 fires only when the second event delivers there. Makes sense. Also \"the first fault's breakpoint\" \u2014 hmm, \"a handler AT 0x40 would make the first fault's breakpoint mask the second event\". Wait, the first fault's breakpoint is at 0x48 (OVF entry, since first fault = OVF and handler at 0x48). Hmm, actually re-read: the probe plants \"a breakpoint at the expected entry\". The expected entry for trapfrozen8 is 0x40. If the handler were planted at 0x40... then when the first fault (OVF) delivers to 0x48, the handler is at 0x48? No wait.\n\nHold on. Let me re-read the run_row logic: the probe plants handler words at 8*handler_at (default V_DIV0=0x40... wait no, 8*handler_at where handler_at is a VECTOR number. Default V_DIV0=8, so 8*8=0x40. For trapfrozen8, handler_at=V_OVF=9, so 8*9=0x48.) And plants a breakpoint at \"the expected entry\" = 8 * vector (the row's vector field). For trapfrozen8, vector = V_DIV0 = 8 -> entry 0x40.\n\nSo if handler_at were the default 0x40-entry (V_DIV0), the handler words (tb1 0,r3,8 then SELF) would be planted at 0x40 \u2014 which is ALSO the expected entry of the second event. The breakpoint at 0x40 would fire when the handler executes at 0x40 (before the trap even raises). Wait, but the breakpoint is planted and then \"continue with a 6s grace\" \u2014 the first breakpoint hit is presumably what's awaited. So the breakpoint at 0x40 would fire on the handler's first word (the tb1 at 0x40) \u2014 which happens BEFORE the second fault. Then the probe would see the breakpoint fire, machine... hmm, the tb1 raises the trap, machine stops? Actually the breakpoint fires at the handler's first instruction, and the probe would record pc=0x40 \u2014 indistinguishable from the second event delivering to 0x40. Actually it says \"would make the first fault's breakpoint mask the second event\". Hmm, \"the first fault's breakpoint\" \u2014 maybe the probe plants a breakpoint at the FIRST fault's expected entry too? No \u2014 from premise 5: \"The probe plants `br .` at every entry of every vector in its VECTORS tuple (both tables when VBR is relocated), the row's handler words at `8 * handler_at` (default DIV0 = 0x40), a breakpoint at the expected entry, then `continue` with a 6 s grace\".\n\nSo `br .` is planted at every entry of every vector in VECTORS \u2014 including 0x40 and 0x48. Then handler words overwrite at 8*handler_at. Then a breakpoint at the expected entry. Then continue.\n\nFor trapfrozen8: VECTORS includes V_OVF (9) and V_DIV0 (8), so `br .` at 0x40 and 0x48. Handler words overwrite 0x48 (handler_at=OVF): tb1 0,r3,8 at 0x48, then SELF (br . presumably, or a self-loop marker) at 0x4c. Breakpoint at 0x40 (expected entry). Continue: first fault OVF delivers to 0x48 -> handler tb1 executes -> trap names vector 8 -> with SFRZ set, trap delivered to 0x40 -> breakpoint at 0x40 fires. \n\nIf instead handler_at were V_DIV0 (0x40): handler words would overwrite the `br .` at 0x40 with tb1 0,r3,8. First fault OVF delivers to 0x48 -> `br .` at 0x48 -> machine halts spinning? Hmm, `br .` is an infinite loop \u2014 the machine would spin at 0x48. The breakpoint at 0x40 would never fire... wait no. Hmm wait, I need to think again.\n\nActually wait \u2014 the handler is the words planted at 8*handler_at for the FIRST fault. For trapfrozen (the original row): first fault = DIV0 (delivers to 0x40), handler planted at 0x40 = tb1 0,r3,7 -> trap names 7 -> delivered to 0x38 (expected entry, breakpoint at 0x38 fires). OK.\n\nFor trapfrozen8: first fault = OVF (delivers to 0x48). If handler planted at default 0x40, then after OVF delivery to 0x48, the machine hits `br .` at 0x48 (since VECTORS includes OVF, `br .` was planted at 0x48, and the handler was planted at 0x40 instead). So the machine spins at 0x48 forever \u2014 the second event never happens. That's why handler_at must be V_OVF: the handler must be planted at the FIRST fault's delivery entry so the handler actually runs. The default V_DIV0 works for every other two-fault row because their first fault is DIV0. trapfrozen8's first fault is OVF (because its frozen trap names DIV0 \u2014 to make the delivery observable, the handler must not be at DIV0's entry 0x40, since the second event delivers to 0x40; so first fault = OVF, handler at 0x48, trap names 8 -> delivers to 0x40 where the breakpoint is).\n\nHmm wait, but the header comment says \"only trapfrozen8 sets it, to OVF (0x48), because its frozen trap names DIV0 and a handler AT 0x40 would make the first fault's breakpoint mask the second event\". \"the first fault's breakpoint\" \u2014 hmm. Maybe the probe also plants a breakpoint at the first fault's entry? Not per premise 5. Let me reconsider: maybe \"the first fault's breakpoint\" is loose phrasing for \"the breakpoint at the (expected) entry [of the second event], which is the same address as the handler\". If the handler were at 0x40 and the expected entry were 0x40, the breakpoint at 0x40 would fire when the handler's first word executes \u2014 the tb1 at 0x40 \u2014 and the probe would record a hit, masking/confounding the second event's delivery. Either way (spin at 0x48, or breakpoint firing on the handler word at 0x40), the row would be broken. The phrase is slightly odd but the mechanism is coherent. Actually, hmm, \"would make the first fault's breakpoint mask the second event\" \u2014 wait, maybe I have it backwards: maybe the breakpoint is planted at the FIRST fault's expected entry? Let's check trapfrozen (original): row vector field = V_BOUNDS (7), entry 0x38. The first fault is DIV0 -> 0x40. The second event (trap naming 7) -> 0x38. The docstring says the row pins \"it delivers to 0x38\". So the expected/observed entry is 0x38, the second event's entry. The breakpoint must be at 0x38 (the row's vector field = V_BOUNDS). So the breakpoint is at the row's vector field's entry \u2014 the SECOND event's entry (for two-fault rows) or the single fault's entry (for one-fault rows). OK so for trapfrozen8, breakpoint at 0x40 (second event). If the handler were at 0x40 too, the breakpoint would fire on the handler's first word (tb1) \u2014 recording pc=0x40 \u2014 before the second event. But wait, would the handler even run? The handler is at 0x40; the first fault (OVF) delivers to 0x48, which has `br .` \u2014 spins. The handler at 0x40 never runs! So the machine would spin at 0x48 and the breakpoint at 0x40 would never fire \u2014 result STOP (grace expiry... well, actually `br .` spinning means the machine keeps running \u2014 \"ran past its grace without the entry breakpoint firing\" -> STOP). Hmm, so with handler_at = V_DIV0 for trapfrozen8, the row would be STOP even on the correct build. So handler_at=OVF is needed for the row to work at all. The header comment's \"mask\" phrasing is a bit off but the engineering is right.\n\nHmm wait, actually, maybe re-read again: \"a handler AT 0x40 would make the first fault's breakpoint mask the second event\". Maybe the probe plants a breakpoint at BOTH the first fault's entry and the expected entry? Premise 5 doesn't say that. It says \"a breakpoint at the expected entry\". OK \u2014 premise 5 is from the packet; the header comment in the diff says \"the first fault's breakpoint\". There's a slight tension but both are in-packet; the header comment might be sloppy. This is a minor wording issue, not a floor breach. Actually wait \u2014 hmm. Let me reconsider. Maybe for two-fault rows the \"expected entry\" is the first fault's entry and the classifier distinguishes... no. trapfrozen's EXPECTED result: the docstring says the row pins delivery to 0x38. The row's vector field is V_BOUNDS (7 -> 0x38). The gate pins \"six `trapfrozen*` rows DELIVER\". DELIVER = \"entry breakpoint fired, machine running, and every assert holds: pc == entry...\" \u2014 entry = 8*vector = 0x38 for trapfrozen. And the frozen-asserts list includes trapfrozen (shadows frozen at FIRST fault's values). So the breakpoint is at 0x38, the second event's entry. OK. The header comment's phrase \"the first fault's breakpoint\" is just wrong/sloppy wording \u2014 or refers to the fact that the handler planted at 0x40 would be hit... eh. Minor. Not worth a pass.\n\n**Now the main question: does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the shipped composite?**\n\nThe enumeration: \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`; the tail switch's labels), operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read, and joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them (class x SFRZ x is_trap; the fallback window's two bounds).\"\n\nSo I need to think about mutants that the composite (fix + probe v8) would fail to catch \u2014 i.e., a mutant that sweeps 36/36.\n\nLet me enumerate what's pinned by the 36 rows and what isn't.\n\nFirst, let me list the rows (as far as I can reconstruct):\n\nFrom the diff and prior passes (v5 had 29 rows; v8 has 36 \u2014 wait, the brief says \"probe v5 -> v8, 36 fault rows\" and \"BASE 36/36\"). v5 had 29 rows, v6 added resid127 (30), v7 added trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9 (35), v8 added usfu1 (36). Let me check: 29 + 1 = 30 (v6), + 5 = 35 (v7), + 1 = 36 (v8). Yes.\n\nThe rows visible in the diff's ROWS list (partial \u2014 the diff only shows context):\n\n- div0: DIVU_R2_R3_R0, r3=0x10, vector V_DIV0, fault_pc 0x10004, PSR_RESET, handler None.\n- ovf: ADD_R2_R3_R4, r3=0x7fffffff, r4=1, V_OVF, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, None.\n- (rows not shown: error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl, unimpl, priv, privtb, privsuptb, unimpl... \u2014 the diff shows \"privsuptb\" context, \"vbrtb\", \"trapfrozen\", new trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9, \"ovfc\", \"indclr\", \"umisalign\", ..., \"udiv0\", \"usertb\", \"resid50\", new \"resid127\", \"usfu1\", \"sfu1h\", \"live\".)\n- vbrtb: STCR_R5_CR7, TB1_0_R3_7, r3=1, r5=VBR_TEST, V_BOUNDS, 0x10008, PSR_RESET, None.\n- ovfc: STCR_R5_CR1, ADD_R2_R3_R4, r3=0x7fffffff, r4=1, r5=PSR_CARRY, V_OVF, 0x10008, PSR_CARRY, None.\n- indclr: STCR_R5_CR1, ADD_R2_R3_R4, r3=0x7fffffff, r4=1, r5=PSR_INDCLR, V_OVF, 0x10008, PSR_INDCLR, None.\n- umisalign: STCR_R5_CR1, LD_R2_R3_0, r3=DATA+1, r5=PSR_USER, V_MISALIGN, 0x10008, PSR_USER, None.\n- udiv0: STCR_R3_CR1, DIVU_R2_R3_R0, r3=PSR_USER, V_DIV0, 0x10008, PSR_USER, None.\n- usertb: TB1_0_R3_128, r3=1, V_USER0, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, None.\n- resid50: TB1_0_R3_50, r3=1, V_RESID, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, None.\n- resid127: TB1_0_R3_127, r3=1, V_RESID2, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, None.\n- usfu1: STCR_R5_CR1, DIVU_R6_R5_R4, r5=PSR_USERSFD1, r4=0x10, V_SFU1, 0x10008, PSR_USERSFD1, None.\n- sfu1h: DIVU_R2_R3_R0, r3=0x10, r5=0x60, r4=0x10, V_SFU1, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, [DIVU_R6_R5_R4].\n- live: DIVU_R2_R3_R4, r3=0x60, r4=0x10, None, None, None, None. (live = live row, no fault \u2014 wait, \"live\" has vector None. This is probably a control that the machine still runs \u2014 not a fault row? The brief says 36 fault rows... hmm, \"36 fault rows\" \u2014 maybe live is a fault row too: divu r2,r3,r4 with r3=0x60, r4=0x10 \u2014 a legal divide, no fault. Hmm, \"live\" \u2014 from table 8: \"BASE 36/36\". The count: v5=29, +resid127=30, +5 trapfrozen=35, +usfu1=36. So the ROWS tuple must have 36 entries total. Is \"live\" one of them? In v5 (29 rows): if live is included, then... I can't fully reconstruct. The brief says \"36 fault rows\" \u2014 perhaps loosely. OK.)\n\nAlso error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl (the {SFRZ, non-trap} x {class} rows from table 5), priv, privtb, unimpl, misalign (supervisor instruction rows), m88kexcsfu1 (negative control for delivering SFU1?), etc.\n\nKnown constants: V_MISALIGN=4, V_UNIMPL=5, V_PRIV=6, V_BOUNDS=7, V_DIV0=8, V_OVF=9, V_ERROR=10, V_SFU1=114, V_USER0=128, V_RESID=50, V_RESID2=127.\n\nWait \u2014 V_ERROR = 10? From \"class 10 is observationally identical under both arms (its own vector IS the re-route target)\" and the SFRZ re-route sends non-traps to ERROR. If ERROR = class 10, entry 0x50. \"a frozen trap naming ERROR lands on 0x50 whether exempted or re-routed (equivalent by construction)\". Yes.\n\nSo the fallback window: vectors < 128 && !implemented. implemented is set by switch 1 per class. Which classes are implemented? Presumably 0..10 (the classes with tail labels) \u2014 \"every class 0..10 has a label and 11..127 return at the fallback\". So switch 1 sets implemented=true for vectors 0..10 (when reached via the exception path \u2014 actually switch 1 is on class, setting implemented for classes 0..10), and SFU1 (114) and SFU2 (115)? Hmm: \"vectors 11..113 and 116..127 (fallback halts by\" \u2014 cut off. And SFU1 (114/115) \u2014 from the docstring: \"vectors 11..113/116..127 and SFU1 (114/115) still stop the machine\". Wait, but the fallback is `vector < 128 && !implemented`. If SFU1 (114) is NOT implemented (switch 1 doesn't mark it implemented), then 114 < 128 and !implemented -> fallback halts. And sfu1h's row expects HALT naming 0x072 (114). Yes: EXPECT_VECTOR sfu1h = 0x072. So SFU1 reaches the fallback and halts \u2014 by design (the fork chose to halt unimplemented vectors rather than deliver garbage). OK.\n\nSo the fallback window = {vectors v : 0 <= v < 128, v not in {0..10}}? Wait \u2014 vectors 0..3 have tail labels but are they \"implemented\"? \"vectors 0..3's tail arms (no row: M_TAILOFF -- filed)\". So 0..3 have tail labels but no rows. Classes 4..10 have rows. So implemented classes = 0..10. The fallback halts vectors 11..127 (including 114, 115).\n\nusertb: tb1 naming 128 -> USER_TRAPS_START, not < 128 -> goes to the common path, delivered to 0x400. Row expects DELIVER at 0x400. \"Pins the tail switch's `< USER_TRAPS_START' guard: `<=' sends 128 into the tail default and stops the cpu\". Hmm wait \u2014 if 128 goes to the common path, then the tail switch: `if (vector < USER_TRAPS_START) { switch(vector) ... default: internal error }`. Wait, premise 2: \"then the tail switch (labels per class; its `default:` prints `internal error` and stops the cpu -- reachable by NO vector below 128 at HEAD-with-fix, since every class 0..10 has a label and 11..127 return at the fallback\". Hmm, so the tail switch is guarded by `vector < USER_TRAPS_START` and has labels for classes... and a default. If 128 is sent into the tail switch by changing the guard to `<=`, then vector 128 hits the default -> TAILHALT. The M_TAILLE mutant: \"28/29 (usertb HALT)\" on v5 \u2014 i.e., with the tail guard `<=`, usertb (128) hits the tail default and halts -> RED. OK.\n\nWait, I need to re-examine: the tail switch's guard `vector < USER_TRAPS_START` \u2014 is that the fallback's guard or the tail's? The fallback is `if (vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented)` -> halt+return. Then SFRZ re-route, common path, then tail switch. The tail switch presumably: `if (vector < USER_TRAPS_START) switch (vector) { case 4: ... case 10: ... default: internal error; stop; }` \u2014 hmm, but what does the tail switch DO for user traps (>= 128)? The docstring: \"the tail switch (labels per class...)\". And usertb DELIVERs at 0x400 \u2014 so for vector 128, the tail switch does nothing (guard false) and the machine continues at the entry. OK.\n\nSo mutants in the (d) enumeration:\n\n1. Switch 1's class labels \u2014 changing a class's `implemented` marking. E.g., `implemented = !is_trap` on the PRIVILEGE arm (M_ISTRAP family) \u2014 killed by the *tb rows. All classes 4..10 have rows; classes 0..3 have no rows (disclosed, M_TAILOFF filed). So a mutant that marks class 2 as unimplemented (or whatever) sweeps \u2014 disclosed. Not a new finding.\n\n2. The fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`:\n   - Bound shrinks: M_FALLBACK127 (29/30 on v6, resid127 RED), M_FALLBACK51 (28/30). Bound grows: M_FALLBACKLE `<=` (usertb RED). `!implemented` dropped: M_FALLBACK_NO_BOUND (28/29, usertb? no wait \u2014 \"M_FALLBACK_NO_BOUND 28/29 (usertb)\" \u2014 dropping the bound means vector 128 also halts -> usertb RED. And M_DELIVERALL etc.\n   - MODE-conditioned: M_FBSUPONLY (killed by usfu1 now).\n   - What about a bound shrink to N in (0, 11)? E.g., `vector < 4`? Then all residual vectors (11..127) would NOT halt at the fallback \u2014 they'd proceed. resid50 (50) and resid127 (127) and sfu1h (114) would all be re-routed... wait, would they proceed to the SFRZ re-route and common path? For resid50: tb1 naming 50, SFRZ not set (PSR_RESET = 0x80000002, no SFRZ). So no re-route; common path: pc = VBR + 8*50 = 0x190; delivered to 0x190, which has `br .` planted (VECTORS includes V_RESID). So the machine spins at 0x190 \u2014 the breakpoint was at the expected entry... wait, what's the expected result for resid50? HALT (the fallback). The breakpoint is at the expected entry \u2014 for a HALT row, is a breakpoint planted? Premise 5: \"a breakpoint at the expected entry\". For HALT rows, the expected entry is the fallback's halt \u2014 there's no entry. Hmm. Let me think: for resid50, EXPECT_RESULT = HALT and EXPECT_VECTOR = 0x032 (the vector the diagnostic must NAME). The classifier for HALT: \"`unimplemented vector=` printed AND the machine stopped ... must also see ITS vector named in the diagnostic\". So for HALT rows, the classifier doesn't need a breakpoint \u2014 it greps the diagnostic. For a mutant that delivers resid50 to 0x190: the machine would hit `br .` at 0x190 and spin \u2014 \"STOP\" (anything else)? Actually \"STOP = anything else\". Hmm wait: STOP is \"the machine stopped for another reason\". If the machine spins at `br .`, it doesn't stop \u2014 it would run past grace. The classifier: \"res[\"result\"] = \"STOP\" # ran past its grace without the entry breakpoint firing\" \u2014 wait, from the diff: \n\n```\n     else:\n         want = dict(...)\n```\nand above it (context): `res[\"result\"] = \"STOP\"          # ran past its grace without the entry breakpoint firing`. Hmm, so STOP = grace expiry without breakpoint. And the docstring says \"STOP the machine stopped for another reason (e.g. a wrong vector base landed in zeros)\". OK, two mechanisms both STOP-ish.\n\nSo a bound shrink to N in (0,11): e.g., `vector < 8`. Then vector 114 (sfu1h/usfu1) \u2014 114 < 8 false -> not halted at fallback. Proceeds: SFRZ set? For sfu1h: the nested SFU1 is raised in the handler with SFRZ set (the first fault DIV0 set SFRZ). So SFU1 (114) with SFRZ set, is_trap=0 -> re-routed to ERROR (10) -> fatal()? Wait, \"if (PSR & SFRZ) { if (!is_trap) { vector = ERROR; fatal(...); } ...}\". So sfu1h's nested SFU1 under a shrunk bound < 114: re-routed to ERROR, fatal() \u2014 what does fatal() do? M_FALLBACK51: \"sfu1h STOP: 114 no longer halted at the fallback, re-routed to ERROR\". And in the docstring for usfu1: \"the mutant delivers to 0x390 and the #if 0'd SFU1 tail arm turns it into TAILHALT\". Hmm \u2014 that's for the M_FBSUPONLY mutant where the fallback predicate is `vector < 128 && !implemented && (PSR & MODE)`: for usfu1 (user mode), the fallback doesn't halt; SFU1 (114) proceeds... wait, SFRZ set? usfu1: r5=0x0000000a = IND|SFD1, MODE clear, SFRZ clear. So the SFRZ re-route doesn't fire; common path: pc = VBR + 8*114 = 0x390; then tail switch: vector 114 < 128 -> switch(114) -> no label for 114 (labels are per class 0..10) -> default: internal error, stop -> TAILHALT. Wait, but the docstring says \"the #if 0'd SFU1 tail arm falls to the default\" \u2014 i.e., there's an `#if 0` SFU1 arm in the tail switch, so under normal compilation 114 hits the default -> TAILHALT. Yes: \"usfu1 TAILHALT (delivered to 0x390, the #if 0'd SFU1 tail arm falls to the default)\". OK consistent with premise 2 (tail default reachable by NO vector below 128 at HEAD-with-fix... wait, that contradicts!).\n\nHold on. Premise 2: \"then the tail switch (labels per class; its `default:` prints `internal error` and stops the cpu -- reachable by NO vector below 128 at HEAD-with-fix, since every class 0..10 has a label and 11..127 return at the fallback).\"\n\nBut M_FBSUPONLY on v8: usfu1 delivered to 0x390 (vector 114, below 128) and hits the tail default -> TAILHALT. How? Because M_FBSUPONLY changes the fallback predicate to include `&& (PSR & MODE)` \u2014 so user-mode SFU1 (114) no longer returns at the fallback; it reaches the tail switch with vector 114 < 128 and hits the default. Right \u2014 \"at HEAD-with-fix\" the default is unreachable because the fallback catches all of 11..127. Under the mutant, the fallback's predicate is MODE-conditioned, so 114-in-user-mode escapes to the tail default. Consistent. Good.\n\nOK so now the (d) enumeration question: is there a clean-sweep mutant?\n\nLet me think about what the 36 rows pin, and look for a body-tested partition of `vector` or a predicate-dominating edit that no row distinguishes.\n\nKey areas:\n\nA. Switch 1 (class -> implemented). Classes 0..3 have no rows (disclosed: M_TAILOFF filed; 0..3 are hardware-event tails, m88kexchwtraps). So a mutant that, e.g., marks class 3 unimplemented would sweep \u2014 disclosed already. Not new.\n\nB. The fallback predicate `vector < USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented`:\n   - `vector < 128`: pinned interior (50), SFU1-interior (114 via sfu1h and usfu1), far bound (127), outside (128). Shrinks to any N in (114,128) -> resid127 RED (127 no longer < N for N in (115..127]? wait: shrink to `< 127` means 127 not halted -> resid127 RED. Shrink to `< 51`: 127 and 114 and 50... 50 < 51 still halted; 114, 127 not halted -> sfu1h and resid127 RED. Shrink to N in (11,50): resid50 RED (50 >= N... wait 50 < N for N in (50,114)? Let me redo: shrink to `< N` means vectors v with v >= N are no longer halted. resid50 (50): RED if N <= 50. sfu1h (114): RED if N <= 114. resid127 (127): RED if N <= 127. usfu1 (114): RED if N <= 114. So shrinks: N <= 50 -> resid50 RED; N in (50,114] -> sfu1h/usfu1 RED; N in (114,127] -> resid127 RED. All shrinks covered. Good.\n   - Grow: `<= 128` -> usertb RED (M_FALLBACKLE measured 28/29 on v5 with usertb RED \u2014 wait, table 6 says M_FALLBACKLE on v5: \"28/29 (usertb HALT)\". Hmm \u2014 \"usertb HALT\"? The gate pins say \"usertb's 128 outside the window is pinned by the existing `M_TAILLE`/`M_FALLBACKLE` shapes\". Wait, M_FALLBACKLE is `vector <= USER_TRAPS_START` on the FALLBACK \u2014 so 128 <= 128 -> fallback halts usertb -> usertb gets HALT instead of DELIVER -> RED. And M_TAILLE is the tail switch's guard `<=` -> 128 into tail default -> TAILHALT -> RED. Both kill usertb. OK.\n   - `!implemented` dropped (M_FALLBACK_NO_BOUND? no \u2014 \"M_FALLBACK_NO_BOUND 28/29 (usertb)\" \u2014 hmm, \"no bound\" means the predicate is just `!implemented`? Then vector 128 with !implemented... wait, is 128 \"implemented\"? Switch 1 sets implemented per class \u2014 128 is not a class, so implemented=false? Hmm, actually what does switch 1 do \u2014 \"switch 1 (class -> `implemented`)\". If the fallback drops the bound: `if (!implemented) halt` \u2014 then usertb (vector 128, implemented=false) would halt -> RED. And what about the *tb rows... those have implemented=true. OK so M_FALLBACK_NO_BOUND kills usertb. 28/29 \u2014 one red row (usertb).\n   - MODE-conditioned: killed by usfu1 (M_FBSUPONLY 35/36).\n   - What about conditioning on other PSR bits the body reads? The body reads PSR (for SFRZ, and writes MODE etc.). A mutant like `vector < 128 && !implemented && (PSR & SFD1)`? For resid50: PSR at the tb1 = PSR_RESET = 0x80000002 \u2014 SFD1 clear -> predicate false -> not halted -> resid50 delivered to 0x190... wait, but the (d) enumeration says \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\". A predicate mutant adding `&& (PSR & SFD1)`: which rows have SFD1 set when reaching the fallback? sfu1h (nested SFU1 with SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE set \u2014 SFD1 is set) and usfu1 (PSR_USERSFD1 = IND|SFD1 \u2014 SFD1 set). resid50/resid127: SFD1 clear. So `&& (PSR & SFD1)`: resid50 would NOT halt -> delivered to 0x190, spins at `br .` -> STOP? But wait \u2014 EXPECT_RESULT for resid50 is HALT; if it gets STOP, the row goes RED. So that mutant is killed by resid50. What about `&& !(PSR & SFD1)`: sfu1h/usfu1 would not halt -> sfu1h: nested SFU1 with SFRZ set, is_trap=0 -> re-routed to ERROR + fatal -> STOP (per M_FALLBACK51's sfu1h STOP) -> RED; usfu1: SFRZ clear -> common path -> 0x390 -> tail default -> TAILHALT -> RED. Killed.\n   - `&& (PSR & SFRZ)`: resid50/resid127 SFRZ clear -> not halted -> delivered -> RED. `&& !(PSR & SFRZ)`: sfu1h (SFRZ set at nested SFU1) -> not halted -> re-routed to ERROR -> RED; usfu1 SFRZ clear -> still halts... sfu1h RED anyway.\n   - `&& (PSR & IND)`: all rows have IND set (PSR_RESET = 0x80000002 = MODE|IND; PSR_USER = 0x00000002 = IND; PSR_USERSFD1 = 0x0000000a = IND|SFD1). So `&& (PSR & IND)` would sweep 36/36! Every row reaching the fallback has IND set. Hmm! Is that a valid (d) mutant? \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary ... operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\". The fallback's edited line is `vector < USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented` (the #502 diff added the fallback \u2014 the fallback IS the diff's edited line). A mutant `vector < 128 && !implemented && (PSR & M88K_PSR_IND)` \u2014 the body reads PSR (the common path writes PSR |= SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE, and the re-route reads PSR & SFRZ). So IND is a field the guarded body reads/writes. Hmm, but is it a field the body READS? The common path WRITES IND (PSR |= IND). The re-route READS SFRZ. Does anything read IND? Hmm \u2014 \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\". The fallback's guarded body (the halt diagnostic + return) reads vector (it prints vector=0x%03x). Hmm, the \"guarded body\" of the fallback predicate = the diagnostic + stop + return. That body reads `vector` only (prints it). The common path reads PSR (for EPSR and the |= ...). Hmm.\n\n   Actually, the enumeration is of mutant classes for the (d) DEPENDS-ON floor: \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`; the tail switch's labels), operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read, and joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them\".\n\n   So the predicate mutants are on: switch 1 labels, fallback predicate, re-route predicate, tail labels. The \"operand swaps\" (like swapping constants/fields) drawn from fields the guarded bodies read.\n\n   For the fallback predicate, a conjunct `&& (PSR & IND)`: would sweep because every row's PSR at fallback time has IND set. Is IND a \"field the guarded bodies read\"? The guarded body of the fallback = the diagnostic + `return`. It reads `vector`. Hmm \u2014 strictly, the fallback's body doesn't read PSR. But \"up to the function boundary\" \u2014 the function body after the fallback reads PSR. Hmm, the phrase \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\" \u2014 this constrains OPERAND SWAPS (e.g., swapping `PSR & SFRZ` to `PSR & IND` in the re-route \u2014 IND is a field the body... hmm).\n\n   Wait, let me reconsider. The (d) enumeration is a floor definition from earlier passes (this is a multi-pass review; (d) is one of the clause classes \u2014 clause (e) is mentioned as \"CONTRACT (clause e)\"). The floor question: \"Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE?\" So I need to find a mutant within that enumeration that sweeps 36/36, or conclude none and close with my own denominator.\n\n   Let me think harder about the re-route: `if (PSR & SFRZ) { if (!is_trap) { vector = ERROR; fatal(...); } update_shadow_regs = false; }`.\n\n   Mutants:\n   - `PSR & SFRZ` -> `PSR & <other bit>`: e.g., `PSR & MODE`. Which rows have SFRZ set at the re-route? The err* rows (handler keeps SFRZ), trapfrozen rows (SFRZ set from first fault), sfu1h (nested SFU1, SFRZ set). MODE at those moments: errpriv/errunimpl handlers drop to user mode keeping SFRZ (PSR_FRZUSER = 0x00000003 = IND|SFRZ, MODE clear!). So `PSR & MODE` for errpriv: MODE clear -> re-route not taken -> the second fault (non-trap) is NOT re-routed to ERROR -> delivered to its own vector -> errpriv expects DELIVER to ERROR's entry? Hmm wait \u2014 what do the err* rows expect? \"{SFRZ set, !is_trap} x {class}: MISALIGNED error; OVERFLOW errovf; DIV0 errdiv0 ... PRIVILEGE errpriv and UNIMPLEMENTED errunimpl\". These rows pin that a NON-TRAP exception with SFRZ set IS re-routed to ERROR. So the expected result for errpriv: re-routed to ERROR -> fatal()? Hmm wait \u2014 \"if (!is_trap) { vector = ERROR; fatal(...); }\" \u2014 fatal() then what? Does it deliver to ERROR's vector or just die? From M_SFRZ_EPSR_SWAP 23/29 and the error row... The \"error\" row: (\"error\", ...) is in the frozen-asserts list. Hmm.\n\n     Actually wait \u2014 let me re-read: \"the SFRZ re-route `if (PSR & SFRZ) { if (!is_trap) { vector = ERROR; fatal(...); } update_shadow_regs = false; }`\". So a non-trap with SFRZ set: vector = ERROR, fatal(...) [fatal prints a message and... continues? In many codebases fatal() exits]. Then the common path delivers to ERROR's entry (0x50)? The error row expects... hmm. The `error` row is in the frozen-assert name list: \"error\", \"trapfrozen\", \"errovf\", \"errdiv0\", \"errpriv\", \"errunimpl\" \u2014 \"the second fault runs with SFRZ set: the shadows stay FROZEN at the first fault's values\". So the error row: first fault = something, handler raises a second fault... Actually \"error\" might be: a first fault, handler does something that raises... hmm. Not fully reconstructible. But the err* rows expect DELIVER to 0x50 (ERROR's entry) with frozen shadows, presumably. The name \"error\" row: maybe the first fault's handler directly triggers... whatever.\n\n     So `PSR & SFRZ` -> `PSR & MODE`: errpriv/errunimpl run in user mode (MODE clear) with SFRZ set -> mutant skips the re-route -> second fault delivered to ITS OWN vector instead of ERROR -> RED (expected 0x50, got own vector). Killed. Also errdiv0/errovf/error run supervisor (MODE set) -> still re-routed. So killed by errpriv at least. \n     - `PSR & SFRZ` -> `PSR & IND`: every row has IND set at all times (PSR_RESET has IND; all rows keep IND) -> the re-route would fire for EVERY row (even without SFRZ) -> every non-trap fault (div0, ovf, etc. \u2014 first faults with IND set) re-routed to ERROR -> all DELIVER rows RED (div0 expects 0x40, gets 0x50). Killed massively.\n     - `PSR & SFRZ` -> `PSR & SFD1`: rows with SFD1 set... PSR_RESET doesn't have SFD1. First faults (div0, ovf, etc.) have SFD1 clear -> not re-routed (fine). After the first fault, PSR |= SFD1 \u2014 so handlers run with SFD1 set. err* rows: second fault with SFD1 set -> re-routed to ERROR \u2014 same as correct (SFRZ also set). trapfrozen rows: second fault is a TRAP with SFD1 set -> is_trap -> exempted \u2014 same. sfu1h: nested SFU1 with SFD1 set -> re-routed to ERROR \u2014 but correct behavior: SFRZ set, non-trap -> re-routed to ERROR too! Same. Hmm \u2014 so `PSR & SFRZ` -> `PSR & SFD1`: when does behavior differ? SFRZ set but SFD1 clear: PSR_FRZSUP = 0x80000003 = MODE|IND|SFRZ (SFD1 clear) \u2014 \"a frozen handler that wants its divu to raise DIV0, not SFU1\". Which rows use PSR_FRZSUP? From v5's rows... hmm, the diff doesn't show a row using PSR_FRZSUP directly, but constants suggest rows exist: errdiv0's handler \"clears SFD1 first\" (so the handler sets PSR to FRZSUP-ish then does divu -> DIV0). So errdiv0: handler clears SFD1, keeps SFRZ, does divu-by-0 -> DIV0 (8) with SFRZ set, SFD1 clear, non-trap -> re-routed to ERROR. Under `PSR & SFD1`: SFD1 clear -> no re-route -> delivered to DIV0's own entry 0x40 instead of 0x50 -> RED. Killed by errdiv0. And SFD1 set but SFRZ clear: usfu1 (PSR_USERSFD1 = IND|SFD1, SFRZ clear) -> SFU1 with SFD1 set, SFRZ clear: correct = no re-route (SFRZ clear) -> fallback halts (114 < 128, !implemented). Mutant `PSR & SFD1`: SFD1 set -> re-route fires -> vector = ERROR + fatal -> hmm, but wait: the fallback runs BEFORE the re-route. The fallback: 114 < 128 && !implemented -> halt + return. So usfu1 never reaches the re-route. OK. And sfu1h: nested SFU1: fallback first! sfu1h's nested SFU1 (114) halts at the fallback (\"it runs before the SFRZ re-route\"). Right \u2014 the fallback runs before the re-route, so sfu1h never reaches the re-route either. So the only rows reaching the re-route with SFRZ/SFD1 differences: err* rows (handlers keep SFRZ; errdiv0 clears SFD1). So `PSR & SFD1`: errdiv0 RED (SFD1 clear, SFRZ set -> expected re-route to ERROR, mutant doesn't). Killed. Good.\n     - `is_trap` dropped / `!is_trap` -> `false`/`true`: M_SFRZTRAP `if (true)` -> every trap re-routed -> trapfrozen* RED (measured 29/35). `if (false)` -> no non-trap re-routed -> err* rows RED. Killed.\n     - `update_shadow_regs = false` moved/dropped: M_UPDSHADOWTRAP, M_SFRZ_TRAP_UPDATE (28/29 by agy) \u2014 killed by trapfrozen rows (shadows must stay frozen).\n\nC. The tail switch's labels: per class. Mutants like dropping a label (M_TAIL_ERROR_DROP, M_TAIL_BOUNDS_DROP by agy: 23/29, 26/29 \u2014 killed by error/bounds rows... wait, on v5. On v8, presumably still killed). Tail bound: M_TAILLE killed by usertb. Tail return: M_TAILRETURN 2/23 killed. Tail labels for classes 4..10: each has a DELIVER row naming it. Class 0..3 labels: no rows (disclosed). SFU1 tail arm is #if 0'd \u2014 the default catches 114/115 under mutants that let them through (usfu1 TAILHALT).\n\nD. Joint cells: class x SFRZ x is_trap \u2014 all combos rowed (non-trap x class via err* + trap x class via trapfrozen*, class 10 disclosed, 0..3 disclosed). Fallback window's two bounds: 50, 114, 127, 128 \u2014 pinned.\n\nHmm. Now let me look for a clean sweep. Candidates:\n\n1. **The tail switch's `default:` behavior itself** \u2014 but that's not a predicate; and it's reachable only under mutants.\n\n2. **A predicate on the fallback using a PSR bit that all fallback-reaching rows share** \u2014 like IND. `vector < 128 && !implemented && (PSR & IND)`: every fallback-reaching row has IND set (resid50: PSR_RESET=MODE|IND; resid127: same; sfu1h: after first fault PSR|=SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE \u2014 IND set; usfu1: PSR_USERSFD1=IND|SFD1). So this conjunct sweeps 36/36. Is it in the (d) enumeration? \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary\" \u2014 the fallback predicate IS a diff-edited line, and adding a conjunct drawn from a field the guarded bodies read... The guarded body (the fallback's body) prints vector and returns \u2014 doesn't read PSR. Hmm. But \"up to the function boundary\" might mean the domination analysis goes up to the function boundary \u2014 i.e., any predicate on values the function reads. The function reads PSR (re-route) and writes PSR bits. Does the function READ IND? PSR |= SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE is a read-modify-write: it reads PSR (all bits, including IND) and writes. Hmm \u2014 \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\". An \"operand swap\" would be like swapping SFRZ for IND in `PSR & SFRZ`. For the fallback predicate, the operands are `vector`, `USER_TRAPS_START` (128), `implemented`. Operand swaps: `vector` -> something else? The body prints vector. Swapping `vector < USER_TRAPS_START` to `vector < something-else` \u2014 covered by bound mutants (M_FALLBACK127/51/LE). Swapping `!implemented` -> `implemented`? That would halt all implemented-class rows... div0 (implemented) -> halt -> RED massively.\n\n   Hmm, the (d) enumeration as stated: \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`; the tail switch's labels), operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read, and joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them (class x SFRZ x is_trap; the fallback window's two bounds).\"\n\n   So the predicate mutants are ON those four predicate sites. For the fallback predicate, the mutant space presumably includes conjuncts/disjuncts on fields read... The MODE conjunct (M_FBSUPONLY) was exactly this class: `&& (PSR & MODE)` \u2014 a conjunct on the fallback predicate drawn from a PSR bit. And it was a floor cell (kimi, pass 5). So the (d) enumeration DOES include PSR-bit conjuncts on the fallback predicate! The MODE conjunct swept v5 because all fallback rows were supervisor; usfu1 closed it. But now: are there OTHER PSR-bit conjuncts that still sweep v8?\n\n   PSR bits (from premise 1): MODE 0x80000000, SFD1 0x00000008, MXM 0x00000004, IND 0x00000002, SFRZ 0x00000001. Also C (carry) 0x10000000 (PSR_CARRY \u2014 from PSR_WANT ovfc: 0x9000000b = MODE|C|SFD1|IND|SFRZ; so C = 0x10000000).\n\n   Fallback-reaching rows (v8): resid50 (PSR_RESET = 0x80000002: MODE|IND; SFD1 clear, SFRZ clear, MXM clear, C clear), resid127 (same PSR_RESET), sfu1h (nested SFU1: PSR after first fault = 0x8000000b = MODE|SFRZ|IND|SFD1; C clear, MXM clear), usfu1 (PSR_USERSFD1 = 0x0000000a = IND|SFD1; MODE clear, SFRZ clear, MXM clear, C clear).\n\n   Conjunct analysis on the fallback predicate:\n   - `&& (PSR & MODE)`: usfu1 MODE clear -> escapes -> TAILHALT -> RED (M_FBSUPONLY, measured 35/36). Killed.\n   - `&& !(PSR & MODE)`: resid50/resid127/sfu1h have MODE set -> escape -> resid50: delivered to 0x190... wait, what happens: not halted at fallback; SFRZ clear (resid50) -> no re-route; common path: pc = VBR + 8*50 = 0x190; PSR |= SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE; shadows written; tail switch: vector 50 < 128 -> switch(50) -> no label -> default -> internal error + stop -> TAILHALT. resid50 expects HALT -> gets TAILHALT -> RED. Killed.\n   - `&& (PSR & SFD1)`: resid50 SFD1 clear -> escapes -> TAILHALT (as above) -> RED. Killed.\n   - `&& !(PSR & SFD1)`: sfu1h (SFD1 set at nested SFU1) -> escapes: SFRZ set, non-trap -> re-routed to ERROR + fatal -> sfu1h STOP (per M_FALLBACK51's sfu1h behavior) -> RED. Also usfu1 (SFD1 set) escapes: SFRZ clear -> common path -> 0x390 -> tail default -> TAILHALT -> RED. Killed.\n   - `&& (PSR & SFRZ)`: resid50 SFRZ clear -> escapes -> TAILHALT -> RED. Killed.\n   - `&& !(PSR & SFRZ)`: sfu1h (SFRZ set) escapes -> re-routed to ERROR -> STOP -> RED. Killed.\n   - `&& (PSR & IND)`: ALL FOUR fallback rows have IND set (0x80000002, 0x80000002, 0x8000000b, 0x0000000a \u2014 all have IND=0x2). So `&& (PSR & IND)` sweeps 36/36!! Every fallback-reaching row has IND set, so the conjunct never changes behavior on any row. And all DELIVER rows (which don't reach the fallback \u2014 wait, do any DELIVER rows pass through the fallback predicate? Yes \u2014 every row's exception evaluates the fallback predicate: `vector < 128 && !implemented && ...`. For DELIVER rows (div0, ovf, etc.): vector < 128 true, implemented true -> predicate false regardless of the IND conjunct. So no change. For usertb (vector 128): predicate false by the bound. So the IND conjunct only affects rows where vector < 128 && !implemented \u2014 exactly the four HALT rows \u2014 and all four have IND set. SWEEP.\n   \n   Wait \u2014 is IND set in every fallback-reaching row? Let me double check. PSR constants in the probe: PSR_RESET = 0x80000002 (MODE|IND). PSR_USER = 0x00000002 (IND only). PSR_FRZSUP = 0x80000003 (MODE|IND|SFRZ). PSR_FRZUSER = 0x00000003 (IND|SFRZ). PSR_USERSFD1 = 0x0000000a (IND|SFD1). PSR_CARRY = ? (from PSR_WANT ovfc = 0x9000000b: MODE(0x8)|C(0x1)|SFD1|IND|SFRZ = 0x9000000b. So PSR_CARRY = 0x10000000). PSR_INDCLR = ? (\"indclr wants the default PSR_EXC (0x8000000b): IND was CLEAR before the fault and must be SET by the exception path; EPSR keeps the pre-fault 0x80000000. A write that drops `| M88K_PSR_IND' leaves 0x80000009 -> bad=psr.\" So PSR_INDCLR = 0x80000000 (MODE only, IND clear). \n\n   So indclr: PSR = MODE, IND clear \u2014 but indclr's fault is OVF (implemented) -> never reaches the fallback. The four fallback rows (resid50, resid127, sfu1h, usfu1): resid50/resid127 use PSR_RESET (IND set); sfu1h's nested SFU1: the first fault (DIV0) went through the common path which set PSR |= SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE -> IND set; usfu1 uses PSR_USERSFD1 (IND set). YES \u2014 all four have IND set. \n\n   But wait \u2014 is the IND conjunct in the (d) enumeration? The enumeration says \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; ...)\". The fallback predicate is listed as an edited line; a conjunct mutant `vector < 128 && !implemented && (PSR & M88K_PSR_IND)` is a predicate ON that edited line, with an operand (PSR, IND) drawn from fields... \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\" \u2014 hmm, this clause is about OPERAND SWAPS, not conjuncts. But M_FBSUPONLY (`&& (PSR & MODE)`) was treated as a floor cell in this exact enumeration family (the fallback's MODE cell \u2014 kimi). The brief says: \"the fallback's MODE cell -- kimi, MEASURED in its own clone: `&& (PSR & MODE)` on the fallback predicate swept 29/29 because every fallback-reaching row was supervisor; one row (`usfu1`...) closes it.\" So the panel's own floor definition treats PSR-bit conjuncts on the fallback predicate as in-enumeration cells. The MODE cell was one; by the same rule, the IND cell exists and SWEEPS v8 (all four fallback rows have IND set). And also the MXM cell and the C cell:\n\n   - `&& (PSR & MXM)`: MXM = 0x00000004. Do any rows set MXM? None of the PSR constants include MXM (0x80000002, 0x00000002, 0x80000003, 0x00000003, 0x0000000a, 0x10000000-ish, 0x9000000b...). No row sets MXM. So `&& (PSR & MXM)` -> all four fallback rows have MXM clear -> predicate false -> no fallback halts -> resid50/resid127 TAILHALT (delivered into tail default... wait: resid50 not halted -> SFRZ clear -> common path -> 0x190? No wait: the tail switch. Hmm \u2014 if the fallback doesn't halt, the vector proceeds to the re-route (SFRZ clear, skip), common path (pc = VBR + 8*50 = 0x190, PSR |= ..., shadows written), tail switch: 50 < 128 -> switch(50) -> default -> internal error + stop -> TAILHALT. RED. Killed.\n   - `&& !(PSR & MXM)`: all rows MXM clear -> !MXM true always -> sweeps 36/36!! Same as IND-negated... wait no: `&& !(PSR & MXM)`: MXM clear in all four fallback rows -> !(PSR & MXM) = true -> predicate unchanged -> SWEEP. Yes!\n   - `&& !(PSR & IND)`: all four IND set -> !(IND) false -> no fallback halts -> resid50 TAILHALT -> RED. Killed.\n   - `&& (PSR & C)`: carry \u2014 resid50: C clear (PSR_RESET) -> escapes -> TAILHALT -> RED. Killed.\n   - `&& !(PSR & C)`: C clear in all four fallback rows (resid50/127: 0x80000002 no C; sfu1h: 0x8000000b no C; usfu1: 0x0000000a no C) -> !C true -> SWEEP 36/36!!\n\n   Hmm \u2014 so conjuncts on bits that are UNIFORMLY set (IND) or UNIFORMLY clear (MXM, C) across all fallback-reaching rows sweep. Is that a real floor breach or clause-(e) debt? The MODE cell precedent: MODE was uniform (set) across all fallback rows on v5, and the panel called it a floor cell ((d) DEPENDS-ON, presumably because the fallback's body/halt is part of the diff and the predicate is body-tested...). Hmm wait \u2014 actually why was MODE a (d) cell and not (e)? Premise 3: \"Because the fallback RETURNS, its predicate DOMINATES the tail labels the #502 diff added, and its constant 128 is a body-tested partition of `vector` -- which is why the far-bound shrink is a (d) floor cell and not clause-(e) debt (minimax read it the other way on pass 5).\"\n\n   So (d) vs (e): (d) cells are... hmm. In mutation-testing floor terms (from the structure of this review): clause (e) seems to be the CONTRACT's \"catches\" list \u2014 the disclosure contract. (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration is the mutant floor: predicates dominating edited lines, operand swaps, joint cells. The MODE conjunct was in-enumeration because... the fallback predicate is an edited line and PSR is a field read within the function (the re-route reads PSR). Hmm \u2014 \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\". The re-route's guarded body reads PSR & SFRZ. The common path reads/writes PSR. So PSR bits are in-scope operands for predicate edits within the function. The MODE cell was ruled a floor cell and closed with a row. By identical reasoning, IND/MXM/C conjunct cells are floor cells of the same shape \u2014 and they SWEEP v8.\n\n   Hmm wait, but hold on. Let me reconsider \u2014 maybe not. The MODE cell mattered because MODE is semantically meaningful for the fallback? No \u2014 the reason it was a floor cell: \"the fallback predicate dominates the tail labels the diff added\". Any conjunct on the fallback predicate that changes behavior for some reachable PSR state is a (d) cell; the probe's rows must cover the PSR states that reach the fallback such that every in-enumeration conjunct is killed. The MODE conjunct was killed by adding a row with MODE clear. The IND conjunct: is IND reachable-clear at the fallback? A row could be constructed: e.g., stcr to clear IND then tb1 naming 50? Wait \u2014 can IND be clear and still raise a trap? tb1 with PSR IND clear: hmm, IND (Interrupt Disable?) \u2014 clearing IND shouldn't prevent traps. A row: stcr r5,cr1 with r5 = 0x80000008 (MODE|SFD1? no \u2014 MODE with IND clear) then tb1 naming 50 -> reaches fallback with IND clear -> `&& (PSR & IND)` would not halt it -> TAILHALT -> RED. So the IND cell is closable with one row. Similarly C: a row with carry set at the fallback (stcr with C set, tb1 naming 50) -> `&& !(PSR & C)` RED. And MXM: MXM set \u2014 a row with MXM set reaching the fallback -> `&& !(PSR & MXM)` RED.\n\n   But the QUESTION is whether a clean-sweep mutant EXISTS in the enumeration of the shipped composite. If IND/MXM/C conjuncts are in-enumeration, then YES \u2014 `&& (PSR & M88K_PSR_IND)` sweeps 36/36, and the row that should have caught it is a fallback-reaching row with IND clear (e.g., \"resid50i\": stcr r5,cr1 with r5 = 0x80000000 (MODE, IND clear) then tb1 0,r3,50 -> HALT naming 0x032).\n\n   Hmm wait \u2014 but is a PSR-bit conjunct on the fallback predicate really \"DEPENDS-ON (d)\"? Let me think about the taxonomy. This panel's clauses: (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration \u2014 mutants that depend on the diff's edited lines; the floor = the set of mutants the probe must kill. \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary\" \u2014 a predicate \"dominates\" edited lines if changing it changes whether the edited lines execute. The fallback predicate dominates the tail labels (the diff added tail labels; the fallback's return means the tail labels only execute if the fallback doesn't return). So mutants on the fallback predicate are (d). The MODE conjunct was accepted as such (kimi's cell, closed by usfu1). So yes \u2014 the same shape with IND (or MXM, or C) is (d) and SWEEPS.\n\n   BUT \u2014 wait. I should double check the claim \"IND is a field the guarded bodies read\". The enumeration's constraint on operand swaps: \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\". This constraint is on OPERAND SWAPS, a separate bullet from \"predicates\". The predicates bullet lists the four predicate sites without an operand constraint. A conjunct `&& (PSR & IND)` on the fallback predicate is a predicate mutant on an edited line. The MODE precedent confirms the panel treats PSR conjuncts on the fallback as in-enumeration. So IND sweeps.\n\n   Hmm, but hold on \u2014 is the fallback predicate itself an \"edited line\" of the #502 diff? Yes: \"the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`\" is listed among \"the diff's edited lines\". And M_FBSUPONLY (`&& (PSR & MODE)`) was scored on the frozen v7/v8 instruments (table 8) \u2014 so the panel measures such mutants. And the brief's closure claim: \"the FALLBACK predicate itself is pinned in user mode by usfu1, the one user-mode route to it\" \u2014 the closure names MODE only. The IND/MXM/C conjunct cells are unpinned.\n\n   Now \u2014 before I commit to this as THE answer, let me consider whether the panel would consider IND/MXM/C conjuncts as \"joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them\". The enumeration's third bullet: \"joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them (class x SFRZ x is_trap; the fallback window's two bounds)\". The MODE cell was framed as \"{class} x {MODE} closure ... the FALLBACK predicate as well as the `implemented` arms\" \u2014 i.e., the panel's own closure writes \"{class} x {PSR.MODE}\" as a joint-cell family, and the fallback predicate is named in it. So the panel's floor includes PSR-bit x fallback-predicate cells. The shipped probe pins MODE on the fallback (usfu1) but NOT IND, MXM, or C. \n\n   So candidate answer: YES, a clean-sweep mutant exists: the fallback predicate conjunct on a PSR bit that is uniform across all fallback-reaching rows \u2014 IND (uniformly set), or the negations of uniformly-clear bits (MXM, C). The strongest, cleanest statement: `vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented && (PSR & M88K_PSR_IND)` \u2014 sweeps 36/36 because every fallback-reaching row (resid50, resid127, sfu1h, usfu1) runs with IND set. The row that should have caught it: a fallback row with IND clear \u2014 e.g., resid50 re-shaped with `stcr` writing 0x80000000 (MODE set, IND clear) before tb1 0,r3,50, expecting HALT naming 0x032 (the mutant would deliver it into the tail default -> TAILHALT).\n\n   Hmm wait \u2014 but wait. Would the mutant with IND-clear row even behave correctly on the SHIPPED code? The shipped fallback halts any vector < 128 && !implemented regardless of PSR \u2014 so yes, HALT. And the probe's PSR asserts for DELIVER rows: PSR == 0x8000000b \u2014 the exception path SETS IND (PSR |= IND). For a HALT row, no PSR assert. OK fine.\n\n   But hold on \u2014 is there a subtlety: can a tb1 execute with IND clear? IND = interrupt disable. Traps are not interrupts; tb1 should raise regardless. The packet says: \"a user-mode tb1 naming a vector < 128 raises PRIVILEGE\" (premise 4) \u2014 nothing about IND blocking traps. And indclr's row proves the exception path sets IND and that clearing IND pre-fault is testable (stcr with PSR_INDCLR = MODE only). So a supervisor tb1 with IND clear: the trap raises, reaches m88k_exception with vector 50, PSR & MODE set... fine. Plausible. But I can't verify from the packet that tb1 with IND clear raises the trap (it should \u2014 IND gates interrupts, not traps). Name it if needed.\n\n   Alternatively the MXM-negation conjunct `&& !(PSR & MXM)`: all rows MXM-clear -> sweeps; closing row: MXM set reaching the fallback (stcr with MXM, tb1 naming 50). And C-negation: `&& !(PSR & C)` sweeps (no fallback row has carry set); closing row: carry set at the fallback.\n\n   Actually \u2014 hmm, wait. Let me reconsider whether \"C\" is a field \"the guarded bodies read\". The common path reads/writes PSR wholesale; ovfc pins carry preservation through the exception path (PSR_WANT ovfc = 0x9000000b \u2014 C preserved). So the function reads C (it must, to preserve it). MXM: does the function read MXM? The common path does PSR |= SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE \u2014 preserves other bits including MXM by reading PSR. IND: same. So all are \"fields the body reads\" in the read-modify-write sense.\n\n   Hmm, but actually, hold on. Let me reconsider the (d) enumeration more carefully. \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary\" \u2014 the four listed predicate sites. For the fallback, the enumeration presumably means mutants OF that predicate \u2014 including conjuncts like M_FBSUPONLY. The MODE precedent (a conjunct) was ruled in. So IND/MXM/C conjuncts are in. At least one sweeps. \n\n   But wait \u2014 is there an even more standard answer expected here? Let me re-scan the brief for hints. \"the {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (same re-route path as errpriv/errunimpl; MODE untested by the function -- clause (e))\" \u2014 disclosed as clause (e) because MODE is UNTESTED BY THE FUNCTION (the re-route doesn't read MODE; the common path writes MODE). So the panel's (d)/(e) line for PSR bits: a bit is in-(d) for a predicate if the function's code READS that bit somewhere relevant... Hmm \u2014 \"MODE untested by the function\" \u2014 so for the re-route predicate, a MODE conjunct is (e) because the function never tests MODE. But M_FBSUPONLY (MODE conjunct on the FALLBACK) was (d) \u2014 a floor cell. Why the difference? Hmm.\n\n   Wait, let me re-read the disclosed list: \"the {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (same re-route path as errpriv/errunimpl; MODE untested by the function -- clause (e))\". So the triple {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 \u2014 i.e., a frozen non-trap in USER mode for those classes: e.g., a handler that drops to user mode keeping SFRZ, then a misaligned access -> re-routed to ERROR. The claim: this is (e) debt because the function doesn't test MODE on that path (the re-route tests SFRZ and is_trap only; MODE doesn't change the behavior). I.e., the mutant space for the re-route predicate is conjuncts on bits the re-route-relevant code reads: SFRZ (read), is_trap (read). A MODE conjunct on the RE-ROUTE would be (e) debt because... hmm, no wait. \"MODE untested by the function\" \u2014 the triple cell {user-mode frozen non-trap x class} is equivalent-by-construction to the rowed {supervisor frozen non-trap x class} cell BECAUSE the function never reads MODE on that path. So it's disclosure (e), not a floor cell.\n\n   By contrast, the FALLBACK predicate with a MODE conjunct: the function doesn't read MODE at the fallback either! But the panel ruled M_FBSUPONLY a floor cell ((d)). Why? Because the fallback predicate is itself an EDITED LINE of the diff \u2014 the diff ADDED the fallback. The (d) enumeration covers predicates on the diff's edited lines, whatever operands they read... hmm, but then \"MODE untested by the function\" would also apply to the fallback MODE conjunct... \n\n   Hmm wait, no. Let me re-read premise 3: \"Because the fallback RETURNS, its predicate DOMINATES the tail labels the #502 diff added, and its constant 128 is a body-tested partition of `vector` -- which is why the far-bound shrink is a (d) floor cell and not clause-(e) debt\". So the (d)/(e) distinction for the FALLBOUND: the bound shrink is (d) because the predicate dominates the tail labels (the diff's added lines) \u2014 changing the bound changes which rows reach the tail labels. And the constant 128 is body-tested (the tail switch's guard tests `vector < USER_TRAPS_START` \u2014 the body tests the same constant). So (d) = mutants whose change is distinguishable by the diff's own added code paths... \n\n   And the MODE cell (M_FBSUPONLY): the brief says kimi found it and v8 row usfu1 closes it \u2014 and the question's disposition list calls it \"three class closures incl. the fallback's MODE cell\" \u2014 a CLASS closure, i.e., a floor cell of the {predicate} x {PSR bit} class... The brief says: \"Second, disclosure-adjacent, measured 29/29: M_FBSUPONLY -- the fallback predicate conditioned on MODE; every fallback-reaching row was supervisor; one row closes it\". \"Disclosure-ADJACENT\" \u2014 hmm! So kimi's M_FBSUPONLY was \"disclosure-adjacent\" \u2014 was it (d) or (e)? The repair added usfu1 regardless. And the CONTRACT now says: \"the FALLBACK predicate itself is pinned in user mode by usfu1, the one user-mode route to it\" \u2014 written into the closure. And the denominator claims M_FBSUPONLY 35/36 as a killed mutant.\n\n   OK so regardless of the (d)/(e) taxonomy, the panel's practice: PSR-bit conjuncts on the fallback predicate are measured and rowed when they sweep. The MODE one swept on v5 and was closed. The IND/MXM/C ones sweep on v8 by the same test. If the panel calls those (e) debt (\"IND untested by the function\"? no wait\u2014) hmm.\n\n   Hmm, hold on. Let me think about whether IND conjunct on the fallback is distinguishable-by-construction (\"the body's own tests distinguish them\"). The enumeration: \"joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them (class x SFRZ x is_trap; the fallback window's two bounds)\". So joint cells are included ONLY WHERE the body's own tests distinguish them. For the fallback predicate x PSR-bit conjuncts: the body (the exception function) does not test IND or MODE at the fallback... so under this reading, fallback-predicate x {MODE, IND, MXM, C} conjuncts are NOT in the (d) enumeration \u2014 they'd be (e) debt! But then why was M_FBSUPONLY (MODE) treated as a floor cell needing a row?? \n\n   Ugh. Let me re-read the pass-5 account: \"the fallback's MODE cell -- kimi, MEASURED in its own clone: `&& (PSR & MODE)` on the fallback predicate swept 29/29 because every fallback-reaching row was supervisor; one row (`usfu1`...) closes it.\" And the closure section: \"the fallback window: resid50 (interior), sfu1h (114, interior via the nested SFU1), resid127 (the far bound), usertb (128, outside).\" \u2014 the window closure does NOT mention MODE. But the CONTRACT diff adds: \"the FALLBACK predicate itself is pinned in user mode by usfu1, the one user-mode route to it\". So the panel DID treat the fallback's MODE conjunct as in-scope \u2014 as part of the {class} x {MODE} closure (\"the {class} x {MODE} closure now names the FALLBACK predicate as well as the `implemented` arms\").\n\n   So the panel's floor includes {fallback predicate} x {MODE} as a cell of the {class} x {MODE} family. The {class} x {MODE} family's rationale: \"a MODE-conditioned `implemented' on any class that user mode can raise\" \u2014 the `implemented` arms are switch-1 labels (edited lines), and a MODE conjunct on them is in-(d)... The `implemented` arm conjunct `implemented = !is_trap || (PSR & MODE)`-style... hmm.\n\n   OK. I think the cleanest reading: the (d) enumeration, as the panel operates it, includes conjuncts of PSR bits (fields the function reads: the function reads PSR wholesale in the re-route and the |= writes) on the diff's edited predicates (switch-1 arms, fallback predicate, re-route predicate, tail labels). MODE on the fallback was one such cell; it swept v5; usfu1 closed it. The remaining PSR-bit conjuncts on the fallback: IND (uniform set -> positive conjunct sweeps), MXM and C (uniform clear -> negated conjuncts sweep). These sweep v8. \n\n   Hmm, but hmm, wait. Would the panel consider IND-conjunct as distinguishable? \"joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them\" \u2014 the body's own tests: does any body test distinguish IND set/clear on the fallback path? The common path tests nothing about IND (just |=). The EPSR write: \"EPSR only when unfrozen\" \u2014 EPSR = pre-fault PSR, which for an IND-clear row would be 0x80000000 vs 0x80000002. But HALT rows don't assert EPSR. So no body test distinguishes IND at the fallback. Under the strict reading of the enumeration, IND conjunct is NOT a (d) cell (the body doesn't distinguish it) \u2014 same reason the {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple is (e) (\"MODE untested by the function\").\n\n   But then M_FBSUPONLY (MODE on the fallback) should ALSO be (e) by that logic \u2014 \"MODE untested by the function\" applies equally! Yet the panel treated it as a floor cell (\"the fallback's MODE cell\", \"one row closes it\", written into the contract, counted in the denominator 35/36). Hmm, unless... the MODE cell was (d) for a different reason: the fallback's body \u2014 the diagnostic + return + the STOP \u2014 no MODE test. Hmm.\n\n   Wait, actually \u2014 maybe the distinction is: the {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple is about the RE-ROUTE path (the function's behavior is provably identical for MODE set/clear because the re-route reads only SFRZ/is_trap \u2014 \"same re-route path as errpriv/errunimpl; MODE untested by the function\"). Whereas the fallback predicate is an edited line whose semantic contract is \"halt unimplemented vectors\" \u2014 and the probe's own contract claims to pin the fallback predicate as a predicate (not per-PSR-state). A MODE conjunct changes the predicate's truth for reachable states (user-mode fallback-reaching rows exist: usfu1), so it's a floor cell. An IND conjunct changes the predicate's truth for reachable states too (IND-clear fallback-reaching rows are constructible: stcr IND-clear then tb1). So IND is a floor cell of the same class as MODE \u2014 and it's OPEN (sweeps).\n\n   Alternatively \u2014 maybe the panel would say IND-clear-at-the-fallback is not reachable... no, it's reachable (stcr can write any PSR value; indclr proves IND-clear pre-fault states are planted).\n\n   Hmm OK wait, but let me reconsider. Maybe the intended answer is something else entirely. The question: \"Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE (the #502 fix + probe v8)?\" with the enumeration spelled out. And the second question: \"of the pass-6 dispositions ..., WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 there are 10-ish dispositions listed and exactly one is wrong. Let me audit the dispositions:\n\n   Pass-6 dispositions (from the question): \n   1. three class closures incl. the fallback's MODE cell (fallback far bound resid127; frozen traps per class trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9; fallback MODE usfu1)\n   2. one harness extension (handler_at)\n   3. one disclosed equivalent (class 10)\n   4. one interval correction ((114,128) not (50,128))\n   5. one driver-listing note (DRIVER_RED_LISTING_NOTE)\n   6. one PRETABLE-order disclosure\n   7. one count correction (seven -> five)\n   8. three grok records\n\n   Which is wrong? Let me audit each:\n\n   1. **The three class closures**: \n      a. Far bound: resid127 pins `vector < 128`; measured M_FALLBACK127 29/30 on v6 (resid127 TAILHALT). Sound.\n      b. Frozen traps per class: trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9 close {SFRZ, is_trap} x {class} for classes 4,5,6,8,9; class 7 rowed (trapfrozen); class 10 disclosed equivalent; 0..3 disclosed. Table 7 measured: each class mutant killed by exactly its own row. Sound. BUT \u2014 wait, check trapfrozen8's expected vector. trapfrozen8: first fault OVF (class 9, entry 0x48), handler tb1 0,r3,8 (trap naming vector 8 = DIV0), expected entry 0x40 (V_DIV0). The mutant M_SFRZTRAP8 (ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE): `if (!is_trap || vector == M88K_EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE)` \u2014 for the frozen trap naming 8: is_trap=1 but vector == DIV0 -> re-routed to ERROR (10) -> delivered to 0x50 instead of 0x40 -> RED (trapfrozen8 STOP? table says \"trapfrozen8 STOP\" \u2014 hmm, STOP not a pc mismatch? The breakpoint is at 0x40; the mutant delivers to 0x50 -> breakpoint never fires -> grace expiry -> STOP. Yes: \"trapfrozen8 STOP\". Consistent.)\n\n      Hmm wait, actually \u2014 there's something to check in trapfrozen8's row: the regs are {\"r3\": 0x7fffffff, \"r4\": 1} and the handler is TB1_0_R3_8 \u2014 tb1 0,r3,8 uses r3 as the trap vector operand? Let me check the encoding: TB1_0_R3_7 = \"tb1 0,r3,7\" \u2014 the encoding 0xf003d807: tb1 with immediate 7? From the comment \"tb1 0,r3,7 (r3=0x11)\" for trapfrozen \u2014 so tb1 0,r3,7 traps to vector 7 if r3's bit... no wait. MC88100 tb1: \"tb1 r3,r1,imm\" \u2014 traps if bit imm of r3 is set? Actually tcnd/tb0/tb1: tb1 rD,rS1,imm \u2014 trap if the bit (imm selects which... hmm. MC88100 tb0/tb1: \"tb1.r D,S1,W5\" \u2014 trap always if W5 = 0? Hmm: tcnd with condition 0 always traps. tb1 with W5 selecting a bit of S1: tb1 0,r3,7 = trap if bit 7 of r3 is set. For trapfrozen, r3=0x11 = 0b10001 \u2014 bit 7 not set?? Hmm wait. Hmm \u2014 0x11 = 17 = 0b10001. Bit 7 = 0x80 \u2014 not set. So tb1 0,r3,7 with r3=0x11 wouldn't trap?!\n\n      Hold on. Let me recheck MC88100 semantics. tb0/tb1: \"tb1 D, S1, W5\" \u2014 trap if bit W5 of S1 is 1 (tb1) or 0 (tb0)? And the trap vector... Hmm, actually no \u2014 in MC88100, tb0/tb1 trap to a FIXED vector (the trap vector, 0x400+n?) \u2014 no wait. Hmm. Let me think about the 88100 trap instructions: tcnd tcnd<cond> \u2014 traps to vector 0x400 + ... no. MC88100 traps: t{b0,b1,cnd} trap to vector... The MC88100 trap vectors are 112..127 (user traps) \u2014 \"tb0/trap\" hmm.\n\n      From the probe: TB1_0_R3_128 = 0xf003d880 = \"tb1 0,r3,128 -- the first USER trap vector\". TB1_0_R3_50 = 0xf003d832 = \"tb1 0,r3,50\". So tb1 0,r3,N traps to vector N?? And r3 = 0x11 for trapfrozen rows / r3=1 for usertb/resid rows. So tb1 0,r3,W5: W5 is the trap VECTOR number (0..127 for tb1 \u2014 \"a user tb1 naming < 128 raises PRIVILEGE\", and 128+ are user traps). And r3 is the VALUE whose bit W5... no wait \u2014 W5 is 5 bits (0..31)? 128 doesn't fit in 5 bits. Hmm, the encoding 0xf003d880: bits [4:0] = 0x00? Let me decode: 0xf003d880 = 1111 0000 0000 0011 1101 1000 1000 0000. Hmm. tb1 encoding: 1111 0000 0000 0000 ... Actually MC88100 tb0/tb1: \"1111 0000 000S SSSS 0W5W5W5W5W5 0D0D0D0D\"? I don't remember exactly. But the packet's own gloss is authoritative: \"tb1 0,r3,128 (f003d880)\" \u2014 the immediate is the vector; and \"tb1 0,r3,7 (r3=0x11)\" \u2014 traps to vector 7 when bit 7 of r3 is... 0x11 bit 7 clear! Hmm \u2014 unless tb1 traps when the bit is CLEAR (tb1 = trap on bit one \u2014 no, tb1 = trap if bit is 1).\n\n      Hmm wait, actually maybe I have it backwards: tb0 = trap if bit is 0; tb1 = trap if bit is 1. trapfrozen has r3=0x11 and tb1 0,r3,7 \u2014 bit 7 of 0x11 (=0b0010001) is 0. So no trap?? But the row expects the trap to fire. Hmm. Unless r3=0x11's bit 4 and bit 0 are set \u2014 bit 4 (0x10) and bit 0 (0x1). tb1 0,r3,4 would trap (bit 4 set). But the row is TB1_0_R3_7. Hmm!\n\n      Wait wait. Let me re-read: \"trapfrozen div0 whose handler at 0x40 is tb1 0,r3,7 (r3=0x11)\". Hmm, and vbrtb: (\"vbrtb\", [STCR_R5_CR7, TB1_0_R3_7], {\"r3\": 1, \"r5\": VBR_TEST}, V_BOUNDS, ...) \u2014 r3=1, tb1 0,r3,7: bit 7 of 1 is 0! Hmm \u2014 so tb1 0,r3,7 with r3=1 doesn't trap on bit-7... unless tb1's semantics differ.\n\n      Hmm, hold on \u2014 maybe MC88100 tb1 is: trap if the SELECTED BIT matches? Let me look at the encoding: 0xf003d807 for tb1 0,r3,7? The listed: TB1_0_R3_128 = 0xf003d880, TB1_0_R3_50 = 0xf003d832, TB1_0_R3_127 = 0xf003d87f, and privsuptb: \"tb1 0,r3,6 (f003d806)\".