m the guest's written bytes (so `data[0]` = the byte written). For a READ, the memcpy fills data from the array (serving). On the end-span path (offset + len beyond array?), zero-filled.\n\nActually, hold on. If the memcpy fills `data` for BOTH reads and writes \u2014 for a read it copies FROM the array TO data (serving), for a write it copies FROM the guest's incoming data TO data (or directly to the array?). But the fix says writes to the default arm are DECLINED, not stored. So the memcpy for writes must NOT store to the array \u2014 the store to the array presumably happens in the cased arms (each cased arm does the store) or after the switch (but the default arm breaks out without storing). Hmm.\n\nActually the original GXemul pattern for devices is:\n\n```c\nDEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2)\n{\n    struct pcc2_data *d = extra;\n    uint64_t d0 = 0, d1 = 0, ...;\n    ...\n    /*  Read from/write to the device:  */\n    if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {\n        /*  store into local d0..d3 from data  */\n    } else {\n        /*  load into data from d0..d3  */\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut this fork clearly has a memcpy above the switch and a switch on relative_addr with a default arm. The packet describes it. I can't verify. I'll name the premise.\n\nOK here's the crux for Question 1: **\"which accepted disposition above is wrong, by number?\"**\n\nThe brief explicitly hints there's a wrong accepted disposition among #1\u2013#10. Let me scrutinize each:\n\n1. M_O1 \u2014 read announce value unpinned; row `srvd val text`; product prints data[0]. Seems fine.\n\n2. M_O2 \u2014 disposition on latched path unpinned; row `sameoff val`. Fine.\n\n3. grok A \u2014 latch SET conditioned on writes; rows `latch rd rd`, `latch rd wr`. Fine.\n\n4. grok B \u2014 `len != 1` re-kill; rows `wide wr`, `wide rd`. Note the packet says \"The pass-1 PRETABLE's own 'byte-and-wide' sentence had no row \u2014 a CLAIM->ROW miss\". Fine.\n\n5. codex \u2014 `|=` -> `=`; row `latch aba`. Fine.\n\n6. fable5 \u2014 r