.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\closing2\fable5_close2.md:8:- *Floor breach: no.* The PORT arm's guarded body tests three things — the read/write flag, `idata >= 8`, and `!warned_port`. It never tests `d->port[COMMAND]`, any data cell, or `current_port`. Under the joint-cell ruling (DEPENDS-ON enumerates cells only where the body's own code distinguishes them), edits conditioned on COMMAND, COLOR_B/Y1/COLOR_R, or a selector value fall outside the (d) closure: clause-(e) coverage debt. The announce-value forms (kimi-k3's `!= 16`, minimax's `< K`) sit inside `idata >= 8`, a range the body does not subdivide — also (e), as both seats concede. Table 4 holds the genuine floor axes: `idata` magnitude, width and class (M_gt 87, M_i32 87, M_clamp 69, M_store 66, the width targets), the latch (M_reflatch/latchcnt/latchinv 78), read/write (E_rd_* red). So what is wrong is the CLAIM — the preamble's "every value of every axis is held" — an L4 wrong record, exactly as the author says.
