.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\grok_dedicated_f2_v6.txt:7:**Against the floor reading.** The dedicated job treated `warned_port == 0` as a floor cell whose generating set is incomplete, because only bits 3, 8, and 32 are decisive on a first refusal, and it owed 58 fresh devices. That reading uses the wrong clause and a repair that does not pass on the fix.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\grok_dedicated_f2_v6.txt:9:The carrier's 2026-09-04 rule, which the brief quotes in shortened form, has three bounds the floor reading drops. The floor is the cross-product of the guard's dominating predicates with the device-state lvalues the repair path reads, and only where that pair is jointly reachable. The bits of the value being written are single-operand marginals, not a floor axis. A joint is owed only when the guarded body's own code distinguishes it; a value the body never tests as a case (`idata == k`, or one bit of a threshold) is clause (e). The generating-set sentence — at each floor cell, every idata bit the body tests, written both clear and set — was added for a body that already masks a bit (`idata & ICLR` at TxICR). It is what makes that bit × state joint L2. It does not turn every threshold-sensitive bit into a tested bit.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\grok_dedicated_f2_v6.txt:94:What the panel did not catch: the floor repair is not merely too big, it fails the fixed arm for every bit an `sw` cannot carry; "every B value" is false for bits 3, 4, and 8; and the duplicate `BR_ge9`/`M_gt` binary makes the "six behaviour changers" sentence false in the same way a correction was false on 2026-09-04, by being one comparison away from the log.
.\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\p2_8bd97e4_20260918\panel_20260918_180558\ollama_kimi.txt:34:I apply the **joint-cells letter, read through observable distinction** — "the body's own code distinguishes them" must mean *the shipped code's guest-observable behavior varies over the partition*, not "the body contains an `if` testing it". Consequences:
.\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\p2_8bd97e4_20260918\panel_20260918_180558\ollama_kimi.txt:36:- **pass 6's "diverges only outside the probed set ⇒ L2" does not govern as a general rule.** It keys breach-ness to an accident of the probe's domain, and if applied uniformly it empties clause (e): *every* disclosed cell is by definition "outside the probed set," so disclosure could never discharge anything. Clause (e) was textually adopted; it must carve a real exception. (Pass 6's *disposition* was still fine — the row it demanded was cheap and, per below, the width space turned out to contain real joint cells.)
.\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\c4_KILLED_20260914\kimi.txt:125:  But hold on — let me consider whether the flagship's option-(c) ruling was specifically "row every OBSERVABLE cell of the class" for x_wfallthru, and RD2 was treated as a separate, single-row addition. The brief says repair 4 adds "4j `iirr' (an lca READ of IIR -- RD2)". Singular. The contract text says RD2 is "rowed since repair 4 at IIR, 4j `iirr'". If the panel reads that sentence as scoping RD2 to the single rowed cell, then the contract is accurate and my GAP is "outside the contract as written" — but then Q2's floor question still stands: "Is there a floor gap in the 111-row detector? The smallest source edit to the FIXED dev_lca.c that changes a guest-visible outcome and still scores 111/111". A floor gap doesn't require the contract to name it — the floor is about dominating predicates... The width-gated RD2 is exactly analogous to W1/W2/W4 which the measuring seat ruled a contract breach and the flagship ruled worthy of class closure. The class as the flagship defined it — "every OBSERVABLE cell" of the phantom-read/forward-count class — includes the read arm's duplicated forward at lane 2 widths 2,4,8. Those cells are observable (armed IIR). They are not rowed. So either the enumeration is incomplete (Q1: yes — the forward-count class is closed on the write arm but not the read arm; read-arm width-gated duplication escapes) or the contract must say RD2 is caught only at (1,2) and DISCLOSED at (2,2),(4,2),(8,2).
.\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\c4_KILLED_20260914\kimi.txt:214:  Hmm, wait — but actually, let me reconsider whether the flagship's ruling already considered and accepted this. The brief says: repair 4 adds 4j "(an lca READ of IIR -- RD2)" and the contract entry says "outside the contract as first written, rowed since repair 4 at IIR, 4j `iirr'". Maybe the flagship deliberately rowed RD2 minimally because RD2 was OUTSIDE the contract as first written — a bonus row, not a class closure. Under that reading, a width-gated RD2 is outside the contract (RD2 as a whole is outside the contract as first written; the 4j row is courtesy). But Q2 asks about the FLOOR, not only the contract: "Is there a floor gap in the 111-row detector? The smallest source edit to the FIXED dev_lca.c that changes a guest-visible outcome and still scores 111/111". The floor (pass-11 definition referenced: "the guard's dominating predicates x the device-state lvalues the repair path reads") — under grok's pass-3 reading, the fallthrough channel wasn't a floor breach because the body tests neither len nor lane and visibility is the forwarded device's property. The same logic would apply to gated RD2: the read body doesn't test len; visibility is the device's property. So under the FLOOR-as-defined, gated RD2 might NOT be a floor gap — but W1-W4 weren't either under that reading, and the panel/flagship nonetheless ruled them a contract breach requiring class closure. The verdict format the brief demands: "`CLOSES` (no floor gap in the 111-row detector) or `GAP: <exact source edit ...>`". The GAP format parenthetical is exactly Q2's question. Given the measuring seat's pass-3 verdict was GAP on precisely this shape (named edit + conjunct), and the chain rule "the chain closes on the first valid pass with zero floor breaches surviving adjudication", consistency demands: if W1 (`if (len != 8) return 1` after bare forward) was a GAP at pass 3, then RD2-len!=1 is a GAP at pass 4 — same channel, same gating shape, other arm, measured-equivalent reasoning. The repair rowed the write arm exhaustively but the read arm minimally; the class closure is asymmetric and incomplete.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\fable5_f2_v6.md:12:The rule as written has three bounds, and the floor reading drops all three. The floor is the guard's dominating predicates crossed with the device-state lvalues the repair path reads, where jointly reachable; "the bits of the single value being written are single-operand MARGINALS, not floor"; and the generating-set sentence adds, at each floor cell, "every idata bit the guarded body TESTS" — minted on `idata & ICLR`, a bit the body MASKS. This arm tests `writeflag`, `idata >= 8` and `!warned_port`. Its floor is {write} × {below, at, past 8} × {latch clear, set}: six cells, all held (A's accepts; 8 first-refused in P, W, B; 9, 0x100, 0x100000005, all-ones first-refused in F, V, X, Y; the set-latch cells by every later refusal). No bit is masked; #548 ruled that a relational compare's generating set is its three regions. Latch-clear × bit k is therefore a two-axis joint of a floor cell with a marginal — the class (0) already discloses in general terms.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\fable5_f2_v6.md:14:**Being hardest on my own rulings.** The Opus seat's challenge is the strongest thing in the packet: for bits 33–63 the escape is not the kept tripwire but silent acceptance of selector 0 — #549's own truncation defect, measured (`WITNESS.out`: a first `sd 2^40` selects 0; the next DATA write runs as command 42). Does #548 survive it? It does, because #548's P_win consequence was ALSO the round's own defect (an unbounded window of the pending count), and the ruling weighed exactly that and refused to grade by consequence, naming its price: M_zge, M_neqB and M_upskip become L2 too. Reading "TESTS" as "depends on" here would make the floor {latch} × {61 bits}, the 2^k growth the bound was written against, and would reopen #548. What the consequence DOES change is the disclosure: the (0) side's tripwire warrant is measured false above bit 32 and comes out. And one text defect is mine: "TESTS" is ambiguous between "masks or compares as a case" and "depends on"; the carrier gets a one-clause clarification (L4): a relational compare's generating set is below/at/past.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\fable5_f2_v6.md:20:`idata == k` and a value class are the carrier's own clause-(e) examples ("a value the body never tests as a case"). Every seat agrees it is real and measured (117/117 for 32, 2^32, 2^40, low-32-zero, 0xffffffff → 5, 0x100000005 → 3); 60 refused values have one anchor (`SIM_ANCHORS.out`), bits 3, 4 and 8 are dual-anchored and red. Its home is (3) plus a LIMITS sentence naming the relative oracle; clause (f) is the wrong shelf. The anchor rule, so the disclosure terminates: a SECOND anchor for a value closes the constant form for that value, one added session at a time; a REPLACED anchor moves it (B's sd group at 7 opens `-> 7` on bits 32 and 34–62, measured); per-width anchors do not close it (kimi-k3); the general `idata == k` is never closed by rows.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\fable5_f2_v6.md:28:> (0) An edit conditioned on TWO axes at once -- a joint cell such as "refuse 9 only by sb after PORT=3", "`ld' sets bit 32 except at selector 5", or THE LATCH CROSSED WITH ONE BIT (while `warned_port' is clear the compare ignores bit k) -- is caught only where a row happens to hold that pair; the enumeration above is one-axis by design, and closing every pair is the unbounded growth the carrier's floor rules refuse.  The latch x bit joint is held at bits 3, 8 and 32 only (the first refusals 8, 0x100 and 0x100000005; all-ones decides no bit), so 58 bits are open at latch-clear (4-7, 9-31, 33-63): measured 117/117 at bits 4 and 40 (opus549_measured/f2v6/TABLE_f2v6.out).  What it lets through is a guest's FIRST out-of-range selector, once per device: below bit 32 the value is stored and the next DATA access ends the process in the kept range check; from bit 32 up it is truncated onto a LEGAL port silently -- 2^40 selects port 0 and the next DATA write runs as a command (WITNESS.out) -- the pre-#549 defect, for one write.  Not owed by the floor, whose generating set for `idata >= 8' is below/at/past the bound, held at both latch states; the close, one fresh device per bit at a second anchor (61 sessions, 9.7 s on the fix: TABLE_U.out), is filed as `fbctrlportdebt'.  One pair is held and is recorded as a pair, not as a class: COMMAND non-zero with the latch set, before a PORT read (R's second refusal; V's and Y's later ones) -- in the 94-row probe R was its only holder (the Opus closing-F2 seat, J_ref_cmdlatch).
.\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\p2_8bd97e4_20260918\panel_20260918_180558\ollama_glm.txt:22:**I apply pass 6's criterion; the joint-cells letter governs only enumeration granularity beyond clause (d)'s named forms.** Three grounds: (1) a later, specific, pre-committed ruling on this exact function binds over an earlier general letter — reversing it here without new evidence unbinds every prior pass; (2) the letter cannot be read to govern clause (d) at all: every `if (X && Y)` introduces a case the body may not test — that is what a weakening *is* — so the letter-as-author-reads-it would empty clause (d) of its own named example; (3) the floor's stated purpose is that the repair be *observable*, which makes **guest-reachability of the divergent cell** the operative test, not whether the body branches on the value. The body's own tests are a proxy for observability, and it fails exactly here: `len` is the repair's load-bearing operand (the diff put it into `% len`) — under the letter's literal reading no len-keyed variant could ever be owed a row, which contradicts pass 6 and the class-closure amendment's premise that a width class can be breached at all.
