.\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\grok_lca6_20260917.txt:83:No device-state lvalue, so the joint-cell floor is not a product over `d->…`. Lane (`relative_addr & 3`) and width (`len`) **flow into** `ofs`, which the body reads and tests (`data[ofs]`, `i == ofs`). That is the `sgimtedepth4dead` “selector flows into a body-read lvalue” shape without a `switch`. The round has been treating `{0,1,2,3} × {1,2,4,8} × {R,W}` plus split heads (lane 3, len 1–7) and tails (lane 0, len 3/5/6/7) as that product. That matches what the `%` can actually change (below).
.\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\c3_20260914\ollama_glm.txt:5:The edit is the contract's own named mutant, x_wfallthru, with one conjunct on `len` — an input the handler itself branches on. By the panel's own joint-cell rule that is in scope: `c1_w1w` (`ofs == 1 && len > 2`) and `t_tails` (`len > 2 && (len & 3)`) were both ruled in-scope and rowed. Its floor shape is the adjudicated clause-(d) shape at the (8,lane) W cells: the guard kept, its routing nulled into the read arm.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_minimax.txt:5:**1. N1 (D1) — FLOOR (REASONED).** The latch is a body-read state lvalue (`warned_port` is read by `!d->warned_port`); the joint-cell rule's generating set ("at each floor cell, every idata bit the guarded body TESTS is written both clear and set") is incomplete at latch-clear. Only bits 3, 8, and 32 are held there (via first refusals 8, 0x100, 0x100000005); 58 bits are open. The dedicated grok's reading is correct: 58 cells is bounded, so (0)'s "unbounded growth" rationale doesn't reach. agy / glm / Opus fallback's (0) reading treats all joint cells as out-of-scope, but the rationale distinguishes bounded from unbounded, and the joint-cell rule is defined *over* the cross-product. **Remedy:** 58 fresh devices, each `PORT=5` + one `sw` of 2^k + one PORT read expecting 5 — one width, since width is a genuine (0) joint.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_kimi.txt:5:**F1 — N1/D1: UPHOLD the gap; OVERTURN the (0) classification; rule FLOOR — from the packet's own clause (e).** (READ/REASONED) The (0) side's examples ("refuse 9 only by sb after PORT=3", "`ld` sets bit 32 except at selector 5") each contain an axis the fix does *not* partition on (width, selector value). Clause (e) as inlined draws the line itself: the body "tests three things — the access direction, the written value… and the latch (!warned_port)"; and it exempts previous-selector/COMMAND/cell conditioning precisely because "the fix makes no partition of them". The fix *does* partition on `warned_port` (`if (!d->warned_port)` — READ from the verbatim C). N1's edit keys on one leg of a partition the fix itself creates, crossed with the compare's own operand. Both legs are body-tested axes; by the packet's internal logic this is floor, not (0). The one premise I cannot settle: the carrier's joint-cell clause text (the 2026-09-04 adjudication) is quoted only second-hand in §8/N1 — settling fact: that clause verbatim. The floor classification here does not need it, because clause (e)'s own partition/non-partition asymmetry suffices. The gap itself needs no artifact: the session list (READ, §5) shows every latch-clear write is 8, 9, 0x100, 0x100000005, or all-ones, so only bits 3, 8, 32 are decisive at latch-clear; 61 − 3 = 58 open (REASONED arithmetic).
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_kimi.txt:9:**F3 — N21/D2: UPHOLD as a real, single-axis gap; rule FLOOR; the mooted single re-anchor only moves it — the stop condition is "no refused value held at exactly one anchor".** (REASONED from READ session data) The constant-5 write is conditioned on idata alone — a floor axis; it is *not* conditioned on current_port's value, COMMAND, or cells, so clause (e)'s exemption does not reach it, and (0) (two axes) does not reach it. It defeats "a refusal keeps the previous selector — previous 5 (P, W, Y, B)" — an effect the contract *claims* (READ §5) — via a relative oracle the rows cannot resolve. Auditing anchors per refused value from §5: already two-anchor: 8 (V@7), 16 (V@7), 0x100 (X7@7), 0x100000003 (V@7), 0xff (V@7), all-ones-sd (X4@4), 9 (many). Deficient (exactly one anchor, all @5): 105 single bits of B (all but 2^3, 2^4, 2^8) plus `0x200000000` (P) and `0xffffffff` (P, sw). That is 107 values. Repair: duplicate B's four groups at anchor 7 (+108 refusals, +4 reads) and add `0x200000000`, `0xffffffff` refused at 7 (P's PORT=7 tail or V). Why this closes rather than moves: after the repair a pure-constant write fails one anchor; surviving edits must read current_port — i.e., condition on a state clause (e) explicitly declares beyond-floor. WARNING to the repair author: (a) re-anchoring *only* B's sd group (grok's "e.g.") moves the gap to the new anchor for the other three groups; (b) distinct anchors *per width* (sb@5, sh@6, …) do **not** close it either — a constant-write keyed on width remains single-axis and invisible. Only same-value-two-anchors closes it. Terminates; mirror audit is symmetric (post-repair no value is @7-only).
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_kimi.txt:17:**F7 — Premises I cannot settle from the packet (named, with the single settling fact).** (a) The carrier joint-cell rule verbatim (quoted second-hand in §8) — settling fact: the 2026-09-04 adjudication text; note my N1 ruling stands on clause (e) as inlined regardless. (b) R's program order (N17, N20) — settling fact: R's session block in `regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py`. (c) The 117-row composition vs gate 16's pin — settling fact: the probe's row list; not load-bearing for this verdict since both repairs add rows and change the pin mechanically. (d) Commit hash a9a2be3d (N6/N18) — uncontroverted here.
.\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\c3_20260914\grok.txt:40:A width-conditioned fallthrough, e.g. keep the write `return` unless `len == 8` (or `len == 2`), would likely score 93/93: `iir` only stores at len 1 and 4, and every other store port the probe uses has a side-effect-free read (MSR/SCR/MCR/IER/8042 command-byte path). That is an extra conjunct on a value the write body never tests as a case (`byte = data[ofs]; return memory_rw(...)`). Joint-cell / generating-set: clause (e) coverage debt, not a floor cell. Same shape as “decline except at the two iir widths.” File it if you want it named; it does not keep this chain open.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_glm.txt:1:VERDICT: SHIP WITH CHANGES — N1 is a floor breach (58 latch-clear rows owed, with a width defect in grok's proposed repair that must be amended before v7); N21's gap is real, one-axis, and outside (0)-(8) as written, but grok's one-anchor remedy does not close it — the classification of both turns on two carrier texts not inlined in this packet, named below with the facts that settle them. If the verbatim 2026-09-04 joint-cell rule contradicts grok's quotation, N1 flips to (0) and the verdict flips to SHIP.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_glm.txt:6:* Floor vs (0), counting arguments not seats: the (0) side's only rationale is "unbounded growth," and it is arithmetically false here — the generating set is the 64 tested bits, not the 2^64 value space (the fallback's "a fresh device per value" mistakes one for the other). The quoted carrier rule ("cross-product of the guard's dominating predicates with the device-STATE lvalues the repair path reads") draws a principled line that puts latch×bit inside the floor and leaves width/previous/COMMAND outside it — which is exactly why (0)'s own examples are all width/previous joints and why the probe specially records the COMMAND×latch pair. The probe's (0) cannot except a joint the carrier's floor includes. agy (N5) and glm (N14) argued honestly from the header, but the header cannot bind the carrier. OVERTURN N5 and N14 on the merits.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_glm.txt:7:* Named caveat: the rule text is grok's quotation, endorsed by the pass driver, not independently inlined. Settling fact: the verbatim 2026-09-04 joint-cell rule. Risk is asymmetric — erring toward (0) ships an edit that restores the process-exit defect; erring toward floor costs 58 tiny sessions.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_glm.txt:8:* **The repair, audited as the brief asked — grok's remedy is DEFECTIVE as written.** "One `sw` of 2^k" cannot write 31 of the 58 bits: sw delivers a zero-extended 32-bit value, so for k ≥ 33 the store delivers idata = 0, which the **true fix accepts**, sets PORT to 0, and reddens the row against the repaired probe itself. Those 31 rows (bits 33-63) must be sd; bits 4-31 may be sw (or all 58 by sd, which also restores a uniform width). No seat flagged this. With that amendment: the repair terminates (the latch-clear generating set becomes complete; bits 3/8/32 already held; bits 0-2 under (3)); it does not open the mirror (latch-SET × bit is held by B — `LB_set16` measured red); the new joints (latch-clear × bit × width/previous) are (0)-class; and 58 processes are forced, since the latch arms on each session's first refusal — grok's cost statement is honest.
