.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_151610\ollama_kimi.txt:3:1. **REASONED — §6 main question: YES, and it is the announce predicate × value axis.** Edit tokens for the fixed arm: change `if (!d->warned_port) { fatal(...); d->warned_port = 1; }` to `if (!d->warned_port && idata != 16) { ... }`. All 94 rows stay green, derived from §4a: the FIRST refusal of every session has value in {8 (P sw, W sb), 9 (V, R, K, F, X2, X4, X6), 0x100000003 (X3), 0x100 (X7)}. Value 16 occurs only as P's 3rd/10th/11th refusal (sw/sb/sh) and V's 2nd — always with the latch already set, where the fix prints nothing anyway. Every announce row is a COUNT row (`once_after` == 1 at r0/r11/v1st/vlast/w1st/wlast/x) or `first_val` on the first print; suppressing a print that never fires removes zero `(once)` lines. So P, V, W, F, X, R, K all score exactly as on the fix. 94/94. Not among the 14 disclosed: value 16 is rowed (so not "a value no row writes"), and (3)'s new tail discloses only an announce whose printed VALUE is mangled, not an announce SUPPRESSED. A second shape in the same cell: demote the announce to `debug()` keyed on `idata != 9` — F's header states debug() prints in every stepped session and only F (free-running, value 9) can see the demotion, so keyed-to-8 or keyed-to-0x100000003 demotion also scores 94/94. Re-arm keyed to these values is NOT a counterexample (it ADDS a print — L1_rebit63 reds via W's wlast count, consistent in §5a). The blind spot is exactly the unheld joint cell "(first refusal) × value", which the single-axis enumeration cannot see because the latch is already part of the announce predicate.
