.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260924_115517\codex.txt:3549:GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\xscaleinten\fable_adjudication_20260912.md:11:**Q1 — D1: clause (e), both cells.** Rule text: *"A value or combination the body never tests as a case (`idata == k`; a byte no conditional distinguishes...) is DISCLOSED under clause (e)"*, and the pass-11 bound: *"the FLOOR ... is the cross-product of the guard's dominating predicates with the device-STATE lvalues the fix's own repair path reads."* The dominating predicates are `crm==0`, `crn==0`, `l_bit`; the body (`:311`) reads `rd` as an index and `i80321_inten` as a source and tests neither, so the floor is the accept cell alone — already pinned by the value rows. Three seats (kimi CLI, ollama-kimi, glm) hold `rd == 0` as floor by the operand-swap clause: substitute the body-read `rd` into the dominating `crn == 0`, inheriting its constant. I rule against that majority-of-three on three grounds: the swap clause binds itself to *"the guard-field/body-field grep"*, which is about STATE fields that can disagree at different write sites, not instruction-encoding operands; the pass-11 bound names device-STATE lvalues, and `rd` is not one; and the swap reading makes floor membership depend on which constant the sibling dispatch happened to carry (`rd==0` only because `crn==0` dominates — had the arm been `crn 4`, `rd==4` would be floor and `rd==0` not). Grading by coincidence is the same vice as grading by cost. Codex's withdrawal is right. **Rule for the next pure deletion whose guarded body tests nothing: the floor is clause (b) verbatim reinstatement plus the dominating predicates' accept cell; every re-introduction keyed on a body-read operand the body does not test (`rd == k`, `field & bit`, `field == v`) is clause (e), disclosed BY CLASS in the CONTRACT; any row added for one is L1 construction — licensed because cheap, never graded floor because cheap.** Grade F1/F2 as L1 + (e) in the CHANGELOG. Two corrections to seat claims: glm's "bit 28 is driven, so F2's record is false" misreads the packet — bit 28 is listed as a DRIVEN value and bit 22 as the key, which is correct; codex's F2 refinement stands (all-ones pins every single-bit-SET predicate, not bit-clear or multi-bit patterns — say so).
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260924_115517\codex.txt:4960:GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\f2_8bd97e4_20260918\panel_20260918_190129\opus5_f2.md-14-  - The joint-cells rule crosses floor partitions with the partitions the body tests. The 09-09 amendment states it as "{labels} x the guard operand's body-tested partition". The body tests lane through `data[ofs]`.
.\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260924_115517\codex.txt:4963:GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\f2_8bd97e4_20260918\panel_20260918_190129\opus5_f2.md-17-- **Against kimi on ve:** kimi is wrong here. The body never compares the port to a constant (the parent's p_port10 class, ruled by the flagship seat). "The destination differs" would make each of 65,536 port values a cell, which the joint-cells rule excludes.
