 113: LATCH IS CLEAR -- an accepted value does not set the latch, so the escape remains possible while it stays clear, not
 114: once per device (a source reading; the witness tests one occurrence): below bit 32 the value is stored and the next
 115: DATA access ends the process in the kept range check; from bit 32 up it is truncated onto a LEGAL port silently --
 116: 2^40 selects port 0 and the next DATA write runs as a command (WITNESS.out) -- the pre-#549 defect.  Not owed by the
 117: floor, whose generating set for `idata >= 8` is below/at/past the bound, held at both latch states; the close, one
 118: fresh device per bit at a second anchor (61 sessions, 9.7 s on the fix: TABLE_U.out), is filed as `fbctrlportdebt`.
 119: One pair is
 120: held and is recorded as a pair, not as a class: COMMAND non-zero with the latch set, before a PORT read (R's second
 121: refusal; V's and Y's later ones) -- in the 94-row probe R was its only holder (the Opus closing-F2 seat,
 122: J_ref_cmdlatch).  (1) A process-global latch instead of the per-instance field, and an announce conditioned on the
 123: machine's serial number.  The probe's `-E testmips` session has ONE fbctrl.  A second instance IS reachable without
 124: adding a device -- two testmips machines in one process from a config file, `gxemul @config` -- and there the
 125: process-global latch reds (measured by the Opus F2 seat, opus549_measured/f2/TABLE_twoinst.out), but that is a new
 126: launcher this probe does not use: filed as `fbctrltwoinst`.  The latch is per instance by construction, a struct
 127: member, untested across two machines.  (2) Any edit of the DATA arm's kept range check -- deleting it, deleting only
 128: its `exit(1)`, weakening `>=` to `>`, or swapping its operand -- is unreachable while the PORT arm refuses, because no
 129: out-of-range selector is ever stored; the check is KEPT as a tripwire.  It exits before it indexes, so an index of 8
 130: cannot reach `warned_port` while it stands, and `port[8]` would be undefined behaviour, not a defined alias of the
 131: latch.  (3) An exception for one value no row writes (`idata != 10`), or for a CLASS the rows never present: the low
 132: three bits (every refused value's residue mod 8 is 0, 1, 3, 5 or 7, never 2, 4 or 6 -- `(idata & 7) != 6` scores the
 133: full count, and a wide value with such a residue is accepted and truncated onto a legal port silently, 0x100000006 to
 134: port 6); an accept-side class (every value with exactly four bits set: 0x700000001 is accepted silently as port 1 --
 135: not always the exit); a latch that re-arms with a period of 108 refusals or more (107 re-announces on B's 108th
 136: refusal and reds the count row; 108 would re-announce on a 109th no session makes -- measured); an announce suppressed
 137: or mangled only for one value no session refuses first (`idata != 16`) or only for a class no first refusal belongs to
 138: (values whose bits all lie in 9..31, say); an announce demoted to debug() for some values but not F's 9 -- only a
 139: free-running session tells them apart; and A REFUSAL THAT WRITES THE CONSTANT EQUAL TO A VALUE'S ONLY ANCHOR: the rows
 140: read "keeps the previous selector" against the stored-int oracle, so `if (idata == 32) current_port = 5` reads as a
 141: keep.  Sixty refused values are observed at exactly one previous selector -- 58 single bits at 5, 0xffffffff at 5,
 142: 0x100000005 at 3 (SIM_ANCHORS.out, a STATIC walk of the session programs) -- and each admits a refusal-time write of
 143: that selector: derived for all sixty, measured for six representative value and class forms
 144: (opus549_measured/f2v6/logs/TABLE_f2v6.out); bits 3, 4 and 8 are dual-anchored (V, X7 at 7) and red.  A second anchor
 145: for a value closes its constant form; a replaced anchor moves it (B's sd group at 7 opens `-> 7` on bits 32 and 34-62:
 146: inferred from the programs, measured at bits 32 and 40); a non-exhaustive selection of values cannot rule out every
 147: value-specific exception.  (A threshold is not in this class, nor is a range of bits the
 148: COMPARE ignores: see "Beyond the effects".)  (4) Equivalent edits, THIRTEEN, each measured at the full score
 149: (table5/TABLE.out, table6/TABLE.out): storing `(uint32_t) idata`, `(int) idata` or `idata & 7` (only 0..7 reach that
 150: store, and each compiles to the fix's own binary), reordering the struct's fields, masking the PORT read with 7 (the
 151: selector is always 0..7), a PORT read that skips its load at selector 0 (odata starts at 0), a latch set to any
 152: nonzero value in place of 1, the bound written `idata > 7`, `(idata & ~7ULL) != 0` or with the literal 8 (each equal
 153: to `idata >= DEV_FBCTRL_NPORTS` on every uint64_t), the direction tested as `writeflag != MEM_WRITE` (the arm receives
 154: only MEM_READ and MEM_WRITE), the latch tested as `warned_port != 1` (it holds only 0 or 1), and the announce
 155: additionally requiring a nonzero idata (a refused value is never 0).  (5) `printf` in place of `fatal()`, or the
 156: announce without its trailing newline: indistinguishable in THIS invocation's output.  (6) Narrowing the DATA path's
 157: widths, and the device's OTHER two process-ending calls -- fbctrl_command()'s default (any command other than 0-2,
 158: including the documented but unimplemented FILL/COPY) and the access function's default (any offset other than
 159: 0x00/0x10): filed as `fbctrlcmdkill` and `fbctrloffkill`.  (7) Edits to this instrument's own logic are reviewed, not
 160: rowed.  (8) The data cells' VALUES are not an axis the rows enumerate.  An edit conditioned on a cell's value
 161: (`port[COLOR_B] == 0`, `port[Y1] != 0`) is caught only where a row's refusal or read holds a triggering state, and the
 162: states held are: every cell 0 (F, W, X, Z, B), port 5 at 0x2a (P, K, R), X1 at 123 with COMMAND 2 (V's first refusal),
 163: ports 1-7 at 0x11*p with COMMAND 2 (V's later refusals and the PORT read right after them), and COMMAND 1 (Y).  The
 164: cells-alone effect is claimed only as listed: no cell is read back after an `sh` refusal or after a refusal at
 165: previous selector 0, 2, 3, 4 or 6, and after a refusal at previous 5 the only cells read back are port 5's under
 166: COMMAND 0 (K) and COMMAND's under COMMAND 1 (Y, its second refusal only).
 167: LIMITS OF A ROW.  Session P re-selects PORT=5 before each refusal, so a refused selector cannot carry over into the
 168: next row WHEN THE LEGAL SELECTION LANDS; an edit that also refuses legal selections defeats the re-select, and later
 169: rows then inherit its value (M_acclatch).  Every read-back is taken after its session ends, so a session that dies
 170: part-way blanks the rows it had already passed (M_acc4: session A died at step 5 and every accept row read `-`).  And
 171: session A's "accept selector 0 written by sw" cannot see an IGNORED store -- the selector is already 0 at power-on --
 172: so the cell "0 by sw is accepted" is held by W's row after PORT=5, not by A's.  Session B reads PORT once per width,
 173: after all of that width's refusals: a passing row says no single-bit value of that width got through; a failing row
 174: prints the LAST value that got through, which for the compare-narrowing family names the bit for k <= 31 (2^k; INT_MIN
 175: prints 0xffffffff80000000) and reads 0 for bits 32-63, where it says only that some value got through.  B's "keeps 5"
 176: is verified against the stored-int oracle only: a refusal that WRITES 5 reads as a keep (NOT CLAIMED (3)).  B depends
 177: on the emulated `dsll` executing -- the encoding is pinned and the disassembly control checks the word, not the
 178: doubling; table 6's width-differentiated reds (bit 9 red at sh/sw/sd and never sb, bit 16 and up never sh, bit 32 and
 179: up never sw) are the evidence each 2^k reached the compare.  After a refusal, the DATA accesses made without a fresh
 180: selector are K's read, K's write and R's write; R's DATA read re-selects port 5 first.  Every refusal row rests on
 181: `fatal()` printing and returning on this path (src/core/debugmsg.c's `fatal()`: no early-out and no exit) -- twelve
 182: refusals in P and 108 in B.
 183: 
 184: Every stepped word is compared with the emulator's own step line (the hand-assembled-encoding trap).
 185: Output: one row per check, then FBCTRLP_CONTROL=OK|FAILED, FBCTRLP_RESULT=n/t, FBCTRLP_DONE.  The exit status is 0
 186: only when the controls are OK and every row is green: a red row is a nonzero exit, not only a crash.
 187: usage: fbctrl_port_probe.py <gxemul-binary>
 188: """
 189: import os
 190: import pty
 191: import re
 192: import select
 193: import signal
 194: import sys
 195: import time
 196: 
 197: BIN = sys.argv[1]
 198: 
 199: STUBADDR = 0xffffffff80100000
 200: CODE = 0xffffffff80100020
 201: FBCTRL = 0xffffffffb2f00000
 202: 
 203: ONCE_RE = re.compile(r"\[ fbctrl: port number 0x[0-9a-f]+ is out of range; the selector is unchanged \(once\) \]")
 204: #  The refused value, captured whole: "0x8" can match only 0x8, never 0x80 (the digits run to " is").
 205: VAL_RE = re.compile(r"\[ fbctrl: port number (0x[0-9a-f]+) is out of range; the selector is unchanged \(once\) \]")
 206: 
 207: REG = dict(zr=0, v0=2, v1=3, a0=4, a1=5, a2=6, a3=7, t0=8, t1=9, t2=10, t3=11, t4=12, t5=13, t6=14, t7=15,
 208:            s0=16, s1=17, s2=18, s3=19, s4=20, s5=21, s6=22, s7=23, t8=24, t9=25)
 209: OPS = dict(sb=0x28, sh=0x29, sw=0x2b, sd=0x3f, lb=0x20, lh=0x21, lw=0x23, ld=0x37)
 210: LOADS = ("lb", "lh", "lw", "ld")
 211: 
 212: 
 213: def ins(op, rt, off, base="t0"):
 214:     if op == "dsll":                                     # SPECIAL dsll rt,rt,off: rd == rt, the shift in sa
 215:         return (REG[rt] << 16) | (REG[rt] << 11) | ((off & 31) << 6) | 0x38
 216:     return (OPS[op] << 26) | (REG[base] << 21) | (REG[rt] << 16) | (off & 0xffff)
 217: 
 218: 
 219: def text(op, rt, off, base="t0"):
 220:     if op == "dsll":
 221:         return "dsll %s,%s,%d" % (rt, rt, off)
 222:     return "%s %s,%d(%s)" % (op, rt, off, base)
 223: 
 224: 
 225: ANCHOR = (0x0f << 26) | (REG["t7"] << 16)                   # lui t7,0 -- the breakpoint anchor, never executed
 226: #  Pinned independently of the helper: a wrong register field would otherwise pass silently.
 227: assert ins("sw", "t1", 0) == 0xad090000
 228: assert ins("lw", "t2", 0x10) == 0x8d0a0010
 229: assert ins("sd", "t5", 0) == 0xfd0d0000
 230: assert ins("sb", "t8", 0) == 0xa1180000
 231: assert ins("sh", "t9", 0) == 0xa5190000
 232: assert ins("ld", "s3", 0) == 0xdd130000
 233: assert ins("sb", "zr", 0) == 0xa1000000
 234: assert ins("lb", "a1", 0) == 0x81050000
 235: assert ins("lh", "a2", 0) == 0x85060000
 236: assert ANCHOR == 0x3c0f0000
 237: assert ins("dsll", "t1", 1) == 0x00094878
 238: assert ins("dsll", "t4", 1) == 0x000c6078
 239: 
 240: 
 241: def stub_path():
 242:     p = "/tmp/gx_fbctrl549p_%d.bin" % os.getpid()
 243:     with open(p, "wb") as f:
 244:         f.write(b"\x00\x00\x00\x00")
 245:     return p
 246: 
 247: 
 248: def session(label, prog, regs, reads, stub, free_run=False):
 249:     """prog: list of (op, rt, off, tag).  Steps each (or free-runs to an anchor after the last), then prints reads.
 250:     Returns dict: status, exit, badis, vals, once_after (tag -> cumulative (once) count right after that step), buf."""
 251:     pid, fd = pty.fork()
 252:     if pid == 0:
 253:         os.execvp(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-E", "testmips", "-M", "64", "0x%x:%s" % (STUBADDR, stub)])
 254:         os._exit(127)
 255:     buf = ""
 256:     dead = [False]
 257: 
 258:     def rd(t=0.4):
 259:         nonlocal buf
 260:         r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], t)
 261:         if fd not in r:
 262:             return True
 263:         try:
 264:             d = os.read(fd, 65536)
 265:         except OSError:
 266:             dead[0] = True
 267:             return False
 268:         if not d:
 269:             dead[0] = True
 270:             return False
 271:         buf += d.decode("latin1", "replace")
 272:         return True
 273: 
 274:     def wait(mark=0, echo=None, timeout=60):
 275:         t = time.time()
 276:         while time.time() - t < timeout:
 277:             if not rd():
 278:                 return False
 279:             resp = buf[mark:]
 280:             if echo is not None and echo not in resp:
 281:                 continue
 282:             if len(buf) > mark and resp.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>"):
 283:                 return True
 284:         return False
 285: 
 286:     def send(x, timeout=60):
 287:         b = (x + "\n").encode("latin1")
 288:         _mark = len(buf)
 289:         n = 0
 290:         try:
 291:             while n < len(b):
 292:                 n += os.write(fd, b[n:])
 293:         except OSError:
 294:             dead[0] = True
 295:             return False
 296:         return wait(mark=_mark, echo=x if x else None, timeout=timeout)
 297: 
 298:     words = [ins(op, rt, off) for op, rt, off, _t in prog] + [ANCHOR]
 299:     status, badis, vals, once_after, steps = "?", [], {}, {}, 0
 300:     if not wait(timeout=120):
 301:         status = "NO-PROMPT"
 302:     else:
 303:         for i, w in enumerate(words):
 304:             send("put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (CODE + 4 * i, w))
 305:         for name, val in regs:
 306:             send("%s=0x%x" % (name, val & 0xffffffffffffffff))
 307:         send("pc=0x%x" % CODE)
 308:         status = "SURVIVED"
 309:         if free_run:
 310:             send("breakpoint add 0x%x" % (CODE + 4 * len(prog)))
 311:             mark = len(buf)
 312:             b = b"continue\n"
 313:             try:
 314:                 os.write(fd, b)
 315:             except OSError:
 316:                 dead[0] = True
 317:             if not wait(mark=mark, timeout=60) or dead[0]:
 318:                 status = "HOST-DIED" if dead[0] else "NO-BREAK"
 319:             elif ("%016x" % (CODE + 4 * len(prog))) not in buf[mark:] and \
 320:                     ("%08x" % ((CODE + 4 * len(prog)) & 0xffffffff)) not in buf[mark:]:
 321:                 status = "NO-BREAK"
 322:             else:
 323:                 steps = len(prog)
 324:                 once_after["free"] = len(ONCE_RE.findall(buf))
 325:         else:
 326:             for i, (op, rt, off, tag) in enumerate(prog):
 327:                 mark = len(buf)
 328:                 if not send("step 1", timeout=30) or dead[0]:
 329:                     status = "HOST-DIED"
 330:                     break
 331:                 steps += 1
 332:                 seg = buf[mark:]
 333:                 w = words[i]
 334:                 m = re.search(r"%016x:?\s+%08x\s+([^\r\n<]+)" % (CODE + 4 * i, w), seg)
 335:                 got = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", m.group(1)).strip() if m else "(no step line)"
 336:                 if not got.startswith(text(op, rt, off)):
 337:                     badis.append("+0x%02x planted 0x%08x -> %r, expected %r" % (4 * i, w, got, text(op, rt, off)))
 338:                 if tag:
 339:                     once_after[tag] = len(ONCE_RE.findall(buf))
 340:         if status == "SURVIVED":
 341:             for name in reads:
 342:                 got = None
 343:                 for _ in range(2):
 344:                     mark = len(buf)
 345:                     if not send("print %s" % name, timeout=30) or dead[0]:
 346:                         break
 347:                     m = re.search(r"(?m)^0x([0-9a-fA-F]+)\s*$", buf[mark:])
 348:                     if m:
 349:                         got = int(m.group(1), 16)
 350:                         break
 351:                 vals[name] = got
 352:     try:
 353:         os.write(fd, b"quit\n")
 354:     except OSError:
 355:         pass
 356:     t1 = time.time()
 357:     while time.time() - t1 < 20.0:
 358:         if not rd(0.1):
 359:             break
 360:     try:
 361:         os.close(fd)
 362:     except OSError:
 363:         pass
 364:     rpid, st = 0, None
 365:     t1 = time.time()
 366:     while rpid == 0 and time.time() - t1 < 10.0:
 367:         try:
 368:             rpid, st = os.waitpid(pid, os.WNOHANG)
 369:         except OSError:
 370:             break
 371:         if rpid == 0:
 372:             time.sleep(0.05)
 373:     if rpid == 0:
 374:         try:
 375:             os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
 376:             _, st = os.waitpid(pid, 0)
 377:         except OSError:
 378:             st = None
 379:     code = os.WEXITSTATUS(st) if st is not None and os.WIFEXITED(st) else None
 380:     print("  session %-2s %-9s steps=%d/%d exit=%s once=%d" % (label, status, steps, len(prog), code,
 381:                                                               len(ONCE_RE.findall(buf))))
 382:     for b in badis:
 383:         print("      DISASSEMBLY MISMATCH: %s" % b)
 384:     return {"status": status, "exit": code, "badis": badis, "vals": vals, "once_after": once_after,
 385:             "steps": steps, "buf": buf, "n": len(prog)}
 386: 
 387: 
 388: RESULTS = []
 389: 
 390: 
 391: def row(name, got, want, note=""):
 392:     ok = got == want
 393:     RESULTS.append(ok)
 394:     print("  %-4s %-48s got=%-20s want=%-20s %s" % ("ok" if ok else "FAIL", name, got, want, note))
 395:     return ok
 396: 
 397: 
 398: def hx(v):
 399:     return "-" if v is None else "0x%x" % v
 400: 
 401: 
 402: def first_val(S):
 403:     """the value the FIRST announce of a session names, or '-'"""
 404:     m = VAL_RE.findall(S["buf"])
 405:     return m[0] if m else "-"
 406: 
 407: 
 408: M64 = 0xffffffffffffffff
 409: 
 410: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- sessions
 411: PROG_C = [("sw", "t1", 0, None), ("sw", "t3", 16, None), ("lw", "t4", 16, None), ("lw", "t5", 0, None),
 412:           ("sw", "t6", 0, None), ("sw", "t7", 16, None),               # PORT=1 (X1), X1 = 123
 413:           ("sw", "s1", 0, None), ("sw", "t7", 16, None),               # PORT=2 (Y1), Y1 = 123
 414:           ("sw", "s0", 0, None), ("sw", "s1", 16, None),               # PORT=0 (COMMAND), DATA=2: GET_RESOLUTION
 415:           ("sw", "t6", 0, None), ("lw", "s2", 16, None),               # read X1
 416:           ("sw", "s1", 0, None), ("lw", "s3", 16, None)]               # read Y1
 417: REGS_C = [("t0", FBCTRL), ("t1", 5), ("t3", 0x2a), ("t6", 1), ("t7", 123), ("s0", 0), ("s1", 2),
 418:           ("t4", 0xdead), ("t5", 0xdead), ("s2", 0xdead), ("s3", 0xdead)]
 419: 
 420: #  A: selector p written at a rotating width and read back through PORT into s<k> (poisoned 0xdead first, so a
 421: #  read that never ran cannot pass -- selector 0 would otherwise match a register's reset value); DATA value 0x11*p
 422: #  for p >= 1, read back through DATA INTO THE SELECTOR REGISTER, which holds p before the read, never 0x11*p.
 423: #  Port 0 is COMMAND: written with NOP (0, from zr) only.
 424: ACC = [(0, "sw"), (1, "sb"), (2, "sh"), (3, "sw"), (4, "sd"), (5, "sb"), (6, "sh"), (7, "sd")]
 425: SEL_REG = ["t1", "t2", "t3", "t4", "t5", "t6", "t7", "t8"]           # the selector values
 426: VAL_REG = ["zr", "a0", "a1", "a2", "a3", "v0", "v1", "t9"]           # the DATA values (port 0 gets zr = NOP)
 427: PORT_RB = ["s0", "s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6", "s7"]           # PORT read-backs
 428: PROG_A, REGS_A = [], [("t0", FBCTRL)]
 429: for k, (p, w) in enumerate(ACC):
 430:     REGS_A.append((SEL_REG[k], p))
 431:     REGS_A.append((PORT_RB[k], 0xdead))
 432:     if k:
 433:         REGS_A.append((VAL_REG[k], 0x11 * p))
 434:     PROG_A.append((w, SEL_REG[k], 0, None))                            # PORT = p at width w
 435:     PROG_A.append(("lw", PORT_RB[k], 0, None))                         # read PORT
 436:     PROG_A.append(("sw", VAL_REG[k], 16, None))                        # DATA = 0x11*p (NOP for port 0)
 437: #  the DATA read-backs, one per port 1..7: re-select p, read DATA into the selector register
 438: for k, (p, w) in enumerate(ACC):
 439:     if k:
 440:         PROG_A.append(("sw", SEL_REG[k], 0, None))
 441:         PROG_A.append(("lw", SEL_REG[k], 16, None))
 442: READS_A = PORT_RB + SEL_REG[1:]
 443: 
 444: #  P: previous selector 5, then nine refusals with read-backs (the FIRST is 8), two count-only refusals, and the
 445: #  twelfth after PORT=7.  The count is sampled after the twelfth, so a latch re-armed every N refusals, N <= 11, reds.
 446: REFUSE = [("sw", "t2", 8, "8 (sw)"), ("sw", "t1", 9, "9 (sw)"), ("sw", "t3", 16, "16 (sw)"),
 447:           ("sd", "t4", 0x200000000, "0x200000000 (sd)"), ("sd", "t5", 0x100000003, "0x100000003 (sd)"),
 448:           ("sd", "t6", M64, "all-ones (sd)"), ("sw", "t7", 0xffffffff, "all-ones (sw)"),
 449:           ("sb", "t8", 0xff, "0xff (sb)"), ("sh", "t9", 0x100, "0x100 (sh)")]
 450: #  16 by sb and by sh, each read back into a register whose own value (0x200000000, 0x100000003) is spent by then and
 451: #  can never read as 5 -- so an ignored read, or a session that died, reds the row too.
 452: EXTRA = [("sb", "t3", "t4", "16 (sb)"), ("sh", "t3", "t5", "16 (sh)")]
 453: READ_P = ["s0", "s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6", "s7", "v0"]
 454: PROG_P = [("sw", "a0", 0, None), ("sw", "a1", 16, None)]
 455: REGS_P = [("t0", FBCTRL), ("a0", 5), ("a1", 0x2a), ("a2", 7)]
 456: for k, (w, r, v, _n) in enumerate(REFUSE):
 457:     REGS_P.append((r, v))
 458:     if k:
 459:         PROG_P.append(("sw", "a0", 0, None))                   # a fresh PORT=5 before every later refusal
 460:     PROG_P.append((w, r, 0, "r%d" % k))
 461:     PROG_P.append(("lw", READ_P[k], 0, None))
 462: for w, r, rb, _n in EXTRA:
 463:     PROG_P += [("sw", "a0", 0, None), (w, r, 0, None), ("lw", rb, 0, None)]
 464: PROG_P += [("sw", "a2", 0, None), ("sw", "t1", 0, "r11"), ("lw", "v1", 0, None)]
 465: READS_P = READ_P + [e[2] for e in EXTRA] + ["v1"]
 466: 
 467: #  R: between two refusals, a PORT read, a DATA write, a NOP command, a DATA READ and a GET_RESOLUTION -- every kind of
 468: #  traffic a guest makes except SET_RESOLUTION -- in its own session.
 469: PROG_R = [("sw", "a0", 0, None), ("sw", "t1", 0, "ra"), ("lw", "s0", 0, None),
 470:           ("sw", "a1", 16, None),                                        # DATA write: port 5 = 0x2a
 471:           ("sw", "zr", 0, None), ("sw", "zr", 16, None),                 # PORT=0, DATA=0: a NOP command
 472:           ("sw", "a0", 0, None), ("lw", "s2", 16, None),                 # PORT=5, DATA READ
 473:           ("sw", "zr", 0, None), ("sw", "s3", 16, None),                 # PORT=0, DATA=2: GET_RESOLUTION
 474:           ("sw", "a0", 0, None), ("sw", "t1", 0, "rb"), ("lw", "s1", 0, None)]
 475: REGS_R = [("t0", FBCTRL), ("a0", 5), ("a1", 0x2a), ("t1", 9), ("s3", 2), ("s0", 0xdead), ("s1", 0xdead),
 476:           ("s2", 0xdead)]
 477: 
 478: PROG_K = [("sw", "t1", 0, None), ("sw", "t3", 16, None), ("sw", "t2", 0, None), ("lw", "s0", 16, None),
 479:           ("sw", "t4", 16, None), ("sw", "t1", 0, None), ("lw", "s1", 16, None)]
 480: REGS_K = [("t0", FBCTRL), ("t1", 5), ("t3", 0x2a), ("t2", 9), ("t4", 0x77), ("s0", 0xdead), ("s1", 0xdead)]
