1290:   them" failure, happening a second time, inside the paragraph that records it. **When a rule is
1291:   superseded, delete its conclusion; do not demote it to a footnote that still reads as the
1292:   verdict.***
1293: 
1294: ## THE VACUITY FLOOR AND THE DE-ESCALATION CLAUSE (flagship adjudication, 2026-08-21)
1295: 
1296: **"Weak earns its own round; vacuous does not get to ship" IS A RATIONALISATION UNTIL A FLOOR IS
1297: ATTACHED** �?" the adjudicating seat said so about the main loop's own call, and it was right:
1298: *weak* has no lower bound, so any detector that kills one mutant can claim it.
1299: 
1300: > **THE FLOOR.** A detector is VACUOUS �?" replace it IN-ROUND �?" unless it (a) fails on the
1301: > pre-fix build, (b) fails on a **VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT of the removed defect at EVERY fixed
1302: > site**, and (c) fails on a null medium (a stale copy, a comment-only file �?" i.e. it carries
1303: > identity rows). Below the floor, the round's own claim to have "shipped a detector with the
1304: > fix" is a WRONG RECORD, which the stopping rule already fixes in-round.
1305: >
1306: > ### *** CLAUSE (d) �?" NULL-REPAIR RED. Adopted 2026-08-23 by OWNER RULING, on the round
1307: > that measured its case. ***
1308: >
1309: > **(d) The detector must also fail when the fix is PRESENT but its REPAIR IS NULLED �?"
1310: > the guard kept, its effect removed.**
1311: >
1312: > **(b) DOES NOT IMPLY (d), and #461 measured both in the same hour on the same detector:**
1313: >
1314: > | mutant | shape | score |
1315: > |---|---|---|
1316: > | `m_dmarevert` | (b): reinstate the `exit(1)` verbatim | **22/31 �?" killed** |
1317: > | `m_align1` | (d): keep the guard, weaken `& 3` to `& 1` | **27/27 �?" ESCAPED** |
1318: >
1319: > The detector passed (a), (b) and (c) and was still blind to a ONE-CHARACTER edit that
1320: > re-opened the very defect the round had just closed. (b) restores the DEFECT; (d) neuters
1321: > the FIX. A detector can be sensitive to one and blind to the other, because they fail at
1322: > different sites: (b) fails where the old code was, (d) fails where the new code is.
1323: >
1324: > **HOW TO BUILD THE (d) MUTANT �?" and it is the same discriminator the accept-row rule
1325: > already uses.** Take each predicate the fix **DEPENDS ON** and weaken it to the
1326: > least-restrictive form that still *reads* like the same check: a mask narrowed
1327: > (`& 3` -> `& 1`), a condition qualified (`if (X)` -> `if (X && writeflag == MEM_WRITE)`),
1328: > a bound relaxed. **Not deletion** �?" deletion is (b) wearing (d)'s clothes and is usually
1329: > already covered. The test is whether a plausible future edit could produce it.
1330: >
1331: > **WHY THE ONE-CHARACTER FORM IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS.** `dma alias`, the row written
1332: > specifically to pin that repair, was **STRUCTURALLY BLIND** to it: it used an ODD address,
1333: > which both guard forms drop. *The row aimed at the defect could not see the defect return.*
1334: > A row written from the FIX's intent tests the intent, not the boundary �?" so (d)'s mutant
1335: > has to come from an adversary, which is exactly what the ask-again buys.
1336: >
1337: > **COST: one mutant per repair predicate**, the same order as the accept-row rule (#448 = 4,
1338: > #449 = 1, #461 = 4 across two arms). It is bounded by the diff, so it cannot become #392:
1339: > the predicates are the ones the fix ADDED, and that list is finite and visible.
1340: >
1341: > ### *** "THE PREDICATE THE FIX INTRODUCED" ENUMERATED THE EMPTY SET FOR MOST OF THIS
1342: > ### QUEUE, AND NOBODY NOTICED FOR THREE ROUNDS. Corrected 2026-08-24 -- clause (d) now
1343: > ### reads DEPENDS ON, not INTRODUCED. ***
1344: >
1345: > Clause (d) says *keep the guard, null its repair*. As written it enumerated only
1346: > predicates the diff ADDS -- and **the two commonest fix shapes here add none**:
1347: >
1348: > * a **pure deletion** introduces no predicate at all;
1349: > * a **DE-FATALISATION** introduces none either -- it changes what an EXISTING
1350: >   predicate's branch does. That is most of this project's queue.
1351: >
1352: > So for those rounds clause (d) enumerated **nothing**, and a round could satisfy the
1353: > floor while having built no (d) mutant whatever. `#461` measured (d) successfully only
1354: > because its fix happened to ADD a predicate (an alignment mask) for the mutant to weaken.
1355: >
1356: > **Reading it as DEPENDS ON fixes both cases at once**, and it is why this now replaces a
1357: > narrower deletion-only rule written earlier the same day: that rule was a special case of
1358: > this gap, and two overlapping statements of one rule is the accumulation this carrier is
1359: > supposed to refuse.
1360: >
1361: > **The two shapes, once the enumeration is right:**
1362: > * **fix ADDS a predicate** -> weaken the added one (unchanged; the original rule).
1363: > * **fix adds none** -> the (d) mutant reroutes an EXISTING guard's repair. For a
1364: >   de-fatalisation: **swap the guard's operand for a field the guarded BODY reads.**
1365: >   For a pure deletion: **re-introduce the removed behaviour under a DIFFERENT predicate**
1366: >   -- not the same one widened, which is (b) wearing (d)'s clothes.
1367: >
1368: > *** THE MEASURED CASE, and it is why this matters rather than being tidy: ***
1369: > `#462` de-fatalised two arms. Its guard reads `d->data`; the body it guards reads
1545: > > **FORM: recorded OUTPUT, never assertion** -- the two-part-clearance precedent, *run it, do
1546: > > not assert it*. One `PRETABLE.out` in the round's kill-table artifacts dir (or a probe-header
1547: > > section), **written BEFORE table 1 runs so its own mtime proves the order** -- the same
1548: > > order discriminator the oracle-correction rule uses. It carries: the sibling arms compared
1549: > > and any asymmetry; each enumerated claim sentence mapped to a row or an owed sentence; the
1550: > > guard-field/body-field lists and any disagreeing-state row owed; and per row, the mutant it
1551: > > kills and the observable that differs.
1552: > >
1553: > > **Any check may discharge `N/A` WITH ITS REASON RECORDED. An unrun check and an inapplicable
1554: > > one must not render the same** -- this project's oldest rule about blanks.
1555: > >
1556: > > ### *** AND A THIRD FAILURE MODE, MEASURED 2026-08-28: THE BLOCK WAS RUN, ITS ROWS WERE
1557: > > ### PRESCRIBED, AND THEY WERE LOST WHEN THE ROW LIST WAS REWRITTEN THREE HOURS LATER. ***
1558: > >
1559: > > `#490`'s `PRETABLE.out` named two mutants at **16:05** -- a latch-bit collision and a brace
1560: > > slip -- and prescribed a row for each (`crossbit`, `latch cond`). At **18:45** a nine-seat
1561: > > panel found a DIFFERENT defect (the row set was site-asymmetric), the addition list was
1562: > > rewritten to carry the panel's findings, and **both earlier rows silently vanished.** The
1563: > > measuring seat then BUILT both mutants and both escaped the committed 13-row detector --
1564: > > two clause-(d) floor breaches that the round's own artifact had predicted and specified.
1565: > >
1566: > > **Nothing was forgotten in the sense of never known.** The knowledge sat in the file that
1567: > > exists precisely to be consulted at row-writing time, and did not survive a rewrite in a
1568: > > DIFFERENT artifact. The new list was not careless -- it correctly added everything the
1569: > > panel had just found.
1570: > >
1571: > > > **A ROW LIST REWRITTEN FROM A PANEL'S FINDINGS IS DIFFED AGAINST THE PRE-TABLE BLOCK'S
1572: > > > OWN PRESCRIBED ROWS, NOT MERELY EXTENDED WITH THE NEW ONES.** The block PRESCRIBES rows;
1573: > > > a later list that does not contain them has dropped an obligation, and the diff is the
1574: > > > only thing that says so.
1575: > >
1576: > > *This is the block's own lesson turned on the block: it exists because a recommended check
1577: > > is memory and memory does not fire. Running it once does not make its OUTPUT durable --
1578: > > the output has to be carried forward, and nothing was checking that it was.*
1579: > >
1580: > > **AND THE GUARD-FIELD CHECK COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT EITHER**, which is worth knowing before
1581: > > relying on it: it asks where the guard field and the body field DIFFER. For the latch
1582: > > collision they are the SAME field (`d->warned`). **For a shared bitfield the question is
1583: > > *"who else writes this field, and can they set my bit?"*** -- measured here as
